window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["github-actions"]=[{"id":"github-actions-f0001","card":"q-github-actions-001-ci-cd-concepts","sec":"github-actions-s002","q":"What is CI/CD, and how does it benefit software teams?","a":"<p>CI/CD automates code integration, testing and deployment. Benefits: faster feedback, fewer bugs, better collaboration, quicker releases.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0002","card":"q-github-actions-001-ci-cd-concepts","sec":"github-actions-s002","q":"What is the difference between Continuous Integration, Continuous Delivery and Continuous Deployment?","a":"<ul>\n  <li>Continuous Integration: frequent code merges with automated tests.</li>\n  <li>Continuous Delivery: CI plus an automated release, with a manual deploy step.</li>\n  <li>Continuous Deployment: CI plus automated deployment to production.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0003","card":"q-github-actions-001-ci-cd-concepts","sec":"github-actions-s002","q":"What are the key components of a CI/CD pipeline?","a":"<p>Source control, build, test, deploy, monitoring.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0004","card":"q-github-actions-001-ci-cd-concepts","sec":"github-actions-s002","q":"How does GitHub Actions fit into the CI/CD ecosystem?","a":"<p>It automates CI/CD workflows inside GitHub using YAML, integrating with repos and tools such as Docker and AWS.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0005","card":"q-github-actions-001-ci-cd-concepts","sec":"github-actions-s002","q":"What are some common use cases for GitHub Actions beyond CI/CD?","a":"<p>Automation (issue labeling), code review (linting), notifications and scheduled tasks.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0006","card":"q-github-actions-001-ci-cd-concepts","sec":"github-actions-s002","q":"Why does automating build and test on every merge reduce the cost of fixing bugs?","a":"<p>Integration problems are found minutes after the change, while the author still remembers it and the diff is small. The longer a bug survives, the more commits sit on top of it and the harder it is to find the cause. Frequent small merges with automatic tests also avoid big, risky \"merge day\" integrations.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0007","card":"q-github-actions-001-ci-cd-concepts","sec":"github-actions-s002","q":"Your team does Continuous Delivery but not Continuous Deployment. What does the release process look like, and why might a team choose it?","a":"<p>Every change that passes the pipeline is built into a releasable artifact, but a human approves the final push to production. Teams choose this for regulatory sign-off, coordinated release windows, or low trust in test coverage. In GitHub Actions this is usually modelled with an environment that has required reviewers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0008","card":"q-github-actions-001-ci-cd-concepts","sec":"github-actions-s002","q":"Which stage of a CI/CD pipeline should fail fastest, and how do you order stages to get quick feedback?","a":"<p>Put cheap, fast checks first (lint, unit tests, type checks) and slow or expensive ones later (integration tests, image builds, deploys). A failure then stops the pipeline early and saves runner time. In GitHub Actions this is expressed with separate jobs and <code>needs</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0009","card":"q-github-actions-001-ci-cd-concepts","sec":"github-actions-s002","q":"What does \"build once, deploy many\" mean in a CI/CD pipeline, and why is it a best practice?","a":"<p>The pipeline builds one artifact (a package or container image) and promotes the same artifact through test, staging and production. Rebuilding for each environment risks shipping something different from what was tested. Environment differences go in configuration and secrets, not in the build.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0010","card":"q-github-actions-001-ci-cd-concepts","sec":"github-actions-s002","q":"What can GitHub Actions automate that is not build, test or deploy?","a":"<p>Anything triggered by a GitHub event: labelling or triaging issues, welcoming first-time contributors, closing stale issues and PRs on a schedule, posting PR comments, syncing repositories, and generating release notes. Events such as <code>issues</code>, <code>pull_request</code> and <code>schedule</code> make it a general automation engine.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0011","card":"q-github-actions-001-ci-cd-concepts","sec":"github-actions-s002","q":"Why is monitoring part of a CI/CD pipeline and not just an operations task?","a":"<p>Deployment is only successful if the new version behaves correctly in production. Post-deploy health checks, alerts and fast rollback close the feedback loop. A pipeline that deploys with no verification only automates the delivery of failures.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0012","card":"q-github-actions-002-github-actions-compared-with-jenkins-and","sec":"github-actions-s003","q":"What are GitHub Actions, and how do they differ from traditional CI/CD tools like Jenkins or CircleCI?","a":"<p>GitHub Actions is a built-in GitHub tool for automating workflows (build, test, deploy) using YAML files triggered by repo events. Jenkins is standalone, plugin-heavy and flexible. CircleCI is cloud-focused and orb-based. GitHub Actions is tightly integrated with GitHub, simpler to set up, and uses a marketplace of reusable actions.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0013","card":"q-github-actions-002-github-actions-compared-with-jenkins-and","sec":"github-actions-s003","q":"What are the main advantages of GitHub Actions over Jenkins or CircleCI?","a":"<p>GitHub integration, easy YAML setup, the action marketplace, a free tier and scalable runners.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0014","card":"q-github-actions-002-github-actions-compared-with-jenkins-and","sec":"github-actions-s003","q":"What file format is used for workflows, and why?","a":"<p>YAML. It is readable, simple and widely used in CI/CD for structured automation.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0015","card":"q-github-actions-002-github-actions-compared-with-jenkins-and","sec":"github-actions-s003","q":"What operational work do you take on with Jenkins that you do not have with GitHub-hosted runners?","a":"<p>With Jenkins you run and patch the controller, manage agents, upgrade plugins and handle plugin compatibility, backups and scaling. GitHub-hosted runners are provisioned by GitHub as a fresh VM for each job, so the operational burden is much lower. The trade-off is less control over the environment, which you recover with self-hosted runners.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0016","card":"q-github-actions-002-github-actions-compared-with-jenkins-and","sec":"github-actions-s003","q":"When would you still choose Jenkins over GitHub Actions?","a":"<p>When you need deep customization, run in an air-gapped or on-premises environment, have large existing investment in Jenkins pipelines and plugins, or the source is not on GitHub. Jenkins also gives full control over the controller and agents. GitHub Actions wins when code already lives on GitHub and you want low setup cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0017","card":"q-github-actions-002-github-actions-compared-with-jenkins-and","sec":"github-actions-s003","q":"How does the unit of reuse differ between GitHub Actions, CircleCI and Jenkins?","a":"<p>GitHub Actions reuses through actions (from the Marketplace or in the repo) and reusable workflows. CircleCI uses orbs, which are packaged config. Jenkins uses plugins and shared libraries. Actions and orbs are declarative config bundles, while Jenkins plugins run inside the controller, which is why they can break it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0018","card":"q-github-actions-002-github-actions-compared-with-jenkins-and","sec":"github-actions-s003","q":"A team is moving a Jenkins pipeline to GitHub Actions. How do the main concepts map?","a":"<p>A Jenkinsfile maps to a workflow YAML file. A Jenkins stage maps roughly to a job, and steps map to steps. Agents map to runners (<code>runs-on</code>). Credentials map to secrets and environments. Jenkins triggers map to the <code>on</code> events. Shared libraries map to reusable workflows and composite actions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0019","card":"q-github-actions-003-workflows-and-the-workflows-directory","sec":"github-actions-s004","q":"What is a workflow in GitHub Actions, and how is it defined?","a":"<p>A workflow is an automated process made of jobs, triggered by events. It is defined in a YAML file under <code>.github/workflows/</code>, specifying a name, triggers (<code>on</code>) and jobs.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0020","card":"q-github-actions-003-workflows-and-the-workflows-directory","sec":"github-actions-s004","q":"What is the purpose of the <code>.github/workflows</code> directory?","a":"<p>It holds all workflow YAML files. GitHub looks there to find and run automation when events occur.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0021","card":"q-github-actions-003-workflows-and-the-workflows-directory","sec":"github-actions-s004","q":"What are the key components of a workflow file?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>name</strong>: workflow label.</li>\n  <li><strong>on</strong>: event triggers (for example <code>push</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>jobs</strong>: tasks to run, each with <code>runs-on</code> (runner type) and steps (actions or commands).</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0022","card":"q-github-actions-003-workflows-and-the-workflows-directory","sec":"github-actions-s004","q":"What is the typical structure of a <code>.yml</code> workflow file?","a":"<p><code>name</code>, <code>on</code> (triggers), then <code>jobs</code> containing <code>runs-on</code> and <code>steps</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0023","card":"q-github-actions-003-workflows-and-the-workflows-directory","sec":"github-actions-s004","q":"What is the purpose of the <code>on</code> keyword?","a":"<p>It defines the events (such as push or pull_request) that trigger the workflow.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0024","card":"q-github-actions-003-workflows-and-the-workflows-directory","sec":"github-actions-s004","q":"In GitHub Actions, what happens if you put a workflow YAML file somewhere other than <code>.github/workflows/</code>?","a":"<p>GitHub does not detect it, so it never runs. Workflow files must sit directly in <code>.github/workflows/</code> (not in a subdirectory) and have a <code>.yml</code> or <code>.yaml</code> extension.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0025","card":"q-github-actions-003-workflows-and-the-workflows-directory","sec":"github-actions-s004","q":"Does a workflow have to live on the default branch to run?","a":"<p>It depends on the event. For <code>push</code> and <code>pull_request</code>, GitHub uses the workflow file from the branch or merge commit being evaluated. For <code>schedule</code>, <code>workflow_dispatch</code> in the UI, and <code>repository_dispatch</code>, the workflow file must exist on the default branch. A common cause of \"my manual trigger button does not appear\" is a workflow that exists only on a feature branch.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0026","card":"q-github-actions-003-workflows-and-the-workflows-directory","sec":"github-actions-s004","q":"What is the minimum a valid GitHub Actions workflow file needs?","a":"<p>An <code>on</code> trigger and at least one job with <code>runs-on</code> and <code>steps</code>. <code>name</code> is optional (GitHub falls back to the file path). Every job needs a runner, and each step needs either <code>run</code> or <code>uses</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: CI\non: push\njobs:\n  build:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - run: echo hello</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0027","card":"q-github-actions-003-workflows-and-the-workflows-directory","sec":"github-actions-s004","q":"In GitHub Actions YAML, why is the key <code>on</code> sometimes a problem for YAML parsers, and what does it mean for linting?","a":"<p>In YAML 1.1 the bare word <code>on</code> can be parsed as the boolean <code>true</code>. GitHub's own parser handles it as a key, so workflows work, but generic YAML tools or scripts that load workflow files can see <code>true</code> as the key. Be aware of it when generating or editing workflow files from code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0028","card":"q-github-actions-003-workflows-and-the-workflows-directory","sec":"github-actions-s004","q":"Can a repository have more than one workflow, and how do they interact?","a":"<p>Yes, any number of files in <code>.github/workflows/</code>. Each one is independent and triggered by its own events, so several can run in parallel for the same push. They can be chained with <code>workflow_run</code>, <code>workflow_call</code> (reusable workflows) or by dispatching events.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0029","card":"q-github-actions-003-workflows-and-the-workflows-directory","sec":"github-actions-s004","q":"Why split CI, release and scheduled maintenance into separate workflow files rather than one large file?","a":"<p>Each file has its own triggers, permissions and concurrency settings, which keeps least privilege and readable status checks. A large file with many <code>if</code> conditions on the event type is harder to read and gives every job the same broad token. Separate files also make required status checks simpler.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0030","card":"q-github-actions-004-events-jobs-and-steps","sec":"github-actions-s005","q":"What is an event, and what are common examples?","a":"<p>An event triggers a workflow. Common ones: <code>push</code> (code push), <code>pull_request</code> (PR activity), <code>schedule</code> (cron-based) and <code>workflow_dispatch</code> (manual).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0031","card":"q-github-actions-004-events-jobs-and-steps","sec":"github-actions-s005","q":"What is the difference between a job and a step?","a":"<p>A job is a group of steps that run on one runner. A step is an individual task (checkout, run a script) inside a job. Jobs can run in parallel by default; steps run sequentially.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0032","card":"q-github-actions-004-events-jobs-and-steps","sec":"github-actions-s005","q":"In GitHub Actions, how do jobs and steps differ in where they run and how they share data?","a":"<p>A job runs on one runner (its own fresh VM or container), and its steps run sequentially on that same machine, so steps can share the filesystem and environment files. Different jobs run on different runners, so they share nothing unless you use job outputs, artifacts or the cache.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0033","card":"q-github-actions-004-events-jobs-and-steps","sec":"github-actions-s005","q":"By default, do jobs in a workflow run in sequence or in parallel, and how do you change it?","a":"<p>In parallel. Use <code>needs</code> on a job to make it wait for other jobs. Jobs that depend on failed jobs are skipped unless you use a condition such as <code>if: always()</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0034","card":"q-github-actions-004-events-jobs-and-steps","sec":"github-actions-s005","q":"A step that sets an environment variable is followed by a step that reads it, but the value is missing. What is a likely cause?","a":"<p>Each <code>run</code> step starts a new shell, so <code>export VAR=x</code> in one step does not persist. Append <code>VAR=x</code> to the file <code>$GITHUB_ENV</code> to make it available to later steps in the same job, or use step outputs through <code>$GITHUB_OUTPUT</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0035","card":"q-github-actions-004-events-jobs-and-steps","sec":"github-actions-s005","q":"Which event types does GitHub Actions support, in broad groups?","a":"<p>Repository activity (<code>push</code>, <code>pull_request</code>, <code>release</code>, <code>issues</code>), manual (<code>workflow_dispatch</code>), time based (<code>schedule</code>), external (<code>repository_dispatch</code>), and workflow-to-workflow (<code>workflow_call</code>, <code>workflow_run</code>). A single workflow can listen to several events.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0036","card":"q-github-actions-005-actions-and-the-checkout-action","sec":"github-actions-s006","q":"What are actions, and how do you use pre-built ones?","a":"<p>Actions are reusable units of work. Pre-built ones from the Marketplace (for example <code>actions/checkout@v4</code>) are used with <code>uses</code> inside steps.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps:\n  - uses: actions/checkout@v4</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0037","card":"q-github-actions-005-actions-and-the-checkout-action","sec":"github-actions-s006","q":"What is <code>actions/checkout</code>, and why is it used so often?","a":"<p>It clones the repo's code onto the runner, which build and test steps need.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0038","card":"q-github-actions-005-actions-and-the-checkout-action","sec":"github-actions-s006","q":"What are the three types of actions you can write or use in GitHub Actions?","a":"<p>JavaScript actions (run directly on the runner, fast, cross-platform), Docker container actions (run in a container, Linux runners only, any language), and composite actions (a sequence of steps packaged in <code>action.yml</code>). Pick JavaScript for speed and portability, Docker for custom tooling, composite for simple reuse.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0039","card":"q-github-actions-005-actions-and-the-checkout-action","sec":"github-actions-s006","q":"In GitHub Actions, why is <code>uses: actions/checkout@v4</code> pinned to a version tag, and what is a stricter option?","a":"<p>A tag keeps the workflow from silently picking up a breaking change. Tags can be moved by the action's owner, so the strictest option is to pin to a full commit SHA, which is immutable. Pinning matters most for third-party actions because they run with access to your secrets and token.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps:\n  - uses: actions/checkout@v4</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0040","card":"q-github-actions-005-actions-and-the-checkout-action","sec":"github-actions-s006","q":"What does the repository look like on the runner if you forget the checkout step?","a":"<p>The workspace is empty, so commands such as <code>npm ci</code> or <code>pytest</code> fail because there is no code or lockfile. GitHub-hosted runners start clean, and the repository is not cloned automatically.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0041","card":"q-github-actions-005-actions-and-the-checkout-action","sec":"github-actions-s006","q":"Which <code>actions/checkout</code> options matter when you need full git history or submodules?","a":"<p>By default checkout fetches a single commit (<code>fetch-depth: 1</code>). Set <code>fetch-depth: 0</code> for full history (needed for tags, changelogs or version tools that read git log). Set <code>submodules: true</code> or <code>recursive</code> for submodules, and <code>token</code> if a private repo is needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0042","card":"q-github-actions-005-actions-and-the-checkout-action","sec":"github-actions-s006","q":"What is the difference between <code>uses</code> and <code>run</code> in a workflow step?","a":"<p><code>uses</code> runs a packaged action, identified as <code>owner/repo@ref</code>, a local path or a Docker image, and takes input through <code>with</code>. <code>run</code> executes shell commands on the runner. Use <code>uses</code> for reusable, versioned logic and <code>run</code> for project-specific commands.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0043","card":"q-github-actions-006-runners","sec":"github-actions-s007","q":"What is the role of runners, and what types exist?","a":"<p>Runners are VMs or containers that execute workflows. GitHub-hosted runners are <code>ubuntu-latest</code>, <code>windows-latest</code> and <code>macos-latest</code>. Self-hosted runners are your own machines.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0044","card":"q-github-actions-006-runners","sec":"github-actions-s007","q":"What does <code>runs-on</code> do, and what are the common values?","a":"<p>It sets the runner type: <code>ubuntu-latest</code>, <code>windows-latest</code>, <code>macos-latest</code> or <code>self-hosted</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0045","card":"q-github-actions-006-runners","sec":"github-actions-s007","q":"Why are GitHub-hosted runners considered safer for untrusted code than self-hosted runners?","a":"<p>Each job gets a fresh, ephemeral VM that is destroyed afterwards, so nothing persists between runs. A self-hosted runner is a machine you own and may be reused across jobs, so malicious code in a pull request could leave a backdoor or reach your internal network. For this reason self-hosted runners are not recommended for public repositories.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0046","card":"q-github-actions-006-runners","sec":"github-actions-s007","q":"When do you choose a self-hosted runner over a GitHub-hosted one?","a":"<p>When you need specific hardware (GPU, large memory, ARM), software already installed, access to a private network or on-premises systems, or lower cost at high volume. The trade-off is that you manage patching, scaling and security yourself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0047","card":"q-github-actions-006-runners","sec":"github-actions-s007","q":"What does <code>ubuntu-latest</code> mean, and why can pinning a runner image version be safer?","a":"<p><code>-latest</code> is a label that GitHub moves to a newer image over time. When it moves, tool versions can change and break a build. Pinning (for example <code>ubuntu-22.04</code>) gives predictable builds, at the cost of manual upgrades before older images are retired.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0048","card":"q-github-actions-006-runners","sec":"github-actions-s007","q":"Why do billing and speed differ across runner OS types for GitHub-hosted runners?","a":"<p>Private repositories use minutes with multipliers: Linux has the lowest rate, Windows costs more per minute, and macOS costs the most. Public repositories on standard hosted runners are free. So run on Linux unless the job really needs Windows or macOS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0049","card":"q-github-actions-007-specifying-events","sec":"github-actions-s008","q":"How do you specify the events that trigger a workflow?","a":"<p>Use <code>on</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on:\n  push:\n  pull_request:\n  schedule:\n    - cron: &#x27;0 0 * * *&#x27;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0050","card":"q-github-actions-007-specifying-events","sec":"github-actions-s008","q":"In GitHub Actions, what is the difference between <code>on: push</code> and <code>on: [push, pull_request]</code>?","a":"<p>The first triggers only on pushes. The second triggers on both. A common side effect is duplicate runs when a branch has an open PR, because both <code>push</code> and <code>pull_request</code> fire. Restrict <code>push</code> to the main branch to avoid it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0051","card":"q-github-actions-007-specifying-events","sec":"github-actions-s008","q":"Why does <code>on: pull_request</code> from a fork get a read-only token and no secrets?","a":"<p>Running untrusted fork code with write access or secrets would let an attacker steal them. GitHub therefore gives fork PRs a read-only <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> and withholds repository secrets. <code>pull_request_target</code> runs in the base repository context with secrets, which is why it is risky if you check out and run the PR's code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0052","card":"q-github-actions-007-specifying-events","sec":"github-actions-s008","q":"What is a safer way to run privileged work, such as commenting on a PR, for pull requests coming from forks?","a":"<p>Run unprivileged build and test with <code>pull_request</code>, upload results as an artifact, then process them in a separate <code>workflow_run</code> workflow that has the needed permissions and never executes untrusted code. Avoid checking out PR code in <code>pull_request_target</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0053","card":"q-github-actions-007-specifying-events","sec":"github-actions-s008","q":"How can one event be narrowed to specific activity types?","a":"<p>Use <code>types</code> under the event. <code>pull_request</code> defaults to <code>opened</code>, <code>synchronize</code> and <code>reopened</code>. Listing types limits reruns, and it is needed for events like <code>issues</code> or <code>release</code> where you want only one action, such as <code>opened</code> or <code>published</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on:\n  pull_request:\n    types: [opened, synchronize]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0054","card":"q-github-actions-008-branch-tag-and-path-filters","sec":"github-actions-s009","q":"How do you run a workflow only on specific branches or tags?","a":"<p>Use <code>branches</code> or <code>tags</code> filters under the event.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\n    tags: [&#x27;v*&#x27;]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0055","card":"q-github-actions-008-branch-tag-and-path-filters","sec":"github-actions-s009","q":"How do you run a workflow only when certain files change (for example <code>.py</code> files)?","a":"<p>Use a <code>paths</code> filter.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on:\n  push:\n    paths:\n      - &#x27;**/*.py&#x27;\njobs:\n  check:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - run: python -m py_compile *.py</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0056","card":"q-github-actions-008-branch-tag-and-path-filters","sec":"github-actions-s009","q":"How do you run jobs by change type in a monorepo (frontend vs backend)?","a":"<p>Use <code>paths</code> (or <code>if</code> with change detection).</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on:\n  push:\n    paths: [&#x27;services/frontend/**&#x27;]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0057","card":"q-github-actions-008-branch-tag-and-path-filters","sec":"github-actions-s009","q":"How do you run a job only when <code>README.md</code> changes?","a":"<p>Check the commit's modified files with an <code>if</code> on the job.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>jobs:\n  check:\n    if: contains(github.event.commits[0].modified, &#x27;README.md&#x27;)\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n      - run: echo &quot;README.md modified!&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0058","card":"q-github-actions-008-branch-tag-and-path-filters","sec":"github-actions-s009","q":"In a workflow with <code>branches</code> and <code>paths</code> filters on the same event, how are they combined?","a":"<p>With AND. The workflow runs only when the branch matches and at least one changed file matches the paths. Use <code>paths-ignore</code> to skip changes such as docs, but you cannot mix <code>paths</code> and <code>paths-ignore</code> for the same event.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0059","card":"q-github-actions-008-branch-tag-and-path-filters","sec":"github-actions-s009","q":"What is the difference between <code>branches</code> and <code>branches-ignore</code>, and can you use both?","a":"<p><code>branches</code> is an allow list and <code>branches-ignore</code> is a deny list. You cannot use both for the same event. To include and exclude, use <code>branches</code> with a negated pattern such as <code>'!releases/**'</code>, where the order of patterns matters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0060","card":"q-github-actions-008-branch-tag-and-path-filters","sec":"github-actions-s009","q":"In GitHub Actions, what does this trigger match?","a":"<p>A push to <code>main</code>, or a push of any tag starting with <code>v</code> (for example <code>v1.2.0</code>). When <code>branches</code> and <code>tags</code> are both given they are OR-ed, since a push is either a branch or a tag push. This pattern is common for release workflows.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\n    tags: [&#x27;v*&#x27;]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0061","card":"q-github-actions-008-branch-tag-and-path-filters","sec":"github-actions-s009","q":"Why might a required status check stay \"pending forever\" when a workflow uses <code>paths</code> filters?","a":"<p>If a PR changes no matching files, the workflow is skipped and never reports a status. A required check that never reports blocks the merge. Fixes: always run a lightweight job and decide inside it, or add a gate job that always runs and reports.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0062","card":"q-github-actions-008-branch-tag-and-path-filters","sec":"github-actions-s009","q":"What is the difference between <code>*</code> and <code>**</code> in GitHub Actions filter patterns?","a":"<p><code>*</code> matches any characters except <code>/</code>, so it stays within one path segment. <code>**</code> matches any characters including <code>/</code>, so it spans directories. <code>'**/*.py'</code> matches Python files at any depth.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0063","card":"q-github-actions-008-branch-tag-and-path-filters","sec":"github-actions-s009","q":"In this job-level condition, what is the weakness of detecting a README change?","a":"<p>It only inspects the first commit in the push payload, so a README change in a later commit is missed. The payload also caps the number of commits listed. For reliable change detection use a <code>paths</code> filter, or a changed-files action or <code>git diff</code> between the base and head SHAs.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>jobs:\n  check:\n    if: contains(github.event.commits[0].modified, &#x27;README.md&#x27;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0064","card":"q-github-actions-008-branch-tag-and-path-filters","sec":"github-actions-s009","q":"How can you run different jobs depending on which part of a monorepo changed, within one workflow?","a":"<p>A <code>paths</code> filter applies to the whole workflow, so for per-job decisions run a first job that detects changes (for example with <code>dorny/paths-filter</code> or <code>git diff</code>), expose the results as job outputs, and gate downstream jobs with <code>needs</code> and <code>if</code>. Alternatively use one workflow file per service, each with its own <code>paths</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0065","card":"q-github-actions-009-manual-and-scheduled-triggers","sec":"github-actions-s010","q":"How can you trigger a workflow manually or on a schedule?","a":"<p>Manual: <code>workflow_dispatch</code>, optionally with inputs. Schedule: <code>schedule</code> with a cron expression.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on:\n  workflow_dispatch:\n    inputs:\n      environment:\n        description: &#x27;Environment&#x27;\n        required: true\n        default: &#x27;staging&#x27;\njobs:\n  deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - run: echo &quot;Deploying to ${{ github.event.inputs.environment }}&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0066","card":"q-github-actions-009-manual-and-scheduled-triggers","sec":"github-actions-s010","q":"How do you schedule a workflow (daily, weekly, every 15 minutes)?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on:\n  schedule:\n    - cron: &#x27;0 0 * * *&#x27;     # daily at midnight\n    # - cron: &#x27;0 9 * * 1&#x27;   # every Monday at 9 AM\n    # - cron: &#x27;*/15 * * * *&#x27; # every 15 minutes\njobs:\n  task:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - run: ./my-script.sh</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0067","card":"q-github-actions-009-manual-and-scheduled-triggers","sec":"github-actions-s010","q":"In GitHub Actions, what time zone do <code>schedule</code> cron expressions use?","a":"<p>UTC. There is no per-workflow time zone setting, so convert local times yourself and remember daylight saving changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0068","card":"q-github-actions-009-manual-and-scheduled-triggers","sec":"github-actions-s010","q":"What is the shortest interval you can schedule in GitHub Actions, and which branch does a scheduled workflow run on?","a":"<p>The shortest interval is every 5 minutes. A scheduled workflow runs from the latest commit on the default branch.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0069","card":"q-github-actions-009-manual-and-scheduled-triggers","sec":"github-actions-s010","q":"What can <code>workflow_dispatch</code> inputs do, and how are they read in the job?","a":"<p>They define a form shown in the Actions tab (or accepted by the API and <code>gh workflow run</code>) with typed values: string, boolean, choice, environment and number. Read them with <code>${{ inputs.environment }}</code> or <code>${{ github.event.inputs.environment }}</code>. Inputs from <code>github.event.inputs</code> are always strings.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on:\n  workflow_dispatch:\n    inputs:\n      environment:\n        description: &#x27;Environment&#x27;\n        required: true\n        default: &#x27;staging&#x27;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0070","card":"q-github-actions-009-manual-and-scheduled-triggers","sec":"github-actions-s010","q":"Write the cron expression for \"every weekday at 08:30 UTC\".","a":"<p><code>30 8 * * 1-5</code>. The fields are minute, hour, day of month, month, day of week.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0071","card":"q-github-actions-010-cross-repository-triggers","sec":"github-actions-s011","q":"How can you trigger a workflow in one repository from another?","a":"<p>Use <code>repository_dispatch</code> in the target workflow and call the GitHub API from the other repo.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on:\n  repository_dispatch:\n    types: [trigger]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0072","card":"q-github-actions-010-cross-repository-triggers","sec":"github-actions-s011","q":"How do you trigger a workflow in repository B from a workflow in repository A, and what authentication is needed?","a":"<p>Call the REST endpoint <code>POST /repos/{owner}/{repo}/dispatches</code> with an <code>event_type</code> (and optional <code>client_payload</code>). The target workflow listens for <code>repository_dispatch</code>. The default <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> cannot act on another repository, so use a PAT, a fine-grained token or a GitHub App token with access to repository B. The target workflow must be on its default branch.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0073","card":"q-github-actions-011-environment-variables-and-contexts","sec":"github-actions-s012","q":"How do you define environment variables?","a":"<p>Use <code>env</code> at workflow, job or step level.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>env:\n  GLOBAL: &quot;value&quot;\njobs:\n  build:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    env:\n      JOB: &quot;job-value&quot;\n    steps:\n      - env:\n          STEP: &quot;step-value&quot;\n        run: echo $STEP</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0074","card":"q-github-actions-011-environment-variables-and-contexts","sec":"github-actions-s012","q":"What is the <code>env</code> context compared with <code>github</code> and <code>secrets</code>?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>env</strong>: custom variables (<code>${{ env.VAR }}</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>github</strong>: event data (<code>${{ github.sha }}</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>secrets</strong>: secure data (<code>${{ secrets.KEY }}</code>).</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0075","card":"q-github-actions-011-environment-variables-and-contexts","sec":"github-actions-s012","q":"In GitHub Actions, if the same variable name is set at workflow, job and step level, which value does a step see?","a":"<p>The most specific one. Step-level <code>env</code> overrides job-level, which overrides workflow-level. This lets you set defaults broadly and override locally.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0076","card":"q-github-actions-011-environment-variables-and-contexts","sec":"github-actions-s012","q":"What is the difference between <code>${{ env.VAR }}</code> and <code>$VAR</code> in a <code>run</code> step?","a":"<p><code>${{ env.VAR }}</code> is an expression evaluated by GitHub before the step starts and substituted into the script text. <code>$VAR</code> is read by the shell at run time from the process environment. The second is safer against injection, and the first is required in places the shell does not apply, such as <code>with:</code> inputs or <code>if:</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0077","card":"q-github-actions-011-environment-variables-and-contexts","sec":"github-actions-s012","q":"Which contexts are available in GitHub Actions, and what is each for?","a":"<p><code>github</code> (event and repo data), <code>env</code>, <code>vars</code> (configuration variables), <code>secrets</code>, <code>job</code>, <code>steps</code> (outputs of earlier steps), <code>runner</code> (OS and paths), <code>matrix</code>, <code>needs</code> (outputs of dependent jobs), and <code>inputs</code>. Availability depends on where you are in the YAML, for example <code>matrix</code> is not available at workflow-level <code>env</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0078","card":"q-github-actions-011-environment-variables-and-contexts","sec":"github-actions-s012","q":"What is the difference between configuration variables (<code>vars</code>) and secrets?","a":"<p>Variables are plain text and visible in logs, for non-sensitive settings such as a region or feature flag. Secrets are encrypted and masked. Both can be set at repository, environment or organization level. Do not put sensitive values in variables.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0079","card":"q-github-actions-011-environment-variables-and-contexts","sec":"github-actions-s012","q":"What is the difference between <code>github.sha</code>, <code>github.ref</code> and <code>github.ref_name</code>?","a":"<p><code>github.sha</code> is the commit SHA that triggered the run. <code>github.ref</code> is the full ref, such as <code>refs/heads/main</code> or <code>refs/tags/v1</code>. <code>github.ref_name</code> is the short name, such as <code>main</code>. For pull request events <code>github.ref</code> is <code>refs/pull/&lt;n&gt;/merge</code>, not the branch.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0080","card":"q-github-actions-012-running-steps-in-a-container","sec":"github-actions-s013","q":"How can you use Docker containers in a workflow?","a":"<p>Run the job in a container with <code>container</code>, or call Docker commands in steps.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>container: node:16</code></pre></div>\n<p>A job-level container can also be written with <code>image</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>jobs:\n  custom:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    container:\n      image: node:16\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n      - run: node --version</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0081","card":"q-github-actions-012-running-steps-in-a-container","sec":"github-actions-s013","q":"When should a GitHub Actions job use a <code>container:</code> instead of installing tools on the runner?","a":"<p>When you need a specific, reproducible toolchain or OS libraries that are not on the runner image, or you want the same environment as production or local development. The cost is image pull time. Container jobs are supported only on Linux runners.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0082","card":"q-github-actions-012-running-steps-in-a-container","sec":"github-actions-s013","q":"What is the difference between a job <code>container</code> and a <code>uses: docker://...</code> step?","a":"<p>A job <code>container</code> runs all steps of the job inside the container. A <code>docker://</code> action step runs only that one step in a container, and the other steps run on the runner. Use the job container for a whole consistent environment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0083","card":"q-github-actions-012-running-steps-in-a-container","sec":"github-actions-s013","q":"How do you run a database for tests alongside a job in GitHub Actions?","a":"<p>Use <code>services:</code> to define service containers (for example postgres or redis) with ports and health checks. When the job itself runs in a container, reach the service by its label name as the hostname. When the job runs directly on the runner, use <code>localhost</code> with the mapped port.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0084","card":"q-github-actions-013-matrix-builds","sec":"github-actions-s014","q":"How do you set up a matrix strategy?","a":"<p>Use <code>strategy.matrix</code> to run a job across configurations such as OS or language versions.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>jobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ${{ matrix.os }}\n    strategy:\n      matrix:\n        os: [ubuntu-latest, windows-latest]\n        node: [14, 16]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0085","card":"q-github-actions-013-matrix-builds","sec":"github-actions-s014","q":"How do you test across multiple versions of a language?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>strategy:\n  matrix:\n    python-version: [&#x27;3.8&#x27;, &#x27;3.9&#x27;]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0086","card":"q-github-actions-013-matrix-builds","sec":"github-actions-s014","q":"How do you run integration tests across Node.js versions?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on: push\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    strategy:\n      matrix:\n        node-version: [14, 16, 18]\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4\n        with:\n          node-version: ${{ matrix.node-version }}\n      - run: npm install &amp;&amp; npm run integration-tests</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0087","card":"q-github-actions-013-matrix-builds","sec":"github-actions-s014","q":"How do you test on Windows and Linux?","a":"<p>Use an OS matrix, and set <code>shell: bash</code> so the same script works on both.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>strategy:\n  matrix:\n    os: [ubuntu-latest, windows-latest]\nsteps:\n  - run: echo &quot;Cross-platform&quot;\n    shell: bash</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0088","card":"q-github-actions-013-matrix-builds","sec":"github-actions-s014","q":"Matrix test across Ubuntu, macOS and Windows?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>jobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ${{ matrix.os }}\n    strategy: { matrix: { os: [ubuntu-latest, macos-latest, windows-latest] } }\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n      - run: echo &quot;Testing on ${{ matrix.os }}&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0089","card":"q-github-actions-013-matrix-builds","sec":"github-actions-s014","q":"Why do matrix builds exist, and what is the pain point they solve?","a":"<p>Without a matrix you would copy a job for each OS or language version. A matrix generates the job combinations from a few lists, so a library is tested across many environments with one definition. It also runs the combinations in parallel.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0090","card":"q-github-actions-013-matrix-builds","sec":"github-actions-s014","q":"By default, what happens to the other matrix jobs when one fails, and how do you change it?","a":"<p>With <code>fail-fast: true</code> (default) GitHub cancels in-progress and queued matrix jobs when one fails. Set <code>fail-fast: false</code> to let all combinations finish, which helps when you want the full compatibility picture. <code>max-parallel</code> caps how many run at once.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0091","card":"q-github-actions-013-matrix-builds","sec":"github-actions-s014","q":"How do <code>include</code> and <code>exclude</code> change a matrix?","a":"<p><code>exclude</code> removes specific combinations, such as macOS with an old Node version. <code>include</code> adds extra combinations or adds extra variables to existing ones, such as an experimental flag for one entry. Together they avoid enumerating every combination by hand.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0092","card":"q-github-actions-013-matrix-builds","sec":"github-actions-s014","q":"In this matrix, how many jobs are created?","a":"<p>Four, the cross product of the two lists (2 OS x 2 Node versions). A single workflow run can generate at most 256 matrix jobs.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>strategy:\n  matrix:\n    os: [ubuntu-latest, windows-latest]\n    node: [14, 16]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0093","card":"q-github-actions-013-matrix-builds","sec":"github-actions-s014","q":"Why does the version list in a Python matrix need quotes, as in <code>['3.8', '3.9']</code>?","a":"<p>YAML parses an unquoted <code>3.10</code> as the float <code>3.1</code>, so Python 3.10 would silently become 3.1. Quoting keeps the value a string. This is a classic pitfall in matrix definitions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0094","card":"q-github-actions-013-matrix-builds","sec":"github-actions-s014","q":"In a matrix job, how does a step use the current matrix value?","a":"<p>Through the <code>matrix</code> context. <code>${{ matrix.os }}</code> resolves to the value for that job instance, and the same works in step inputs such as <code>with: node-version: ${{ matrix.node-version }}</code>. Each combination becomes a separate job with its own runner.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>runs-on: ${{ matrix.os }}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0095","card":"q-github-actions-013-matrix-builds","sec":"github-actions-s014","q":"Why is <code>shell: bash</code> set on steps in a Windows and Linux matrix?","a":"<p>The default shell on Windows runners is PowerShell, whose syntax differs from bash. Setting <code>shell: bash</code> makes one script run the same way on both, since Windows runners include Git Bash. Alternatively set <code>defaults.run.shell</code> once for the whole job.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0096","card":"q-github-actions-013-matrix-builds","sec":"github-actions-s014","q":"How do you mark one matrix combination as allowed to fail without failing the workflow?","a":"<p>Set <code>continue-on-error: ${{ matrix.experimental }}</code> on the job, with an <code>experimental: true</code> flag added through <code>include</code> for that combination. Useful for testing against nightly or pre-release versions without blocking merges.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0097","card":"q-github-actions-014-job-dependencies-with-needs","sec":"github-actions-s015","q":"What is the purpose of <code>needs</code>?","a":"<p>It makes a job run only after the listed jobs finish successfully.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>jobs:\n  build:\n  deploy:\n    needs: build</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0098","card":"q-github-actions-014-job-dependencies-with-needs","sec":"github-actions-s015","q":"How do you run a security scan after the build?","a":"<p>Put the scan in its own job with <code>needs: build</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>jobs:\n  build:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n      - run: echo &quot;Building...&quot;\n  scan:\n    needs: build\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n      - run: echo &quot;Scanning...&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0099","card":"q-github-actions-014-job-dependencies-with-needs","sec":"github-actions-s015","q":"What happens to a job that has <code>needs: build</code> when the build job fails, and how do you run it anyway?","a":"<p>The dependent job is skipped. To run it regardless of the result use <code>if: always()</code>, or <code>if: failure()</code> for a cleanup or notification job. Without an explicit status function, the implicit condition is <code>success()</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0100","card":"q-github-actions-014-job-dependencies-with-needs","sec":"github-actions-s015","q":"How do you make a job wait for several jobs, and how do you read their results?","a":"<p>List them: <code>needs: [build, test]</code>. Read outputs through the <code>needs</code> context, <code>${{ needs.build.outputs.version }}</code>, and the result through <code>${{ needs.build.result }}</code>. Only jobs listed in <code>needs</code> are visible.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0101","card":"q-github-actions-014-job-dependencies-with-needs","sec":"github-actions-s015","q":"Why do jobs that depend on each other not share files automatically?","a":"<p>Each job runs on a separate runner, so the workspace is not shared. To pass files, upload them as an artifact in the first job and download them in the next. To pass small values, use job outputs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0102","card":"q-github-actions-014-job-dependencies-with-needs","sec":"github-actions-s015","q":"In GitHub Actions, how do you declare an output on a job so that later jobs can use it?","a":"<p>Under the job's <code>outputs</code>, map a name to an expression, here the output of the step with <code>id: ver</code>. A downstream job with <code>needs: build</code> then reads <code>needs.build.outputs.version</code>. Outputs are strings and have a size limit, so use artifacts for large data.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>jobs:\n  build:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    outputs:\n      version: ${{ steps.ver.outputs.version }}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0103","card":"q-github-actions-014-job-dependencies-with-needs","sec":"github-actions-s015","q":"What happens if there is a circular dependency in <code>needs</code>?","a":"<p>The workflow is invalid and GitHub rejects it with an error when it parses the file, so no jobs run. Workflows must form a directed acyclic graph.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0104","card":"q-github-actions-015-passing-data-between-steps","sec":"github-actions-s016","q":"How do you pass outputs between steps?","a":"<p>Write <code>name=value</code> to <code>$GITHUB_OUTPUT</code> in a step with an <code>id</code>, then read <code>steps.&lt;id&gt;.outputs.&lt;name&gt;</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps:\n  - id: step1\n    run: echo &quot;output=hello&quot; &gt;&gt; $GITHUB_OUTPUT\n  - run: echo ${{ steps.step1.outputs.output }}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0105","card":"q-github-actions-015-passing-data-between-steps","sec":"github-actions-s016","q":"How can you share data between steps in a job?","a":"<p>Use files, <code>GITHUB_ENV</code>, or artifacts.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- run: echo &quot;VAR=value&quot; &gt;&gt; $GITHUB_ENV\n- run: echo ${{ env.VAR }}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0106","card":"q-github-actions-015-passing-data-between-steps","sec":"github-actions-s016","q":"In GitHub Actions, why was <code>echo \"::set-output name=x::y\"</code> replaced by writing to <code>$GITHUB_OUTPUT</code>?","a":"<p>The <code>set-output</code> and <code>save-state</code> workflow commands were deprecated (announced in October 2022) because of injection risks from untrusted values written to logs. The file-based approach is <code>echo \"name=value\" &gt;&gt; $GITHUB_OUTPUT</code>. Older tutorials still show the old command.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0107","card":"q-github-actions-015-passing-data-between-steps","sec":"github-actions-s016","q":"How do you pass a multi-line value as a step output or environment variable?","a":"<p>Use a heredoc form with a delimiter: write <code>name&lt;&lt;EOF</code>, then the value lines, then <code>EOF</code> into <code>$GITHUB_OUTPUT</code> or <code>$GITHUB_ENV</code>. Pick a random delimiter if the value is untrusted, so the value cannot end the block early.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0108","card":"q-github-actions-015-passing-data-between-steps","sec":"github-actions-s016","q":"What is the difference between a step output and <code>$GITHUB_ENV</code>?","a":"<p>A step output is read explicitly through <code>steps.&lt;id&gt;.outputs.&lt;name&gt;</code> and needs the step <code>id</code>. <code>$GITHUB_ENV</code> adds a variable to the environment of all later steps in the same job, and is read as <code>$VAR</code> or <code>${{ env.VAR }}</code>. Neither crosses jobs, for that use job <code>outputs</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0109","card":"q-github-actions-015-passing-data-between-steps","sec":"github-actions-s016","q":"Why does <code>$GITHUB_ENV</code> not affect the step that writes it?","a":"<p>The environment file is read by the runner after the step finishes, and the values apply to the following steps only. In the same step, use a normal shell variable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0110","card":"q-github-actions-015-passing-data-between-steps","sec":"github-actions-s016","q":"Which mechanism do you choose to move a built binary between jobs, and which for a small version string?","a":"<p>A built binary or report: artifacts (<code>upload-artifact</code> and <code>download-artifact</code>). A small string such as a version or image tag: job <code>outputs</code>. The cache is for speeding up repeated dependency downloads, not for handing results between jobs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0111","card":"q-github-actions-016-conditional-execution","sec":"github-actions-s017","q":"What is the <code>if</code> conditional, and how is it used?","a":"<p>It controls whether a step or job runs, using expressions.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps:\n  - if: github.event_name == &#x27;push&#x27;\n    run: echo &quot;Push only&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0112","card":"q-github-actions-016-conditional-execution","sec":"github-actions-s017","q":"How do you run steps differently for pull requests and pushes?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps:\n  - if: github.event_name == &#x27;pull_request&#x27;\n    run: npm test\n  - if: github.event_name == &#x27;push&#x27;\n    run: npm run deploy</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0113","card":"q-github-actions-016-conditional-execution","sec":"github-actions-s017","q":"How do you run a job only on a given branch?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>jobs:\n  build:\n    if: github.ref == &#x27;refs/heads/main&#x27;\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n      - run: echo &quot;Building on main&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0114","card":"q-github-actions-016-conditional-execution","sec":"github-actions-s017","q":"How do you handle failures (for example continue on error)?","a":"<p>Use <code>continue-on-error: true</code> on a step, or <code>if: failure()</code> to run a step after a failure. Combine them to handle errors gracefully, and avoid overusing <code>continue-on-error</code> because it can mask real issues.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps:\n  - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n  - name: Optional step\n    continue-on-error: true\n    run: curl http://optional-service.com\n  - name: Log if failed\n    if: failure()\n    run: echo &quot;Optional step failed, continuing&quot;\n  - name: Critical step\n    run: ./build.sh</code></pre></div>\n<p>Typical uses of <code>continue-on-error</code>: notifications to services that may be down, optional linting, and metrics collection that is not critical to deployment.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0115","card":"q-github-actions-016-conditional-execution","sec":"github-actions-s017","q":"Why can an <code>if:</code> expression omit the <code>${{ }}</code> wrapper, and when is it required?","a":"<p>GitHub evaluates <code>if</code> as an expression automatically, so <code>if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'</code> works. The wrapper is needed if the expression starts with <code>!</code>, because a leading <code>!</code> is a YAML special character. In that case write <code>if: ${{ !cancelled() }}</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0116","card":"q-github-actions-016-conditional-execution","sec":"github-actions-s017","q":"What do the status functions <code>success()</code>, <code>failure()</code>, <code>always()</code> and <code>cancelled()</code> do?","a":"<p><code>success()</code> is true if no previous step failed (the default). <code>failure()</code> is true if any previous step failed. <code>always()</code> runs regardless of outcome, including cancellation. <code>cancelled()</code> is true if the run was cancelled. Use <code>always()</code> for cleanup and artifact upload, though <code>!cancelled()</code> is often preferred so that cancelled runs stop.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0117","card":"q-github-actions-016-conditional-execution","sec":"github-actions-s017","q":"What is the difference between <code>continue-on-error: true</code> and <code>if: failure()</code>?","a":"<p><code>continue-on-error</code> lets the step fail without failing the job, and the job continues as success. <code>if: failure()</code> is a condition on a later step that runs only when something earlier failed. They answer different questions, \"tolerate this failure\" and \"react to a failure\".</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0118","card":"q-github-actions-016-conditional-execution","sec":"github-actions-s017","q":"Why is <code>continue-on-error: true</code> considered risky?","a":"<p>It hides real problems, because the job reports success even when the step failed. Use it only for non-critical steps like optional notifications, and avoid it for tests, builds and security scans.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0119","card":"q-github-actions-016-conditional-execution","sec":"github-actions-s017","q":"How do you make a job run only on pushes to <code>main</code>, and not on pull requests?","a":"<p>Add <code>if: github.event_name == 'push' &amp;&amp; github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'</code> on the job. A skipped job reports as skipped and does not block merging unless it is a required check.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0120","card":"q-github-actions-016-conditional-execution","sec":"github-actions-s017","q":"In GitHub Actions, what happens to the status of a job skipped by <code>if:</code> when another job lists it in <code>needs</code>?","a":"<p>The dependent job is skipped too, because the implicit <code>success()</code> check fails for a skipped dependency. To continue, add a condition such as <code>if: always() &amp;&amp; needs.x.result != 'failure'</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0121","card":"q-github-actions-017-timeouts","sec":"github-actions-s018","q":"What is <code>timeout-minutes</code>, and why use it?","a":"<p>It cancels a job or step after a time limit, which prevents hangs.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>jobs:\n  build:\n    timeout-minutes: 10</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0122","card":"q-github-actions-017-timeouts","sec":"github-actions-s018","q":"What is the default job timeout in GitHub Actions, and why set a lower one?","a":"<p>360 minutes (6 hours). A hung job would otherwise consume runner minutes for hours, and on self-hosted runners it can block the machine. A realistic limit, such as 10 to 30 minutes, makes hangs fail fast.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0123","card":"q-github-actions-017-timeouts","sec":"github-actions-s018","q":"Can <code>timeout-minutes</code> be set on a step, and what does it do?","a":"<p>Yes, on both jobs and steps. A step timeout stops only that step, which then fails the job unless <code>continue-on-error</code> is set. It suits a single step that tends to hang, such as a network call.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0124","card":"q-github-actions-018-concurrency-control","sec":"github-actions-s019","q":"How does GitHub Actions handle concurrency control?","a":"<p><code>concurrency</code> limits simultaneous runs in a group and can cancel in-progress runs for the same context.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\nconcurrency:\n  group: ${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.ref }}\n  cancel-in-progress: true</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0125","card":"q-github-actions-018-concurrency-control","sec":"github-actions-s019","q":"What problem does <code>concurrency</code> solve in a deployment workflow?","a":"<p>Two runs can deploy at the same time and race, or an older commit can finish after a newer one and overwrite it. A concurrency group allows only one run at a time per group, so deploys are serialized.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0126","card":"q-github-actions-018-concurrency-control","sec":"github-actions-s019","q":"In this snippet, what does <code>cancel-in-progress: true</code> change, and when is it a bad idea?","a":"<p>When a new run starts in the same group, the one already running is cancelled. It fits CI on pull requests, where only the latest commit matters. For production deployments it is risky, since cancelling halfway through a deploy can leave a half-applied state. Leave it <code>false</code> there so runs queue.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>concurrency:\n  group: ${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.ref }}\n  cancel-in-progress: true</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0127","card":"q-github-actions-018-concurrency-control","sec":"github-actions-s019","q":"If cancel-in-progress is false and three runs are triggered quickly in the same concurrency group, which ones run?","a":"<p>One runs, and only one more can wait as pending. When a newer run arrives, an older pending run is cancelled and replaced, so the middle run is dropped. The latest and the currently running one survive.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0128","card":"q-github-actions-018-concurrency-control","sec":"github-actions-s019","q":"Why is the workflow name plus ref a good concurrency group key?","a":"<p><code>github.workflow</code> keeps different workflows from affecting each other, and <code>github.ref</code> separates branches and PRs, so a new push to one branch cancels only that branch's older run. A fixed group name would serialize everything across all branches.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0129","card":"q-github-actions-019-secrets","sec":"github-actions-s020","q":"How does GitHub Actions handle secrets, and why does it matter?","a":"<p>Secrets are encrypted variables set in repository settings and read as <code>${{ secrets.NAME }}</code>. They are masked in logs. They keep sensitive data such as API keys out of the code.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0130","card":"q-github-actions-019-secrets","sec":"github-actions-s020","q":"How do you use a secret in a step?","a":"<p>Pass it through <code>env</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps:\n  - env:\n      API_KEY: ${{ secrets.API_KEY }}\n    run: ./deploy.sh</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0131","card":"q-github-actions-019-secrets","sec":"github-actions-s020","q":"How do you secure a workflow that calls an external API needing auth?","a":"<p>Store the key in secrets, reference <code>${{ secrets.KEY }}</code>, and keep it out of logs.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>env:\n  API_KEY: ${{ secrets.API_KEY }}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0132","card":"q-github-actions-019-secrets","sec":"github-actions-s020","q":"At which levels can GitHub Actions secrets be defined, and which takes precedence?","a":"<p>Repository, environment and organization (plus Dependabot and Codespaces secrets as separate stores). If the same name exists at several levels, the most specific wins: environment over repository over organization. Environment secrets are available only to jobs that reference that environment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0133","card":"q-github-actions-019-secrets","sec":"github-actions-s020","q":"Why does masking secrets in logs not make them fully safe?","a":"<p>Masking matches the exact secret string. If a script transforms the value (base64, splits it, reverses it), the masked form no longer matches and it can leak. Structured values such as JSON should be stored as separate secrets. Also, anyone who can edit the workflow can print a secret in an obfuscated way, so restrict who can push workflows.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0134","card":"q-github-actions-019-secrets","sec":"github-actions-s020","q":"How are secrets passed to a reusable workflow in GitHub Actions?","a":"<p>Secrets are not inherited automatically by a reusable workflow. The caller passes them with <code>secrets:</code> or <code>secrets: inherit</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0135","card":"q-github-actions-019-secrets","sec":"github-actions-s020","q":"Why prefer passing a secret through <code>env</code> and not inline in the <code>run</code> command?","a":"<p>With <code>env</code> the value lives only in that step's process environment and the script reads it as <code>$API_KEY</code>. Inline interpolation puts the value in the script text, which can appear in logs, process lists and error output, and allows injection if it contains shell characters. <code>env</code> also limits exposure: set it on the step that needs the secret, not at job or workflow level, so the fewest steps can read it.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps:\n  - env:\n      API_KEY: ${{ secrets.API_KEY }}\n    run: ./deploy.sh</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0136","card":"q-github-actions-019-secrets","sec":"github-actions-s020","q":"Is there a size limit or naming rule for secrets?","a":"<p>A secret can be up to 48 KB. Names may contain only alphanumeric characters and underscores, cannot start with <code>GITHUB_</code> or a number, and are case-insensitive. For larger values, encrypt the file and store the key as the secret.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0137","card":"q-github-actions-020-github-token-versus-a-personal-access-to","sec":"github-actions-s021","q":"What is <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code>, and how does it differ from a PAT?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>GITHUB_TOKEN</strong>: created automatically for each run, scoped to the repo, expires when the run ends.</li>\n  <li><strong>PAT</strong>: created by a user, customizable scope, persistent.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- run: curl -H &quot;Authorization: token ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}&quot; https://api.github.com/repos/${{ github.repository }}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0138","card":"q-github-actions-020-github-token-versus-a-personal-access-to","sec":"github-actions-s021","q":"Why prefer <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> over a PAT when the workflow acts only on its own repository?","a":"<p>It is created automatically per run, scoped to the repository, limited by the <code>permissions</code> key and expires when the job ends. A PAT is tied to a person, has a long life, and often broader scope. If the person leaves, workflows using their PAT break.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0139","card":"q-github-actions-020-github-token-versus-a-personal-access-to","sec":"github-actions-s021","q":"When is a PAT or a GitHub App token needed in place of <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code>?","a":"<p>When acting on another repository, or when the action must trigger further workflows. Events created with <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> do not start new workflow runs (except <code>workflow_dispatch</code> and <code>repository_dispatch</code>), to prevent recursive loops.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0140","card":"q-github-actions-020-github-token-versus-a-personal-access-to","sec":"github-actions-s021","q":"Does <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> need to be created as a secret?","a":"<p>No. GitHub creates it automatically for each run and available as <code>secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN</code> or <code>github.token</code>. Actions such as <code>actions/checkout</code> use it by default.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0141","card":"q-github-actions-021-the-permissions-key","sec":"github-actions-s022","q":"In GitHub Actions, what does the <code>permissions</code> key control, and why set it explicitly?","a":"<p>It sets the scopes of the automatic <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code>. Setting it explicitly applies least privilege, so a compromised step or third-party action cannot write to the repository. Once any permission is set, all scopes you do not list become <code>none</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0142","card":"q-github-actions-021-the-permissions-key","sec":"github-actions-s022","q":"What is the default <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> permission, and how can it be changed?","a":"<p>Repositories and organizations created recently default to read-only for contents and packages, while older ones may default to read and write. The default is set in the repository, organization or enterprise settings under Actions, and the <code>permissions</code> key in a workflow overrides it. Always check your own setting and declare permissions explicitly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0143","card":"q-github-actions-021-the-permissions-key","sec":"github-actions-s022","q":"In GitHub Actions, what happens in a workflow with this setting when a job needs to push a tag?","a":"<p>The push fails with a 403, since the token has only read access to contents. Grant <code>contents: write</code> at job level for just the job that pushes, and leave the workflow default at read. Job-level <code>permissions</code> override the workflow-level block.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>permissions:\n  contents: read</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0144","card":"q-github-actions-021-the-permissions-key","sec":"github-actions-s022","q":"Which permission do you need to publish a package, to comment on a PR, and to use OIDC?","a":"<p>Publishing a package to GitHub Packages: <code>packages: write</code>. Commenting on a pull request: <code>pull-requests: write</code> (and <code>issues: write</code> for issue comments through the issues API). OIDC: <code>id-token: write</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0145","card":"q-github-actions-021-the-permissions-key","sec":"github-actions-s022","q":"How do you remove all token permissions from a workflow?","a":"<p>Set <code>permissions: {}</code> at workflow level. Then add only the scopes each job needs. This is a good baseline for workflows that only run tests.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0146","card":"q-github-actions-022-securing-a-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s023","q":"How can you secure a workflow against unauthorized access or misuse?","a":"<p>Use secrets, limit <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> permissions, pin actions to a version or commit SHA, use environments, control concurrency and audit workflows. Avoid logging secrets.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Secure Workflow\non: push\npermissions:\n  contents: read\n  issues: write\nconcurrency:\n  group: ${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.ref }}\n  cancel-in-progress: true\njobs:\n  deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v3.0.0\n      - name: Deploy with secret\n        env:\n          API_KEY: ${{ secrets.API_KEY }}\n        run: ./deploy.sh</code></pre></div>\n<p>The token is limited to reading contents and writing issues, <code>checkout</code> is pinned, the secret is passed through <code>env</code>, and only one run per branch executes at a time.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0147","card":"q-github-actions-022-securing-a-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s023","q":"Why pin third-party actions to a full commit SHA?","a":"<p>Tags and branches can be moved or rewritten by the action owner, or by an attacker who compromises the repo, so a pinned tag can change what it runs. A commit SHA is immutable. A tag compromise is a real supply-chain risk, as seen in incidents where popular actions were modified. Dependabot can update SHA-pinned actions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0148","card":"q-github-actions-022-securing-a-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s023","q":"What is script injection in GitHub Actions, and how do you prevent it?","a":"<p>Expressions like <code>${{ github.event.issue.title }}</code> are substituted into a <code>run</code> script before the shell executes it, so a crafted title can run arbitrary commands. Prevent it by passing untrusted values through an <code>env</code> variable and using it as a quoted shell variable, or by using an action that takes the value as an input.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0149","card":"q-github-actions-022-securing-a-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s023","q":"Which settings reduce the blast radius of a compromised workflow?","a":"<p>Least-privilege <code>permissions</code>, OIDC rather than stored cloud keys, environments with required reviewers for production secrets, restricting which actions are allowed at the org level, SHA pinning, and branch protection plus CODEOWNERS on <code>.github/workflows</code>. Avoid self-hosted runners on public repos.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0150","card":"q-github-actions-022-securing-a-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s023","q":"What is the risk of <code>pull_request_target</code> combined with checking out the pull request head?","a":"<p><code>pull_request_target</code> runs with the base repo's secrets and a write token, so running code from the PR head gives an attacker those privileges. Keep untrusted code out of privileged workflows, or do not check out the PR head in them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0151","card":"q-github-actions-023-environments","sec":"github-actions-s024","q":"What are GitHub Actions environments, and how are they used in deployments?","a":"<p>Environments represent deployment targets (dev, staging, production) with protection rules (such as approvals), secrets and variables.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0152","card":"q-github-actions-023-environments","sec":"github-actions-s024","q":"How do you deploy to dev, staging and prod with different configs?","a":"<p>Use environments, environment-specific secrets, conditional jobs and approvals.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>jobs:\n  deploy-prod:\n    environment: production\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - run: ./deploy.sh</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0153","card":"q-github-actions-023-environments","sec":"github-actions-s024","q":"What protection can an environment add that a plain repository secret cannot?","a":"<p>Required reviewers (manual approval before the job runs), a wait timer, restriction to specific branches or tags, and environment-scoped secrets and variables that only jobs targeting that environment can read. A job that references <code>environment: production</code> pauses until the rules pass.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0154","card":"q-github-actions-023-environments","sec":"github-actions-s024","q":"Where are secrets for production stored, and why there?","a":"<p>As environment secrets on the <code>production</code> environment, so only jobs that declare that environment, after approval, can read them. A repository secret is available to every job in every workflow, which is too broad for production credentials.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0155","card":"q-github-actions-023-environments","sec":"github-actions-s024","q":"How do you approve a production deploy in a pipeline with a staging stage first?","a":"<p>Make the production job depend on the staging job with <code>needs</code>, and give it <code>environment: production</code> with required reviewers. After staging succeeds the production job waits for approval, and then runs with production secrets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0156","card":"q-github-actions-024-credentials-exposed-in-a-repo","sec":"github-actions-s025","q":"A junior developer pushes credentials to a public repo. How do you mitigate and prevent it?","a":"<p>Remove the credentials, rotate the secrets, move them into GitHub Secrets, enable secret scanning and train the team. Remove the secret from history as well.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- run: deploy.sh\n  env:\n    API_KEY: ${{ secrets.API_KEY }}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0157","card":"q-github-actions-024-credentials-exposed-in-a-repo","sec":"github-actions-s025","q":"After a secret was committed, why is rotating it more important than deleting the commit?","a":"<p>Anyone could have cloned the repo or scraped it (automated bots scan public pushes within minutes), and git history, forks and caches keep copies. Treat the credential as compromised: revoke and rotate first, then clean history. Deleting alone does not remove copies.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0158","card":"q-github-actions-024-credentials-exposed-in-a-repo","sec":"github-actions-s025","q":"How do you remove a committed secret from git history?","a":"<p>Rewrite history with <code>git filter-repo</code> or BFG Repo-Cleaner, force push, and ask collaborators to re-clone. Contact GitHub support to purge cached views and pull request refs if needed. Cleaning history is a second step after rotation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0159","card":"q-github-actions-024-credentials-exposed-in-a-repo","sec":"github-actions-s025","q":"Which GitHub features help prevent credentials from being pushed?","a":"<p>Secret scanning detects known token formats and alerts, and push protection blocks the push when a secret is detected. Pre-commit hooks such as gitleaks add a local check, and a <code>.gitignore</code> covers files like <code>.env</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0160","card":"q-github-actions-025-security-scans-before-deployment","sec":"github-actions-s026","q":"How do you run a security scan before deployment?","a":"<p>Use Dependabot or tools such as Snyk, and fail the job on vulnerabilities.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- run: snyk test\n  env:\n    SNYK_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.SNYK_TOKEN }}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0161","card":"q-github-actions-025-security-scans-before-deployment","sec":"github-actions-s026","q":"Which kinds of security scans fit into a CI pipeline before deployment?","a":"<p>SAST for source code (CodeQL, SonarQube), dependency or SCA scans (Dependabot, Snyk, <code>npm audit</code>), secret scanning, container image scanning (Trivy), and IaC scanning (Checkov, tfsec). Each finds a different class of issue.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0162","card":"q-github-actions-025-security-scans-before-deployment","sec":"github-actions-s026","q":"How do you make a vulnerability scan block a deployment?","a":"<p>Run the scan in its own job, have the scanner exit non-zero when findings reach a severity threshold, and make the deploy job <code>needs</code> the scan. Pair it with branch protection that requires the scan check. A scan that only reports and never fails does not gate anything.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0163","card":"q-github-actions-026-caching-dependencies","sec":"github-actions-s027","q":"How does GitHub Actions support caching, and what are the steps to cache npm or pip?","a":"<p><code>actions/cache</code> stores files or directories under a unique key and restores them when the key matches. If there is no match, a new cache is saved for future runs. The cache is scoped to the repository and branch, with optional restore from parent branches.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>path</strong>: files or directories to cache (<code>~/.m2/repository</code>, <code>node_modules</code>, <code>~/.npm</code>, <code>~/.cache/pip</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>key</strong>: unique identifier, usually including a file hash so the cache is invalidated when dependencies change.</li>\n  <li><strong>restore-keys</strong>: fallback keys used when the primary key does not match.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Use cases: build tools (Maven, Gradle), package managers (npm, Yarn, pip), compiled artifacts and intermediate build outputs. Use <code>hashFiles</code> on dependency files (<code>pom.xml</code>, <code>package-lock.json</code>) and specify multiple paths for complex projects.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0164","card":"q-github-actions-026-caching-dependencies","sec":"github-actions-s027","q":"What is the purpose of <code>restore-keys</code> in <code>actions/cache</code>?","a":"<p>It lists fallback key prefixes tried when no cache matches the exact <code>key</code>. You get a partially useful old cache (for example from before a dependency change), and the new full cache is saved at the end of the job. It speeds up installs, since only changed packages are downloaded.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0165","card":"q-github-actions-026-caching-dependencies","sec":"github-actions-s027","q":"Why does a good cache key contain a hash of the lockfile?","a":"<p>When the lockfile changes, the key changes and a fresh cache is built, so you do not use stale dependencies. Without the hash a key like <code>npm-cache</code> would be a hit forever and never refresh. Use <code>hashFiles('**/package-lock.json')</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0166","card":"q-github-actions-026-caching-dependencies","sec":"github-actions-s027","q":"Which branches can use a cache created on another branch?","a":"<p>A run can restore caches created on its own branch, the base branch of a pull request and the default branch. Caches from one feature branch are not visible to a sibling branch. So warm the cache on the default branch to let all PRs benefit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0167","card":"q-github-actions-026-caching-dependencies","sec":"github-actions-s027","q":"What is a simpler way to cache dependencies for common languages?","a":"<p>The <code>setup-*</code> actions have built-in caching, such as <code>actions/setup-node</code> with <code>cache: npm</code> or <code>actions/setup-python</code> with <code>cache: pip</code>. They generate the key from the lockfile for you, and replace a hand-written <code>actions/cache</code> step.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0168","card":"q-github-actions-026-caching-dependencies","sec":"github-actions-s027","q":"In which step does <code>actions/cache</code> save the cache, and what happens if the job fails?","a":"<p>The save happens in a post step at the end of the job, only if the job succeeds and there was no exact hit. If the job fails, the cache is not saved by the plain <code>actions/cache</code> action. Newer versions provide separate <code>restore</code> and <code>save</code> sub-actions for finer control.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0169","card":"q-github-actions-026-caching-dependencies","sec":"github-actions-s027","q":"In GitHub Actions, what limits apply to the cache, and what does that mean for relying on it?","a":"<p>Each repository has a total cache size cap. Entries not accessed for 7 days are evicted, and the oldest entries are evicted when the cap is exceeded. A cache is only an optimisation, so the workflow must still work on a cache miss.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0170","card":"q-github-actions-027-cache-examples","sec":"github-actions-s028","q":"In this cache step, what does each <code>path</code> entry do, and why list two tool directories?","a":"<p><code>path</code> lists the directories saved to and restored from the cache. Maven stores downloaded dependencies in <code>~/.m2/repository</code> and Gradle in <code>~/.gradle/caches</code>. Both are listed here because the workflow may use either build tool. Cache downloaded packages, not build output you need to be correct.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>path: |\n  ~/.m2/repository\n  ~/.gradle/caches</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0171","card":"q-github-actions-027-cache-examples","sec":"github-actions-s028","q":"Why is <code>runner.os</code> part of this cache key?","a":"<p>Caches built on one OS can contain OS-specific binaries or paths that do not work on another. Including the OS keeps Linux, Windows and macOS caches separate. <code>hashFiles</code> over <code>pom.xml</code> refreshes the cache when dependencies change.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>key: ${{ runner.os }}-maven-${{ hashFiles(&#x27;**/pom.xml&#x27;) }}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0172","card":"q-github-actions-027-cache-examples","sec":"github-actions-s028","q":"Which directory do you cache for npm, and why not <code>node_modules</code>?","a":"<p>Cache the npm download cache <code>~/.npm</code>, and run <code>npm ci</code>, which installs reproducibly from the lockfile and removes <code>node_modules</code> first. Caching <code>node_modules</code> can hide a stale or mismatched install and breaks across Node versions.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>path: ~/.npm\nkey: ${{ runner.os }}-npm-${{ hashFiles(&#x27;package-lock.json&#x27;) }}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0173","card":"q-github-actions-027-cache-examples","sec":"github-actions-s028","q":"What is the pip cache path on Linux and how is the key built?","a":"<p>Pip stores downloaded wheels in <code>~/.cache/pip</code> on Linux. The key combines the OS and a hash of <code>requirements.txt</code>, so changing a dependency creates a new cache. On Windows and macOS the pip cache lives elsewhere, so use the <code>setup-python</code> cache option for portability.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>path: ~/.cache/pip\nkey: ${{ runner.os }}-pip-${{ hashFiles(&#x27;requirements.txt&#x27;) }}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0174","card":"q-github-actions-027-cache-examples","sec":"github-actions-s028","q":"What does a cache hit mean for the install step that follows?","a":"<p>The restored directory already holds the downloaded packages, so the package manager finds them locally and skips the network. The install command still runs, but much faster. Use the <code>cache-hit</code> output to skip steps when the full set is restored, such as skipping install when <code>node_modules</code> itself is cached.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0175","card":"q-github-actions-028-speeding-up-a-slow-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s029","q":"A workflow takes too long and delays feedback. How do you optimize it?","a":"<p>Cache dependencies, parallelize jobs, skip unnecessary steps with <code>if</code>, optimize commands and use faster runners.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0176","card":"q-github-actions-028-speeding-up-a-slow-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s029","q":"A workflow is too slow (about 20 minutes). What do you do?","a":"<p>The same measures: cache, parallelize, skip steps.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0177","card":"q-github-actions-028-speeding-up-a-slow-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s029","q":"A workflow fails intermittently because of network issues while downloading dependencies. How do you fix it?","a":"<p>Cache dependencies, use a proxy, retry steps and increase timeouts.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- uses: nick-invision/retry@v2\n  with:\n    command: npm install</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0178","card":"q-github-actions-028-speeding-up-a-slow-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s029","q":"A pipeline takes 20 minutes. How do you find where the time goes before optimizing?","a":"<p>Open the run's timing view to see per-job and per-step durations and find the longest step or the critical path of <code>needs</code>. Optimize that first. Guessing leads to optimizing parts that do not matter.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0179","card":"q-github-actions-028-speeding-up-a-slow-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s029","q":"Which changes shorten a slow GitHub Actions pipeline?","a":"<p>Cache dependencies, run independent jobs in parallel, split tests across matrix shards, skip work with <code>paths</code> filters, fail fast on cheap checks first, cancel superseded runs with <code>concurrency</code>, use smaller Docker layers with build cache, and use larger runners where compute-bound.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0180","card":"q-github-actions-028-speeding-up-a-slow-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s029","q":"How do you handle a flaky download that fails sometimes in CI?","a":"<p>Treat the cause first: cache dependencies, use a mirror or registry proxy. Then add limited retries around the network step (a retry action, or a shell loop with backoff) and a step <code>timeout-minutes</code>. Do not retry whole test suites to hide flaky tests.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0181","card":"q-github-actions-028-speeding-up-a-slow-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s029","q":"Why does running jobs in parallel not always shorten the pipeline?","a":"<p>Each job has a startup cost (runner provisioning, checkout, dependency install), and jobs that depend on each other still run in order. Splitting a short job into many adds overhead and uses more billable minutes. Parallelize long, independent work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0182","card":"q-github-actions-029-reducing-actions-minutes","sec":"github-actions-s030","q":"A workflow exceeds the free-tier minutes. How do you analyze and reduce usage?","a":"<p>Check billing, cache dependencies, limit triggers, and use self-hosted runners.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0183","card":"q-github-actions-029-reducing-actions-minutes","sec":"github-actions-s030","q":"A pipeline consumes too many minutes. How do you reduce them?","a":"<p>Cache dependencies, parallelize, skip steps and use self-hosted runners.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0184","card":"q-github-actions-029-reducing-actions-minutes","sec":"github-actions-s030","q":"How do you find out which workflows consume the most Actions minutes?","a":"<p>Check billing and usage in the account or organization settings, which breaks usage down by repository, workflow and runner OS. Start with the largest consumers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0185","card":"q-github-actions-029-reducing-actions-minutes","sec":"github-actions-s030","q":"How do multipliers affect Actions minute consumption on private repositories?","a":"<p>Windows minutes count at 2x and macOS at 10x of Linux minutes (rates are set by GitHub and can change). Moving jobs that do not need Windows or macOS to Linux is a direct saving. Check the current pricing page for the numbers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0186","card":"q-github-actions-029-reducing-actions-minutes","sec":"github-actions-s030","q":"Which trigger changes reduce wasted runs?","a":"<p>Use <code>paths</code> and <code>branches</code> filters, <code>paths-ignore</code> for docs, run expensive tests only on pull requests to main, use <code>concurrency</code> to cancel superseded runs, and <code>timeout-minutes</code> so hung jobs end early. Schedule heavy jobs less often.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0187","card":"q-github-actions-029-reducing-actions-minutes","sec":"github-actions-s030","q":"What is a trade-off of moving to self-hosted runners to save minutes?","a":"<p>Self-hosted runner usage is not billed as Actions minutes, but you pay for the machines and take on patching, scaling and security work. It pays off for high volume or special hardware, not for small workloads.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0188","card":"q-github-actions-030-artifacts","sec":"github-actions-s031","q":"What are artifacts, and how are they used?","a":"<p>Artifacts are files (builds, reports) uploaded with <code>actions/upload-artifact</code> for sharing between jobs or for debugging.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Upload Artifact\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\njobs:\n  build:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n      - run: zip -r build.zip .\n      - uses: actions/upload-artifact@v3\n        with:\n          name: build-artifact\n          path: build.zip</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0189","card":"q-github-actions-030-artifacts","sec":"github-actions-s031","q":"How do you run a linter and upload its output?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps:\n  - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n  - run: npm install &amp;&amp; npm run lint &gt; lint-output.txt 2&gt;&amp;1\n  - uses: actions/upload-artifact@v3\n    with:\n      name: lint-report\n      path: lint-output.txt</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0190","card":"q-github-actions-030-artifacts","sec":"github-actions-s031","q":"How do you clean old artifacts after a run?","a":"<p>Use a cleanup step that only runs on success.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- if: success()\n  uses: actions/delete-package-versions@v4\n  with:\n    package-type: &#x27;artifact&#x27;\n    min-versions-to-keep: 1</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0191","card":"q-github-actions-030-artifacts","sec":"github-actions-s031","q":"What is the difference between artifacts and the cache in GitHub Actions?","a":"<p>Artifacts are the outputs of a run, uploaded for download, sharing between jobs or debugging, and kept for a retention period (90 days by default, configurable). The cache is for reusing dependencies between runs and is evicted automatically. Artifacts are expected to exist, the cache may not.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0192","card":"q-github-actions-030-artifacts","sec":"github-actions-s031","q":"How do you hand a built file from one job to a later job in GitHub Actions?","a":"<p>Upload it in the first job with <code>actions/upload-artifact</code> and download it in the later job with <code>actions/download-artifact</code>, with <code>needs</code> to order them. The second job runs on a new runner, so files are not shared otherwise.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0193","card":"q-github-actions-030-artifacts","sec":"github-actions-s031","q":"Why upload test reports and logs as artifacts even when the tests fail?","a":"<p>A failed run is when you need the report most. Steps after a failure are skipped by default, so add <code>if: always()</code> (or <code>if: ${{ !cancelled() }}</code>) to the upload step so the artifact is saved either way.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0194","card":"q-github-actions-030-artifacts","sec":"github-actions-s031","q":"What changed between <code>actions/upload-artifact</code> v3 and v4?","a":"<p>v3 was deprecated and stopped working on GitHub.com in January 2025, so workflows must use v4 or later. In v4 artifacts are immutable and each name must be unique within a run, so a matrix needs a unique name per job, for example including <code>${{ matrix.os }}</code>. Uploads are also faster.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0195","card":"q-github-actions-030-artifacts","sec":"github-actions-s031","q":"How do you control how long an uploaded artifact is kept?","a":"<p>Set <code>retention-days</code> on <code>upload-artifact</code>, within the limit set by the repository or organization settings. Short retention reduces storage cost for large build outputs. The default is 90 days.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0196","card":"q-github-actions-030-artifacts","sec":"github-actions-s031","q":"In this GitHub Actions step that calls <code>actions/delete-package-versions</code> with <code>package-type: 'artifact'</code>, what is that action meant for, and is it the right tool for workflow artifacts?","a":"<p>That action is designed to delete versions of packages in GitHub Packages, not workflow run artifacts, so <code>package-type: 'artifact'</code> is not a documented option. To manage run artifacts, set <code>retention-days</code> on upload, use the REST API to delete artifacts, or use a purpose-built cleanup action.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- if: success()\n  uses: actions/delete-package-versions@v4\n  with:\n    package-type: &#x27;artifact&#x27;\n    min-versions-to-keep: 1</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0197","card":"q-github-actions-030-artifacts","sec":"github-actions-s031","q":"With <code>actions/download-artifact</code> v4, how do you download artifacts?","a":"<p>Download one by <code>name</code>, or several with <code>pattern</code> (add <code>merge-multiple: true</code> to put them in one directory). To get artifacts from another workflow run, set <code>run-id</code> and pass a <code>github-token</code> with access to that run.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0198","card":"q-github-actions-031-composite-actions","sec":"github-actions-s032","q":"What are composite actions, and how do you create one?","a":"<p>A composite action bundles steps into one reusable unit. Create it in <code>.github/actions/my-action/action.yml</code>. Each <code>run</code> step needs a <code>shell</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: &#x27;My Action&#x27;\nruns:\n  using: &#x27;composite&#x27;\n  steps:\n    - run: echo &quot;Step 1&quot;\n      shell: bash\n    - run: echo &quot;Step 2&quot;\n      shell: bash</code></pre></div>\n<p>Use it with <code>uses: ./.github/actions/my-action</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0199","card":"q-github-actions-031-composite-actions","sec":"github-actions-s032","q":"In GitHub Actions, when would you write a composite action instead of a reusable workflow?","a":"<p>Write a composite action when you want to share a few steps that run inside the caller's job, on the caller's runner and workspace. Write a reusable workflow when you want to share whole jobs (with their own runners, <code>needs</code>, environments and secrets). A composite action is called as one step with <code>uses</code>. A reusable workflow is called as a job with <code>uses</code> at job level.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0200","card":"q-github-actions-031-composite-actions","sec":"github-actions-s032","q":"In a GitHub Actions composite action, how does the action receive values from the workflow that calls it, and how does a step read them?","a":"<p>Declare an <code>inputs:</code> section in <code>action.yml</code> and pass values with <code>with:</code> in the calling step. Steps read them as <code>${{ inputs.name }}</code>. A composite action cannot read the <code>secrets</code> context directly, so the caller must pass a secret in as an input.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: &#x27;My Action&#x27;\nruns:\n  using: &#x27;composite&#x27;\n  steps:\n    - run: echo &quot;Step 1&quot;\n      shell: bash</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0201","card":"q-github-actions-031-composite-actions","sec":"github-actions-s032","q":"In a local composite action, why does a workflow need an <code>actions/checkout</code> step before <code>uses: ./.github/actions/my-action</code>?","a":"<p>A local action path is resolved against the files on the runner. Until the repository is checked out, <code>action.yml</code> does not exist there, so the step fails with an error that the action cannot be found. Actions referenced as <code>owner/repo@ref</code> are downloaded by the runner and do not need a checkout.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0202","card":"q-github-actions-031-composite-actions","sec":"github-actions-s032","q":"What is the trade-off between keeping a composite action in the same repository (<code>./.github/actions/...</code>) and in its own repository?","a":"<p>A same-repo action changes together with the workflows that use it, needs no versioning and is easy to review in one pull request. A separate repository lets many repositories share it, and you can version it with tags (<code>@v1</code>) and release it. The cost is that every consumer has to upgrade, and the action must be public or the repo must allow access to it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0203","card":"q-github-actions-031-composite-actions","sec":"github-actions-s032","q":"In a GitHub Actions composite action, why must every <code>run</code> step declare <code>shell:</code>?","a":"<p>Unlike a workflow job, a composite action has no default shell, so each <code>run</code> step must state which shell runs it (for example <code>shell: bash</code>). The workflow validation fails without it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0204","card":"q-github-actions-032-reusable-workflows","sec":"github-actions-s033","q":"What are reusable workflows, and when would you use them?","a":"<p>They are callable YAML workflows for shared tasks, used to standardize pipelines. Define one with <code>workflow_call</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># .github/workflows/reusable.yml\non:\n  workflow_call:\n    inputs:\n      param:\n        required: true\n        type: string\njobs:\n  run:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - run: echo ${{ inputs.param }}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Call it from another workflow:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>jobs:\n  call:\n    uses: ./.github/workflows/reusable.yml\n    with:\n      param: &quot;Hello&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0205","card":"q-github-actions-032-reusable-workflows","sec":"github-actions-s033","q":"In GitHub Actions, how do you call a reusable workflow stored in another repository, and how do you pin it?","a":"<p>Use <code>uses</code> at the job level: <code>uses: owner/repo/.github/workflows/build.yml@ref</code>. The ref can be a tag, a branch or a full commit SHA. A SHA is the strictest pin. A local reusable workflow uses <code>./.github/workflows/file.yml</code> and runs the version from the same commit as the caller.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0206","card":"q-github-actions-032-reusable-workflows","sec":"github-actions-s033","q":"A reusable workflow needs a deploy token. How do you pass secrets from the caller in GitHub Actions?","a":"<p>Declare the secret under <code>on.workflow_call.secrets</code> in the reusable workflow, and pass it in the caller with <code>secrets:</code>. If the called workflow is in the same organization or enterprise, <code>secrets: inherit</code> passes all the caller's secrets. Secrets are not passed automatically otherwise.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0207","card":"q-github-actions-032-reusable-workflows","sec":"github-actions-s033","q":"How does a reusable workflow return a value to the workflow that called it in GitHub Actions?","a":"<p>Declare <code>outputs</code> under <code>on.workflow_call</code>, mapped to a job output inside the reusable workflow (<code>value: ${{ jobs.build.outputs.version }}</code>). The caller reads it as <code>${{ needs.&lt;call-job-id&gt;.outputs.version }}</code> in a later job.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0208","card":"q-github-actions-032-reusable-workflows","sec":"github-actions-s033","q":"Can a job that calls a reusable workflow also have its own <code>steps</code> or <code>runs-on</code> in GitHub Actions?","a":"<p>No. A job that uses <code>uses:</code> can only set a small set of keys such as <code>with</code>, <code>secrets</code>, <code>needs</code>, <code>if</code>, <code>strategy</code> and <code>permissions</code>. The called workflow defines its own jobs, runners and steps. This is why you cannot add an extra step before or after it. Put that work in another job linked with <code>needs</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0209","card":"q-github-actions-032-reusable-workflows","sec":"github-actions-s033","q":"How do reusable workflows help when 30 microservice repositories need the same build and deploy pipeline?","a":"<p>Keep one pipeline definition in a central repository and have each service call it with <code>uses</code> and <code>with</code> inputs (language version, service name). A fix or security change is made once and rolled out by moving a tag. The trade-off is that a bad change to the shared workflow can break every consumer, so version it and release with tags instead of pointing everyone at <code>main</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0210","card":"q-github-actions-032-reusable-workflows","sec":"github-actions-s033","q":"In GitHub Actions, how do you define a reusable workflow and pass it inputs?","a":"<p>Give the called workflow an <code>on: workflow_call</code> trigger with typed <code>inputs</code> (and optional <code>secrets</code>). The caller uses the workflow at job level with <code>uses: ./.github/workflows/build.yml</code> and passes values with <code>with:</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0211","card":"q-github-actions-033-popular-marketplace-actions","sec":"github-actions-s034","q":"Why is it risky to use an unreviewed third-party action from the GitHub Marketplace, and how do you reduce the risk?","a":"<p>An action runs inside your job with access to the workspace, the job's <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> and any secrets you pass to it. A malicious or compromised action can steal them. Reduce the risk by preferring actions from GitHub (<code>actions/*</code>) or verified creators, reading the source, pinning to a full commit SHA, limiting <code>permissions</code>, and passing only the secrets the action needs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0212","card":"q-github-actions-033-popular-marketplace-actions","sec":"github-actions-s034","q":"What does it mean that a Marketplace action shows a \"verified creator\" badge, and does it make the action safe?","a":"<p>The badge means the publisher's identity has been verified by GitHub as an organization. It does not audit the action's code. You still review the source and pin the version.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0213","card":"q-github-actions-033-popular-marketplace-actions","sec":"github-actions-s034","q":"Why use <code>actions/setup-node</code> instead of relying on the Node.js version preinstalled on the runner?","a":"<p>The preinstalled version changes whenever GitHub updates the runner image, so builds can change without any commit. <code>setup-node</code> installs the exact version you request, supports matrix versions and can cache the package manager's download folder. This makes the build repeatable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0214","card":"q-github-actions-034-deprecated-actions-and-microservices","sec":"github-actions-s035","q":"An action you use is deprecated. What do you do?","a":"<p>Update to a newer version or replicate its behavior manually.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- uses: actions/setup-node@v4</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0215","card":"q-github-actions-034-deprecated-actions-and-microservices","sec":"github-actions-s035","q":"How do you set up CI/CD for microservices in separate repos?","a":"<p>Standardize with reusable workflows, keep repo-specific configs, manage secrets centrally and use cross-repo triggers.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>jobs:\n  build:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n      - run: npm test</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0216","card":"q-github-actions-034-deprecated-actions-and-microservices","sec":"github-actions-s035","q":"Your workflow log shows a warning that an action runs on a deprecated Node.js runtime. What does this mean and what do you do?","a":"<p>JavaScript actions declare a runtime in <code>action.yml</code> (for example <code>using: node20</code>). GitHub retires old runtimes as Node.js versions reach end of life, and actions on old runtimes (such as Node 16) were forced onto newer ones and logged warnings. Upgrade to a newer major version of the action that uses a supported runtime. If none exists, fork it, replace it with another action, or write the same logic as <code>run</code> steps.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0217","card":"q-github-actions-034-deprecated-actions-and-microservices","sec":"github-actions-s035","q":"In GitHub Actions, why is <code>actions/create-release</code> a poor choice for new workflows, and what can you use instead?","a":"<p>The <code>actions/create-release</code> repository is archived and no longer maintained. Use the GitHub CLI in a step (<code>gh release create \"$TAG\" --generate-notes</code>, with <code>GH_TOKEN</code> and <code>contents: write</code>) or a maintained action such as <code>softprops/action-gh-release</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0218","card":"q-github-actions-034-deprecated-actions-and-microservices","sec":"github-actions-s035","q":"You maintain CI/CD for 20 microservices in separate repositories. How do you stop their pipelines from drifting apart?","a":"<p>Put the common stages (build, test, scan, deploy) in reusable workflows in one repository, and keep a thin workflow in each service that calls it with inputs. Store shared secrets and variables at organization level. Use <code>repository_dispatch</code> or <code>workflow_dispatch</code> when one repository must start work in another. Keep service-specific steps in the service repository.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0219","card":"q-github-actions-035-build-and-test-a-node-js-app","sec":"github-actions-s036","q":"Write a simple workflow to build and test a Node.js application.","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Node.js CI\non: [push, pull_request]\njobs:\n  build:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n      - uses: actions/setup-node@v3\n        with: { node-version: &#x27;16&#x27;, cache: &#x27;npm&#x27; }\n      - run: npm ci\n      - run: npm run build\n      - run: npm test</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0220","card":"q-github-actions-035-build-and-test-a-node-js-app","sec":"github-actions-s036","q":"Run tests on push to main.","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on:\n  push:\n    branches:\n      - main\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - run: npm install &amp;&amp; npm test</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0221","card":"q-github-actions-035-build-and-test-a-node-js-app","sec":"github-actions-s036","q":"Run tests on a pull request to <code>develop</code> (Python).","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Test PR\non:\n  pull_request:\n    branches: [develop]\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n      - uses: actions/setup-python@v4\n        with: { python-version: &#x27;3.9&#x27; }\n      - run: pip install -r requirements.txt\n      - run: pytest</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0222","card":"q-github-actions-035-build-and-test-a-node-js-app","sec":"github-actions-s036","q":"Build and test a Go app on push.","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on:\n  push:\n    branches: [&#x27;*&#x27;]\njobs:\n  build-test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n      - uses: actions/setup-go@v3\n        with: { go-version: &#x27;1.18&#x27; }\n      - run: go build ./...\n      - run: go test ./...</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0223","card":"q-github-actions-035-build-and-test-a-node-js-app","sec":"github-actions-s036","q":"Checkout the repo and list files.","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\njobs:\n  list:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n      - run: ls -la</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0224","card":"q-github-actions-035-build-and-test-a-node-js-app","sec":"github-actions-s036","q":"In a GitHub Actions Node.js workflow, why use <code>npm ci</code> and not <code>npm install</code>?","a":"<p><code>npm ci</code> installs exactly what <code>package-lock.json</code> says. It deletes <code>node_modules</code> first, fails if the lockfile and <code>package.json</code> disagree, and never rewrites the lockfile. This gives repeatable builds and is usually faster. <code>npm install</code> can resolve different versions and modify the lockfile.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0225","card":"q-github-actions-035-build-and-test-a-node-js-app","sec":"github-actions-s036","q":"In this GitHub Actions step, what does the <code>cache</code> key do, and what does it cache?","a":"<p><code>cache: 'npm'</code> makes <code>setup-node</code> restore and save the npm download cache (<code>~/.npm</code>), keyed on a hash of the lockfile. It saves you the download time on later runs. It does not cache <code>node_modules</code>. The lockfile must exist, or the step errors.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- uses: actions/setup-node@v3\n  with: { node-version: &#x27;16&#x27;, cache: &#x27;npm&#x27; }</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0226","card":"q-github-actions-035-build-and-test-a-node-js-app","sec":"github-actions-s036","q":"Why should a Node.js CI workflow run the build step as well as the tests?","a":"<p>A build can fail (TypeScript errors, bundler errors, missing imports) even when unit tests pass. Running <code>npm run build</code> in CI catches this before merge, and it proves that the artifact you would deploy can be produced from a clean checkout.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0227","card":"q-github-actions-035-build-and-test-a-node-js-app","sec":"github-actions-s036","q":"A Node.js workflow works on your machine but fails in CI with \"module not found\". What do you check?","a":"<p>Look for a dependency installed globally or only in your local <code>node_modules</code> and missing from <code>package.json</code>. Check that the lockfile is committed and that <code>npm ci</code> was used. Check for case-sensitive file paths (Linux runners are case-sensitive, macOS and Windows machines often are not). Check that the Node version matches your local one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0228","card":"q-github-actions-036-linting","sec":"github-actions-s037","q":"Lint on push to main.","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Lint Code\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\njobs:\n  lint:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n      - uses: actions/setup-node@v3\n        with: { node-version: &#x27;16&#x27; }\n      - run: npm install\n      - run: npm run lint</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0229","card":"q-github-actions-036-linting","sec":"github-actions-s037","q":"How do you run static analysis or linting in Python?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- run: pip install flake8\n- run: flake8 .</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0230","card":"q-github-actions-036-linting","sec":"github-actions-s037","q":"Lint a pull request and comment the result on it.","a":"<p>Save the report, flag a failure, then post it with <code>github-script</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on: pull_request\njobs:\n  lint:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - run: npm install\n      - id: lint\n        run: npm run lint -- --output-file eslint-report.txt || echo &quot;LINT_FAILED=true&quot; &gt;&gt; $GITHUB_ENV\n      - if: env.LINT_FAILED == &#x27;true&#x27;\n        uses: actions/github-script@v6\n        with:\n          script: |\n            const fs = require(&#x27;fs&#x27;);\n            const report = fs.readFileSync(&#x27;eslint-report.txt&#x27;, &#x27;utf8&#x27;);\n            github.rest.issues.createComment({\n              issue_number: context.issue.number,\n              owner: context.repo.owner,\n              repo: context.repo.repo,\n              body: `Linting failed:\\n\\`\\`\\`\\n${report}\\n\\`\\`\\``\n            });\n        env:\n          GITHUB_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0231","card":"q-github-actions-036-linting","sec":"github-actions-s037","q":"In this GitHub Actions lint workflow, the job stays green even when linting fails. Why?","a":"<p>The <code>|| echo ...</code> makes the step exit with code 0 even if lint fails, and nothing later fails the job. The comment is posted, but the check passes. Add a final step such as <code>if: env.LINT_FAILED == 'true'</code> with <code>run: exit 1</code> so the job still fails after the report is posted.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- id: lint\n  run: npm run lint -- --output-file eslint-report.txt || echo &quot;LINT_FAILED=true&quot; &gt;&gt; $GITHUB_ENV\n- if: env.LINT_FAILED == &#x27;true&#x27;\n  uses: actions/github-script@v6</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0232","card":"q-github-actions-036-linting","sec":"github-actions-s037","q":"In <code>npm run lint -- --output-file eslint-report.txt</code>, what does the standalone <code>--</code> do?","a":"<p>It ends npm's own option parsing. Everything after it is passed to the script (here the ESLint command) as-is, so <code>--output-file</code> goes to ESLint and not to npm.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0233","card":"q-github-actions-036-linting","sec":"github-actions-s037","q":"What permission does a GitHub Actions workflow need to post a comment on a pull request through <code>github.rest.issues.createComment</code>, and why can it fail on a fork PR?","a":"<p>The <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> needs <code>pull-requests: write</code> (or <code>issues: write</code>). On <code>pull_request</code> events from forks the token is read-only, so the call fails with a \"Resource not accessible by integration\" error. Use a separate privileged workflow (<code>workflow_run</code>) for commenting, and do not run untrusted code in it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0234","card":"q-github-actions-036-linting","sec":"github-actions-s037","q":"Why run linting as a separate job from tests in a GitHub Actions workflow?","a":"<p>Linting is quick and needs no test services, so a separate job reports style failures within seconds and runs in parallel with the slower tests. Each job also shows its own status check, which you can require separately in branch protection.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0235","card":"q-github-actions-036-linting","sec":"github-actions-s037","q":"Why does <code>flake8 .</code> fail a GitHub Actions step, and what is a modern alternative?","a":"<p>flake8 returns a non-zero exit code when it finds violations, and a non-zero exit code fails a <code>run</code> step. Ruff is a common faster alternative that replaces flake8 and several plugins. Formatters offer a check mode (<code>black --check</code>, <code>prettier --check</code>) that fails without changing files.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0236","card":"q-github-actions-037-code-quality-gate","sec":"github-actions-s038","q":"In ESLint, what does <code>--max-warnings=0</code> change, and why is it used in CI?","a":"<p>By default ESLint exits with a failure code only for errors. Warnings pass. <code>--max-warnings=0</code> makes any warning fail the command, so warnings cannot pile up unnoticed. It is a common way to turn a lint rule into a hard gate.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- run: npx eslint . --max-warnings=0</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0237","card":"q-github-actions-037-code-quality-gate","sec":"github-actions-s038","q":"Why run <code>eslint --fix</code> only locally and not as a gate in CI?","a":"<p><code>--fix</code> rewrites files on the runner, and those changes are thrown away when the job ends. The check would then pass without the fix ever reaching the repository. A gate must only report. Developers run <code>--fix</code> locally (or in a pre-commit hook) and commit the result.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0238","card":"q-github-actions-037-code-quality-gate","sec":"github-actions-s038","q":"In an ESLint config, what do <code>extends: [\"eslint:recommended\"]</code> and a <code>rules</code> entry such as <code>\"no-unused-vars\": \"error\"</code> do?","a":"<p><code>extends</code> loads a shared rule set (here ESLint's recommended rules). <code>rules</code> overrides individual rules, and the value sets the severity: <code>off</code>, <code>warn</code> or <code>error</code>. This <code>.eslintrc.json</code> is the legacy config format. In ESLint v9 the default is a flat config file, <code>eslint.config.js</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{\n  &quot;env&quot;: { &quot;node&quot;: true, &quot;es2021&quot;: true },\n  &quot;extends&quot;: [&quot;eslint:recommended&quot;],\n  &quot;rules&quot;: { &quot;no-unused-vars&quot;: &quot;error&quot; }\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0239","card":"q-github-actions-037-code-quality-gate","sec":"github-actions-s038","q":"How do you turn a passing lint workflow into something that actually blocks merging in GitHub?","a":"<p>Run the workflow on <code>pull_request</code>, then mark its job as a required status check in a branch protection rule or ruleset. Without this, a failed check is only advisory and the merge button still works.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0240","card":"q-github-actions-038-docker-images","sec":"github-actions-s039","q":"Build and push a Docker image.","a":"<p>Log in with <code>docker/login-action</code>, then use <code>docker/build-push-action</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on:\n  push:\n    branches:\n      - main\njobs:\n  build:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: docker/login-action@v3\n        with:\n          username: ${{ secrets.DOCKER_USERNAME }}\n          password: ${{ secrets.DOCKER_PASSWORD }}\n      - uses: docker/build-push-action@v5\n        with:\n          push: true\n          tags: user/app:latest</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0241","card":"q-github-actions-038-docker-images","sec":"github-actions-s039","q":"Build and push to Docker Hub with plain docker commands.","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Build Docker\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\njobs:\n  build:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n      - uses: docker/login-action@v2\n        with:\n          username: ${{ secrets.DOCKER_USERNAME }}\n          password: ${{ secrets.DOCKER_PASSWORD }}\n      - run: |\n          docker build -t ${{ secrets.DOCKER_USERNAME }}/my-app:latest .\n          docker push ${{ secrets.DOCKER_USERNAME }}/my-app:latest</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0242","card":"q-github-actions-038-docker-images","sec":"github-actions-s039","q":"In GitHub Actions, why use <code>docker/login-action</code> and <code>docker/build-push-action</code> instead of plain <code>docker build</code> and <code>docker push</code> commands?","a":"<p><code>build-push-action</code> uses BuildKit through Buildx, so you get layer caching in CI (<code>cache-from</code> and <code>cache-to</code> with <code>type=gha</code>), multi-platform builds, build secrets, and push control from one step. <code>login-action</code> handles registry authentication and logs out at the end of the job. Plain commands work but need manual caching and cleanup.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0243","card":"q-github-actions-038-docker-images","sec":"github-actions-s039","q":"How do you make a GitHub Actions workflow build a Docker image on pull requests but push it only on <code>main</code>?","a":"<p>Build on every event and set push conditionally, for example <code>push: ${{ github.event_name != 'pull_request' }}</code> on <code>build-push-action</code>. Pull request builds then verify the Dockerfile without publishing images from unreviewed code, and they work on forks where secrets are unavailable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0244","card":"q-github-actions-038-docker-images","sec":"github-actions-s039","q":"Why is tagging an image only as <code>latest</code> a problem in a deployment pipeline, and what do you do instead?","a":"<p><code>latest</code> moves, so you cannot tell which commit a running container came from, and rollback to a previous image is not possible. Tag with the commit SHA (and a version for releases). <code>docker/metadata-action</code> can compute these tags and labels.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0245","card":"q-github-actions-038-docker-images","sec":"github-actions-s039","q":"How can a workflow push a Docker image to GitHub Container Registry without storing a registry password?","a":"<p>Log in to <code>ghcr.io</code> with <code>docker/login-action</code> using <code>username: ${{ github.actor }}</code> and <code>password: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}</code>. The job needs <code>permissions: packages: write</code>. Image names must be lowercase.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0246","card":"q-github-actions-038-docker-images","sec":"github-actions-s039","q":"In a GitHub Actions <code>docker/login-action</code> step for Docker Hub, should the <code>password</code> secret hold the account password?","a":"<p>No. Create a Docker Hub access token with the least scope needed (for example read and write for one repository) and store that in the secret. An access token can be revoked on its own and does not expose the account password.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- uses: docker/login-action@v3\n  with:\n    username: ${{ secrets.DOCKER_USERNAME }}\n    password: ${{ secrets.DOCKER_PASSWORD }}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0247","card":"q-github-actions-039-deploying-to-aws","sec":"github-actions-s040","q":"How do you deploy to AWS (or another cloud)?","a":"<p>Use the provider's action, such as <code>aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials</code>, with credentials from secrets.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on:\n  push:\n    branches:\n      - main\njobs:\n  deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v4\n        with:\n          aws-access-key-id: ${{ secrets.AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID }}\n          aws-secret-access-key: ${{ secrets.AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY }}\n          aws-region: us-east-1\n      - run: aws s3 sync ./dist/ s3://my-bucket/</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0248","card":"q-github-actions-039-deploying-to-aws","sec":"github-actions-s040","q":"In GitHub Actions, how do you deploy to AWS without storing an access key and secret key in repository secrets?","a":"<p>Use OpenID Connect. Give the job <code>permissions: id-token: write</code> and <code>contents: read</code>, and use <code>aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials</code> with <code>role-to-assume</code> and <code>aws-region</code>. GitHub issues a short-lived OIDC token, and AWS STS exchanges it for temporary credentials through <code>AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity</code>. There is no long-lived key to leak or rotate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0249","card":"q-github-actions-039-deploying-to-aws","sec":"github-actions-s040","q":"What should the trust policy of an AWS IAM role used with GitHub OIDC check, and why?","a":"<p>It names GitHub's OIDC provider (<code>token.actions.githubusercontent.com</code>) as the federated principal and adds conditions on the token claims: <code>aud</code> equal to <code>sts.amazonaws.com</code>, and <code>sub</code> matching the repository and ref, for example <code>repo:my-org/my-repo:ref:refs/heads/main</code> (or <code>:environment:prod</code>). Without a <code>sub</code> condition, any repository on GitHub could assume the role.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0250","card":"q-github-actions-039-deploying-to-aws","sec":"github-actions-s040","q":"What does <code>aws s3 sync ./dist/ s3://my-bucket/</code> do, and what changes with <code>--delete</code>?","a":"<p>It uploads files that are new or changed in <code>./dist/</code> and skips files that are the same. It does not remove anything from the bucket. With <code>--delete</code>, it also removes objects in the bucket that are missing locally, so the bucket mirrors the build. Use <code>--delete</code> carefully, because it can remove files other tools put there.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0251","card":"q-github-actions-039-deploying-to-aws","sec":"github-actions-s040","q":"After deploying a static site to S3 behind CloudFront from a GitHub Actions workflow, users still see the old files. What is the likely cause and fix?","a":"<p>CloudFront serves cached copies until they expire. Add a step that creates an invalidation (<code>aws cloudfront create-invalidation --distribution-id ID --paths \"/*\"</code>), or use content-hashed file names so each deploy has new URLs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0252","card":"q-github-actions-040-deploy-after-tests-and-multi-stage-deplo","sec":"github-actions-s041","q":"How do you deploy only after tests pass?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>jobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - run: npm test\n  deploy:\n    needs: test\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - run: npm run deploy</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0253","card":"q-github-actions-040-deploy-after-tests-and-multi-stage-deplo","sec":"github-actions-s041","q":"How do you do a multi-stage deployment?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>jobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - run: npm test\n  deploy-dev:\n    needs: test\n    if: github.ref == &#x27;refs/heads/dev&#x27;\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - run: echo &quot;Deploy dev&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0254","card":"q-github-actions-040-deploy-after-tests-and-multi-stage-deplo","sec":"github-actions-s041","q":"In GitHub Actions, if the <code>test</code> job uses a matrix and one combination fails, does a job with <code>needs: test</code> run?","a":"<p>No. A job that needs another job runs only when every job in it succeeded (unless you override with <code>if: always()</code> or similar). A failed matrix leg fails the whole <code>test</code> job, so the deploy job is skipped. This is the behavior you want for a deploy gate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0255","card":"q-github-actions-040-deploy-after-tests-and-multi-stage-deplo","sec":"github-actions-s041","q":"How would you design a dev, staging and production pipeline in GitHub Actions?","a":"<p>Build and test once, then chain deploy jobs with <code>needs</code>. Give each deploy job an <code>environment</code> (dev, staging, production). Add required reviewers on production and restrict which branches can deploy to it. Keep environment-specific secrets and variables in the environments. Deploy the same built artifact or image to each stage instead of rebuilding.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0256","card":"q-github-actions-040-deploy-after-tests-and-multi-stage-deplo","sec":"github-actions-s041","q":"In a multi-stage workflow, what does the <code>if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/dev'</code> condition on a deploy job do?","a":"<p>The job runs only when the workflow was triggered for the <code>dev</code> branch. The <code>test</code> job still runs on every ref, but the dev deployment is skipped elsewhere. <code>needs</code> and <code>if</code> are both required to be satisfied.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>deploy-dev:\n  needs: test\n  if: github.ref == &#x27;refs/heads/dev&#x27;\n  runs-on: ubuntu-latest</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0257","card":"q-github-actions-040-deploy-after-tests-and-multi-stage-deplo","sec":"github-actions-s041","q":"How do you roll back a bad deployment with GitHub Actions?","a":"<p>Redeploy a previous known-good version. A common setup is a <code>workflow_dispatch</code> workflow with an input for the tag, SHA or image tag to deploy, so you can run the same deploy job against the earlier version. Keep old artifacts or images so they can be redeployed. If the deployment platform has its own rollback (ECS, Kubernetes, Lambda aliases), the job can call that.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0258","card":"q-github-actions-041-releases-and-publishing","sec":"github-actions-s042","q":"Automate a GitHub release on a version tag.","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on:\n  push:\n    tags:\n      - &#x27;v*&#x27;\njobs:\n  release:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: actions/create-release@v1\n        env:\n          GITHUB_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}\n        with:\n          tag_name: ${{ github.ref_name }}\n          release_name: Release ${{ github.ref_name }}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0259","card":"q-github-actions-041-releases-and-publishing","sec":"github-actions-s042","q":"Auto-bump a version and release.","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on: workflow_dispatch\njobs:\n  release:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - run: npm version patch &amp;&amp; git push --follow-tags\n      - uses: actions/create-release@v1\n        env:\n          GITHUB_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0260","card":"q-github-actions-041-releases-and-publishing","sec":"github-actions-s042","q":"How do you publish a package to npm or PyPI?","a":"<p>Authenticate with a secret and run the publish command.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- run: npm publish\n  env:\n    NODE_AUTH_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.NPM_TOKEN }}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0261","card":"q-github-actions-041-releases-and-publishing","sec":"github-actions-s042","q":"Auto-release a Python package to PyPI on a tag push.","a":"<p>Trigger on tags, store the PyPI token as a secret, then build and publish with twine.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on:\n  push:\n    tags: [&#x27;v*&#x27;]\njobs:\n  publish:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - run: twine upload dist/*\n        env:\n          TWINE_PASSWORD: ${{ secrets.PYPI_TOKEN }}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0262","card":"q-github-actions-041-releases-and-publishing","sec":"github-actions-s042","q":"In GitHub Actions, a release workflow triggered by a tag push creates a GitHub release. What permission does the <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> need, and why?","a":"<p><code>contents: write</code>. Creating a release and pushing tags changes repository contents, and the default token in many repositories is read-only. Set it at the job level so other jobs keep the minimum.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0263","card":"q-github-actions-041-releases-and-publishing","sec":"github-actions-s042","q":"A workflow bumps the version and pushes a tag with the default <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code>, but the tag-triggered release workflow never starts. Why?","a":"<p>Events caused by the <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> do not start new workflow runs. This prevents recursive runs. The only exceptions are <code>workflow_dispatch</code> and <code>repository_dispatch</code>. To start the release workflow, push the tag with a personal access token or GitHub App token, or call the release workflow directly with <code>workflow_call</code> or <code>gh workflow run</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0264","card":"q-github-actions-041-releases-and-publishing","sec":"github-actions-s042","q":"What does <code>github.ref_name</code> hold when a workflow runs because of a tag push, and why use it for the release name?","a":"<p>It holds the short ref name, so for <code>refs/tags/v1.2.0</code> it is <code>v1.2.0</code>. Using it ties the release to the tag that triggered the run, so there is no manual version entry.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0265","card":"q-github-actions-041-releases-and-publishing","sec":"github-actions-s042","q":"Why is <code>on: push: tags: ['v*']</code> a good trigger for publishing a package, compared with publishing on every push to <code>main</code>?","a":"<p>A tag is an explicit decision to release a specific commit. Packages and releases are published only on purpose, versions are traceable to a commit, and the workflow can read the version from the tag. Publishing on every push would release unfinished work and could try to publish the same version twice.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0266","card":"q-github-actions-041-releases-and-publishing","sec":"github-actions-s042","q":"How should a GitHub Actions workflow authenticate to PyPI to publish a package?","a":"<p>The preferred way is trusted publishing: configure the PyPI project to trust the repository and workflow, give the job <code>id-token: write</code>, and use <code>pypa/gh-action-pypi-publish</code>. No API token is stored. The alternative is an API token in a secret, passed to <code>twine</code> as <code>TWINE_USERNAME=__token__</code> and <code>TWINE_PASSWORD</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0267","card":"q-github-actions-041-releases-and-publishing","sec":"github-actions-s042","q":"In the npm publish step, what is <code>NODE_AUTH_TOKEN</code> and how does it reach npm?","a":"<p>When <code>actions/setup-node</code> is used with <code>registry-url</code>, it writes an <code>.npmrc</code> that reads the token from the <code>NODE_AUTH_TOKEN</code> environment variable. npm then sends it as the auth token to the registry. Without <code>registry-url</code> in <code>setup-node</code>, the <code>.npmrc</code> entry does not exist and the publish is not authenticated.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- run: npm publish\n  env:\n    NODE_AUTH_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.NPM_TOKEN }}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0268","card":"q-github-actions-042-notifications","sec":"github-actions-s043","q":"Send Slack notifications.","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on: push\njobs:\n  notify:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - run: echo &quot;Running&quot;\n      - if: success() || failure()\n        uses: slackapi/slack-github-action@v1.26.0\n        with:\n          slack-bot-token: ${{ secrets.SLACK_BOT_TOKEN }}\n          channel-id: &#x27;my-channel&#x27;\n          text: &#x27;Workflow ${{ job.status }}!&#x27;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0269","card":"q-github-actions-042-notifications","sec":"github-actions-s043","q":"Send a Slack notification only on failure.","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps:\n  - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n  - run: exit 1\n  - if: failure()\n    uses: slackapi/slack-github-action@v1.23.0\n    with:\n      slack-bot-token: ${{ secrets.SLACK_BOT_TOKEN }}\n      channel-id: &#x27;general&#x27;\n      text: &#x27;Workflow failed!&#x27;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0270","card":"q-github-actions-042-notifications","sec":"github-actions-s043","q":"In GitHub Actions, what is the difference between <code>if: success() || failure()</code> and <code>if: always()</code> for a notification step?","a":"<p><code>always()</code> runs the step even when the workflow is cancelled. <code>success() || failure()</code> does not run on cancellation. Use <code>always()</code> only when you need the step to run for cleanup or when you want cancellations reported too.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0271","card":"q-github-actions-042-notifications","sec":"github-actions-s043","q":"A workflow has five jobs. How do you send one Slack message with the overall result?","a":"<p>Add a final <code>notify</code> job with <code>needs: [job1, job2, ...]</code> and <code>if: always()</code>. It reads each result from <code>needs.&lt;job&gt;.result</code> (<code>success</code>, <code>failure</code>, <code>cancelled</code> or <code>skipped</code>) and builds one message. Without <code>if: always()</code> it would be skipped as soon as a needed job fails.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0272","card":"q-github-actions-042-notifications","sec":"github-actions-s043","q":"In GitHub Actions, how does a Slack bot token differ from an incoming webhook for notifications, and where do you store either?","a":"<p>An incoming webhook posts to one channel and is simple. A bot token can post to any channel the bot is invited to, reply in threads and update messages, so it needs more scopes. Both are credentials, so store them as encrypted secrets and never in the workflow file.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0273","card":"q-github-actions-042-notifications","sec":"github-actions-s043","q":"Why do notification steps in pull request workflows from forks usually not work, and what should you do?","a":"<p>Fork pull requests do not receive repository secrets, so the Slack token is empty. Send the notification from a trusted workflow, for example one triggered by <code>workflow_run</code> or by a push to the main repository, and do not run untrusted code in it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0274","card":"q-github-actions-043-auto-formatting-and-external-repos","sec":"github-actions-s044","q":"Automate code formatting and push the changes.","a":"<p>Run the formatter, commit with an action, and guard with <code>if</code> to avoid a loop.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- uses: stefanzweifel/git-auto-commit-action@v4\n  with:\n    commit_message: &quot;Auto-format&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0275","card":"q-github-actions-043-auto-formatting-and-external-repos","sec":"github-actions-s044","q":"Clone a repo from another organization and run a script.","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps:\n  - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n    with:\n      repository: other-org/other-repo\n      token: ${{ secrets.PAT }}\n  - run: ./script.sh</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0276","card":"q-github-actions-043-auto-formatting-and-external-repos","sec":"github-actions-s044","q":"A workflow formats code and pushes a commit, which triggers the same workflow again. How do you prevent an endless loop?","a":"<p>Add a guard. For example, skip when the actor is the bot (<code>if: github.actor != 'github-actions[bot]'</code>), include <code>[skip ci]</code> in the commit message, or use <code>paths-ignore</code> so the workflow does not run for the changed files. Commits pushed with the default <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> do not retrigger workflows, but commits pushed with a personal access token do.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0277","card":"q-github-actions-043-auto-formatting-and-external-repos","sec":"github-actions-s044","q":"Why does a formatting workflow on <code>pull_request</code> need <code>ref: ${{ github.head_ref }}</code> in <code>actions/checkout</code> to push changes back?","a":"<p>For <code>pull_request</code> events, checkout by default gets a temporary merge commit in a detached HEAD state. You cannot push from it to the PR branch. Checking out the head branch name gives you a real branch to commit to.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0278","card":"q-github-actions-043-auto-formatting-and-external-repos","sec":"github-actions-s044","q":"Why can an auto-format workflow not push to a pull request opened from a fork?","a":"<p>The <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> on <code>pull_request</code> events from forks is read-only and cannot write to the fork's branch. Restrict auto-format to same-repository branches, or have the workflow only report the formatting problem and let the author fix it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0279","card":"q-github-actions-043-auto-formatting-and-external-repos","sec":"github-actions-s044","q":"Why does cloning a private repository from another organization in <code>actions/checkout</code> need a <code>token</code>, and what are safer options than a personal access token?","a":"<p>The default <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> only has access to the repository that runs the workflow. A token with access to the other repository is needed. A personal access token is tied to a person's account and may have wide scope. Safer options are a GitHub App installation token or a read-only deploy key limited to that one repository.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0280","card":"q-github-actions-043-auto-formatting-and-external-repos","sec":"github-actions-s044","q":"In <code>actions/checkout</code>, what does the <code>path</code> option do when you check out a second repository?","a":"<p>It places the checkout in a subfolder of the workspace. Without it, the second checkout writes into the workspace root, where it can overwrite or conflict with the first checkout.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0281","card":"q-github-actions-044-self-hosted-runner-and-debug-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s045","q":"How do you run a workflow on a self-hosted runner?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>runs-on: self-hosted</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0282","card":"q-github-actions-044-self-hosted-runner-and-debug-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s045","q":"How do you print debug output in a workflow?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Debug Workflow\non: [push]\njobs:\n  debug:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - run: echo &quot;Debugging enabled&quot; &amp;&amp; ls -la\n        env:\n          ACTIONS_STEP_DEBUG: true</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0283","card":"q-github-actions-044-self-hosted-runner-and-debug-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s045","q":"In GitHub Actions, how do you target a particular self-hosted runner, for example a Linux machine with a GPU?","a":"<p>Give the runner labels when you register it, then list all of them in <code>runs-on</code>: <code>runs-on: [self-hosted, linux, x64, gpu]</code>. The job runs only on a runner that has every label. <code>self-hosted</code> alone matches any self-hosted runner.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0284","card":"q-github-actions-044-self-hosted-runner-and-debug-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s045","q":"In this debug workflow, will setting <code>ACTIONS_STEP_DEBUG</code> in a step's <code>env</code> turn on step debug logging, and what is the documented way?","a":"<p>The documented way is to set <code>ACTIONS_STEP_DEBUG</code> to <code>true</code> as a repository secret or variable, or to re-run the workflow with \"Enable debug logging\". Debug logging is controlled by the run configuration, not by an environment variable set inside one step. <code>ACTIONS_RUNNER_DEBUG</code> does the same for runner diagnostics. With debug on, <code>::debug::</code> messages appear in the log.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- run: echo &quot;Debugging enabled&quot; &amp;&amp; ls -la\n  env:\n    ACTIONS_STEP_DEBUG: true</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0285","card":"q-github-actions-044-self-hosted-runner-and-debug-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s045","q":"Besides debug logging, what other techniques help you debug a failing GitHub Actions step?","a":"<p>Print context data with <code>toJSON(github)</code> or <code>env</code>, use <code>if: failure()</code> steps to dump logs or state, write details to <code>$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY</code>, group output with <code>::group::</code>, upload logs and reports as artifacts, re-run only the failed jobs, and open an SSH session to the runner with an action such as <code>mxschmitt/action-tmate</code> (only in trusted repositories, because it gives shell access).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0286","card":"q-github-actions-045-github-packages","sec":"github-actions-s046","q":"Why would a team use GitHub Packages instead of npmjs.com or Docker Hub?","a":"<p>Packages live next to the code, inherit repository and organization permissions, and publish with the workflow's <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code>, so no extra account or token is needed. This suits private internal packages. The downsides are that consumers need authentication even to install some packages, and public discovery is better on npmjs.com.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0287","card":"q-github-actions-045-github-packages","sec":"github-actions-s046","q":"In a GitHub Actions npm publish to GitHub Packages, what is the role of <code>registry-url</code> and the <code>.npmrc</code> entry?","a":"<p><code>registry-url</code> tells <code>setup-node</code> which registry to publish to and configures auth through <code>NODE_AUTH_TOKEN</code>. The repository <code>.npmrc</code> line <code>@owner:registry=https://npm.pkg.github.com</code> tells npm to resolve packages in that scope from GitHub Packages, so installs use the right registry.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- uses: actions/setup-node@v4\n  with:\n    node-version: &#x27;20&#x27;\n    registry-url: https://npm.pkg.github.com</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0288","card":"q-github-actions-045-github-packages","sec":"github-actions-s046","q":"Why must an npm package published to GitHub Packages have a scoped name such as <code>@owner/name</code>?","a":"<p>GitHub's npm registry only accepts scoped packages, and the scope must match the owner of the repository (user or organization). The scope is also what maps the package to the registry in <code>.npmrc</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0289","card":"q-github-actions-045-github-packages","sec":"github-actions-s046","q":"Which permission does a job need to publish a package to GitHub Packages with the <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code>?","a":"<p><code>packages: write</code>, plus <code>contents: read</code> if the job also checks out code. Set this in the job's <code>permissions</code> block. Without it, the publish step fails with a 403 or 401 error when the default token is read-only.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0290","card":"q-github-actions-046-deploy-to-github-pages","sec":"github-actions-s047","q":"Deploy a static site to GitHub Pages on a tag.","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Deploy Pages\non:\n  push:\n    tags: [&#x27;v*&#x27;]\njobs:\n  deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n      - run: npm install &amp;&amp; npm run build\n      - uses: peaceiris/actions-gh-pages@v3\n        with:\n          github_token: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}\n          publish_dir: ./dist</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0291","card":"q-github-actions-046-deploy-to-github-pages","sec":"github-actions-s047","q":"In GitHub Actions, how does deployment through <code>actions/upload-pages-artifact</code> and <code>actions/deploy-pages</code> differ from pushing to a <code>gh-pages</code> branch?","a":"<p>The artifact-based flow uploads the built site as a workflow artifact and deploys it directly with the Pages service. No built files are committed to a branch, the deployment appears in the Environments view, and the repository's Pages source must be set to \"GitHub Actions\". The older flow (for example <code>peaceiris/actions-gh-pages</code>) commits the build to a <code>gh-pages</code> branch that Pages serves.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0292","card":"q-github-actions-046-deploy-to-github-pages","sec":"github-actions-s047","q":"Why does <code>actions/deploy-pages</code> need <code>pages: write</code> and <code>id-token: write</code> permissions?","a":"<p><code>pages: write</code> lets the job create the Pages deployment. <code>id-token: write</code> lets it request an OIDC token, which the Pages service uses to verify that the deployment request comes from the correct repository and workflow.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0293","card":"q-github-actions-046-deploy-to-github-pages","sec":"github-actions-s047","q":"A single-page app deployed to GitHub Pages shows blank assets and 404 on refresh. What are the usual causes?","a":"<p>A project site is served under <code>/repository-name/</code>, so the build tool needs a matching base path (for example <code>base</code> in Vite), or asset URLs point at the domain root and break. For client-side routes, Pages returns 404 for unknown paths, so add a <code>404.html</code> that redirects to the app or use hash-based routing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0294","card":"q-github-actions-046-deploy-to-github-pages","sec":"github-actions-s047","q":"Why is the <code>environment: github-pages</code> block with a <code>url</code> useful in a Pages deployment job?","a":"<p>It records the deployment in the repository's Environments, so you can see the history and the live URL. The URL comes from the output of the deploy step (<code>steps.deployment.outputs.page_url</code>), and environment rules such as branch restrictions can apply.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0295","card":"q-github-actions-047-terraform-in-a-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s048","q":"In this GitHub Actions Terraform job, what is wrong with how the AWS credentials are provided?","a":"<p>The <code>env</code> block is attached only to the <code>init</code> step. The <code>plan</code> and <code>apply</code> steps run without credentials and fail when they call AWS. Set <code>env</code> at the job (or workflow) level, or authenticate once with <code>aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials</code>, preferably through OIDC.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- run: terraform init\n  env:\n    AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID: ${{ secrets.AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID }}\n    AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY: ${{ secrets.AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY }}\n- run: terraform plan -out=tfplan\n- run: terraform apply -auto-approve tfplan</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0296","card":"q-github-actions-047-terraform-in-a-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s048","q":"Why does a Terraform CI workflow usually run <code>plan</code> on pull requests and <code>apply</code> only after merge to <code>main</code>?","a":"<p>The plan shows reviewers exactly what would change before it is approved. Apply changes real infrastructure, so it should run only from the protected branch, and for production behind an environment with required reviewers. Add <code>concurrency</code> so two applies never run at once.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0297","card":"q-github-actions-047-terraform-in-a-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s048","q":"Why save the plan with <code>terraform plan -out=tfplan</code> and apply that file?","a":"<p>Applying the saved plan guarantees that the changes applied are the ones reviewed, even if the infrastructure or configuration changed in between. Applying a saved plan file also does not prompt for approval, so <code>-auto-approve</code> is not needed in that case.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0298","card":"q-github-actions-047-terraform-in-a-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s048","q":"Why must Terraform state be stored in a remote backend when it runs in GitHub Actions?","a":"<p>Runners are temporary, so a local state file is lost after the job and Terraform would try to recreate existing resources. A remote backend (such as S3 with encryption) keeps one shared state and, with locking, prevents two runs from changing it at once. State can also contain secrets, so the backend must be encrypted and access-controlled.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0299","card":"q-github-actions-047-terraform-in-a-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s048","q":"Which Terraform checks belong in the pull request stage of a CI workflow?","a":"<p><code>terraform fmt -check</code> for formatting, <code>terraform validate</code> for syntax and configuration errors, and <code>terraform plan</code> to preview changes. Many teams add a static analysis scanner for security misconfiguration. All of these run without applying anything.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0300","card":"q-github-actions-047-terraform-in-a-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s048","q":"What does <code>hashicorp/setup-terraform</code> do, and why pin <code>terraform_version</code>?","a":"<p>It installs the Terraform CLI on the runner and can configure credentials for HCP Terraform. Pinning the version avoids a newer CLI changing behavior or upgrading the state format unexpectedly, and it keeps CI aligned with what developers run locally.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0301","card":"q-github-actions-048-codeql-code-scanning","sec":"github-actions-s049","q":"What does CodeQL do, and how does it differ from Dependabot and secret scanning?","a":"<p>CodeQL is static analysis (SAST). It builds a database from your source and runs queries to find vulnerability patterns in your own code, such as SQL injection or XSS. Dependabot finds and updates vulnerable or outdated dependencies. Secret scanning finds committed credentials. They cover different risks and are normally used together.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0302","card":"q-github-actions-048-codeql-code-scanning","sec":"github-actions-s049","q":"In the CodeQL workflow, why does <code>github/codeql-action/init</code> come before <code>analyze</code>, and what sits between them for compiled languages?","a":"<p><code>init</code> sets up the CodeQL tools and starts creating the database, and for compiled languages it must be active during the build to observe it. <code>analyze</code> finishes the database, runs the queries and uploads results. For Java, C/C++, C# and Go, a build step (<code>autobuild</code> or a manual build) goes between them. For JavaScript and Python no build is needed.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- uses: github/codeql-action/init@v3\n  with:\n    languages: javascript\n- uses: github/codeql-action/analyze@v3</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0303","card":"q-github-actions-048-codeql-code-scanning","sec":"github-actions-s049","q":"Why does the CodeQL job set <code>permissions: security-events: write</code>?","a":"<p>The <code>analyze</code> step uploads results in SARIF format to the repository's code scanning alerts. That upload needs <code>security-events: write</code>. Without it the step fails when the token is read-only.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0304","card":"q-github-actions-048-codeql-code-scanning","sec":"github-actions-s049","q":"Where do CodeQL findings show up, and how does code scanning help in a pull request?","a":"<p>They appear in the repository's Security tab as code scanning alerts. For pull requests, new alerts are annotated on the changed lines and shown in the check results, so reviewers see problems before merge. A required code scanning check can block merging.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0305","card":"q-github-actions-048-codeql-code-scanning","sec":"github-actions-s049","q":"Why run CodeQL on a schedule as well as on push and pull request?","a":"<p>New queries and new vulnerability knowledge are added over time, so code that was clean last month may now match a rule. A weekly scheduled run finds these without any code change. It also scans the default branch regularly when a repository is quiet.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0306","card":"q-github-actions-048-codeql-code-scanning","sec":"github-actions-s049","q":"Is CodeQL code scanning free?","a":"<p>As of current GitHub plans, it is free for public repositories. For private repositories it requires the paid GitHub code security (formerly part of GitHub Advanced Security) licensing. Check the current plan details, because the packaging has changed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0307","card":"q-github-actions-049-dependabot","sec":"github-actions-s050","q":"What does Dependabot do, and what is the difference between version updates and security updates?","a":"<p>Dependabot opens pull requests to update dependencies. Version updates follow the schedule in <code>.github/dependabot.yml</code> and keep everything current. Security updates are opened when a vulnerability advisory affects a dependency, and they use the minimum fix version. Security updates are enabled in repository settings and do not need the config file.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0308","card":"q-github-actions-049-dependabot","sec":"github-actions-s050","q":"In <code>.github/dependabot.yml</code>, what do <code>package-ecosystem</code>, <code>directory</code> and <code>schedule.interval</code> control?","a":"<p><code>package-ecosystem</code> selects the package manager to check (npm, pip, maven, docker, github-actions and others). <code>directory</code> is the folder that holds the manifest, and a monorepo needs one entry per folder. <code>schedule.interval</code> sets how often Dependabot looks for updates (daily, weekly or monthly).</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>version: 2\nupdates:\n  - package-ecosystem: npm\n    directory: /\n    schedule:\n      interval: weekly</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0309","card":"q-github-actions-049-dependabot","sec":"github-actions-s050","q":"In GitHub Actions, why might a workflow triggered by a Dependabot pull request fail with missing secrets even though the same workflow works on normal PRs?","a":"<p>Workflows started by Dependabot on <code>pull_request</code> run with a read-only <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> and do not get the repository's Actions secrets. They can read secrets stored under \"Dependabot secrets\". Put the needed secrets there.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0310","card":"q-github-actions-049-dependabot","sec":"github-actions-s050","q":"How do you auto-merge Dependabot pull requests only when tests pass and only for small updates?","a":"<p>Enable auto-merge on the repository, require status checks in branch protection, and add a workflow that runs <code>dependabot/fetch-metadata</code>. If <code>update-type</code> is a patch or minor update, run <code>gh pr merge --auto --squash \"$PR_URL\"</code>. GitHub then merges only after required checks pass. Major updates stay manual.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0311","card":"q-github-actions-049-dependabot","sec":"github-actions-s050","q":"How can Dependabot keep GitHub Actions workflows themselves up to date?","a":"<p>Add an <code>updates</code> entry with <code>package-ecosystem: github-actions</code> and <code>directory: /</code>. Dependabot then opens pull requests that raise the versions of the actions used in <code>.github/workflows</code>, including pinned SHA versions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0312","card":"q-github-actions-049-dependabot","sec":"github-actions-s050","q":"How do you reduce pull request noise from Dependabot?","a":"<p>Use <code>groups</code> to combine related updates into one pull request, set a less frequent <code>schedule.interval</code>, and limit simultaneous PRs with <code>open-pull-requests-limit</code>. Auto-merge safe updates so they do not need attention.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0313","card":"q-github-actions-050-multi-language-projects","sec":"github-actions-s051","q":"A repository contains a Node.js frontend and a Python backend. Should CI run in one job or in separate jobs?","a":"<p>Prefer separate jobs, one per language. They run in parallel, each has its own setup, cache and failure status, and each can be a separate required check. One job is simpler for a small project but runs sequentially, and a failure in the first language hides the result of the second.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0314","card":"q-github-actions-050-multi-language-projects","sec":"github-actions-s051","q":"In this multi-language matrix, how many jobs run and what is the problem?","a":"<p>Four jobs run, one for every pair of Node and Python versions. Each job runs both test suites, so the Node tests run twice per Node version and the Python tests twice per Python version. Use two jobs, each with its own matrix, so each suite runs once per version.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>strategy:\n  matrix:\n    node-version: [&#x27;18&#x27;, &#x27;20&#x27;]\n    python-version: [&#x27;3.9&#x27;, &#x27;3.11&#x27;]\nsteps:\n  - uses: actions/setup-node@v4\n    with:\n      node-version: ${{ matrix.node-version }}\n  - run: npm test\n  - uses: actions/setup-python@v5\n    with:\n      python-version: ${{ matrix.python-version }}\n  - run: pytest</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0315","card":"q-github-actions-050-multi-language-projects","sec":"github-actions-s051","q":"How do you cache dependencies for both Node and Python in one GitHub Actions workflow?","a":"<p>Use <code>cache: npm</code> in <code>setup-node</code> and <code>cache: pip</code> in <code>setup-python</code>, which key the caches on the lockfile and requirements file. Each language gets its own cache entry. If you use <code>actions/cache</code> directly, give each language a separate key that includes the hash of its own dependency file.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0316","card":"q-github-actions-051-monorepos","sec":"github-actions-s052","q":"Monorepo: run workflows for specific directory changes.","a":"<p>Use <code>paths</code> and conditional jobs.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on:\n  push:\n    paths: [&#x27;services/frontend/**&#x27;]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0317","card":"q-github-actions-051-monorepos","sec":"github-actions-s052","q":"In a monorepo workflow, why use <code>working-directory</code> in a step instead of <code>cd packages/app &amp;&amp;</code>?","a":"<p><code>working-directory</code> is set per step (or for all steps with <code>defaults.run.working-directory</code>), applies cleanly to every <code>run</code> step, and keeps the command readable. A <code>cd</code> only affects the one command line it appears in and is easy to forget on the next step.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0318","card":"q-github-actions-051-monorepos","sec":"github-actions-s052","q":"Why is <code>npm install -g pnpm</code> a weak way to set up pnpm in a GitHub Actions workflow?","a":"<p>It downloads and installs pnpm on every run with no version control or caching. <code>pnpm/action-setup</code> installs a pinned version, and <code>setup-node</code> with <code>cache: pnpm</code> caches the pnpm store. Install pnpm before <code>setup-node</code> when using the cache option.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0319","card":"q-github-actions-051-monorepos","sec":"github-actions-s052","q":"A shared library in <code>packages/shared</code> changes, but the workflow filtered on <code>packages/app/**</code> does not run. How do you fix it?","a":"<p>The <code>paths</code> filter only matches changed files in the listed paths. Add the shared directories and the root files that affect the build, such as <code>packages/shared/**</code>, <code>pnpm-lock.yaml</code> and the workflow file itself, to the <code>paths</code> list. Build tools such as Nx or Turborepo can compute which packages are affected and run only those.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0320","card":"q-github-actions-051-monorepos","sec":"github-actions-s052","q":"What does <code>pnpm-workspace.yaml</code> do in a monorepo?","a":"<p>It lists the folders that belong to the workspace. pnpm then installs all their dependencies in one pass, links local packages to each other instead of downloading them, and shares one lockfile and one content-addressed store. Commands such as <code>pnpm --filter app test</code> target a single package.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>packages:\n  - &#x27;packages/app&#x27;\n  - &#x27;packages/api&#x27;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0321","card":"q-github-actions-051-monorepos","sec":"github-actions-s052","q":"What are the CI trade-offs between a monorepo and many small repositories?","a":"<p>A monorepo gives atomic changes across packages and one place for shared tooling, but CI must work out what changed to avoid running everything, and a single workflow set must scale. Multiple repositories keep pipelines small and independent, but cross-repo changes need coordinated releases and shared workflows are harder to keep consistent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0322","card":"q-github-actions-052-open-source-projects","sec":"github-actions-s053","q":"Which GitHub Actions workflows are commonly set up for an open-source repository besides tests?","a":"<p>Linting and formatting checks, CodeQL scanning, Dependabot for updates, <code>actions/labeler</code> to label pull requests by changed files, <code>actions/stale</code> to close inactive issues, a release workflow on version tags that publishes to the package registry, and a docs deployment to GitHub Pages.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0323","card":"q-github-actions-052-open-source-projects","sec":"github-actions-s053","q":"How do branch protection rules and <code>CODEOWNERS</code> work together in an open-source repository?","a":"<p>Branch protection requires status checks and reviews before merge. <code>CODEOWNERS</code> assigns reviewers by path, and with \"require review from code owners\" a change to those paths needs approval from the named owners. Together they stop merges that skip CI or that touch sensitive areas unreviewed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0324","card":"q-github-actions-053-debugging-a-failing-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s054","q":"How do you debug a failing GitHub Actions workflow?","a":"<p>Check the logs, add <code>echo</code> or debug steps, enable <code>ACTIONS_STEP_DEBUG</code>, re-run the workflow, or test locally with <code>act</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0325","card":"q-github-actions-053-debugging-a-failing-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s054","q":"A GitHub Actions job passes locally but fails in CI. How do you narrow down the cause?","a":"<p>Read the failing step's log from its first error, not only the last line. Compare the environment: OS, tool and runtime versions, environment variables, working directory and checkout depth. Turn on debug logging, print versions and environment details, and re-run. Re-create the environment locally with <code>act</code> or the same container image. Check for missing secrets, case-sensitive paths and timing or network differences.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0326","card":"q-github-actions-053-debugging-a-failing-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s054","q":"What does \"Re-run failed jobs\" do in GitHub Actions, and when is a full re-run better?","a":"<p>It runs only the jobs that failed (and the jobs that depend on them), reusing the successful results and artifacts from the earlier attempt. It saves time and minutes for flaky jobs. A full re-run is better when the failure might come from state shared across jobs, such as a bad cache or artifact.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0327","card":"q-github-actions-053-debugging-a-failing-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s054","q":"In GitHub Actions, how do you see all the values in the <code>github</code> context during a run?","a":"<p><code>toJSON</code> prints the whole context as JSON, so you can check <code>event_name</code>, <code>ref</code>, <code>sha</code>, <code>actor</code> and the event payload. Be careful that contexts printed this way can contain sensitive data, and never print <code>secrets</code>. A safer pattern passes the value through <code>env</code> and echoes the variable.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- run: echo &#x27;${{ toJSON(github) }}&#x27;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0328","card":"q-github-actions-054-testing-workflows-locally-with-act","sec":"github-actions-s055","q":"What is <code>act</code> and what problem does it solve in GitHub Actions development?","a":"<p><code>act</code> runs GitHub Actions workflows locally in Docker containers, using the workflow files in the repository. It gives a fast feedback loop for workflow changes without commit-push-wait cycles and without using Actions minutes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0329","card":"q-github-actions-054-testing-workflows-locally-with-act","sec":"github-actions-s055","q":"What does <code>-P ubuntu-latest=ghcr.io/catthehacker/ubuntu:act-latest</code> do when running <code>act</code>?","a":"<p><code>-P</code> maps a <code>runs-on</code> label to the Docker image that <code>act</code> uses for that label. The default images are small and miss many tools, so people choose a larger image that is closer to GitHub's hosted runner image. This reduces \"works on GitHub, fails in act\" differences.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>act -W .github/workflows/my-workflow.yml -P ubuntu-latest=ghcr.io/catthehacker/ubuntu:act-latest</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0330","card":"q-github-actions-054-testing-workflows-locally-with-act","sec":"github-actions-s055","q":"What are the limitations of <code>act</code> compared with real GitHub-hosted runners?","a":"<p>Runner images are not identical, so tools and versions differ. Secrets and the <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> are not available unless you provide them. Features that depend on GitHub's services behave differently or do not work, for example OIDC tokens, some caching, artifacts, environments and approvals, and a few contexts. Matrix and service containers work but may differ in detail. Treat <code>act</code> as a quick check, not as proof.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0331","card":"q-github-actions-054-testing-workflows-locally-with-act","sec":"github-actions-s055","q":"How should you supply secrets to <code>act</code>, and what must you avoid?","a":"<p>Pass them with <code>-s NAME=value</code>, or put them in a file and use <code>--secret-file</code>. Keep the file out of version control (add it to <code>.gitignore</code>), and use test or fake values instead of real production credentials.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0332","card":"q-github-actions-054-testing-workflows-locally-with-act","sec":"github-actions-s055","q":"How do you run only one job, or trigger a specific event with <code>act</code>?","a":"<p>Use <code>-j &lt;job-id&gt;</code> for one job, and give the event name as the argument, such as <code>act pull_request</code>, to simulate that event. <code>act -l</code> lists the jobs it finds, and <code>-n</code> does a dry run.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0333","card":"q-github-actions-055-self-hosted-runners","sec":"github-actions-s056","q":"What network access does a GitHub self-hosted runner need, and does it need open inbound ports?","a":"<p>It needs outbound HTTPS access to GitHub. The runner polls GitHub for jobs, so no inbound port needs to be open. This makes it possible to run it inside a private network, with access to internal resources, as long as outbound access to GitHub is allowed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0334","card":"q-github-actions-055-self-hosted-runners","sec":"github-actions-s056","q":"Why are ephemeral, one-job self-hosted runners recommended?","a":"<p>A persistent runner keeps its disk, caches, credentials and any processes left behind between jobs, so one job can affect or read the data of the next. An ephemeral runner takes one job and is then removed, so each job starts clean. Register with the <code>--ephemeral</code> option, or use just-in-time runners created through the API.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0335","card":"q-github-actions-055-self-hosted-runners","sec":"github-actions-s056","q":"How do you autoscale self-hosted runners for GitHub Actions on Kubernetes?","a":"<p>Use Actions Runner Controller (ARC), a Kubernetes operator that starts runner pods when jobs are queued and removes them afterwards. Runner scale sets scale on the number of queued jobs. Other options are cloud-specific autoscaling groups with a webhook or just-in-time runner registration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0336","card":"q-github-actions-055-self-hosted-runners","sec":"github-actions-s056","q":"How do you control which repositories can use an organization's self-hosted runners?","a":"<p>Put runners in runner groups at organization (or enterprise) level, and set which repositories and workflows may use each group. Use labels so jobs request the right runner type. Keep runners that can reach production in a group limited to trusted repositories.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0337","card":"q-github-actions-055-self-hosted-runners","sec":"github-actions-s056","q":"What are the main costs of self-hosted runners that are easy to overlook?","a":"<p>You maintain the OS, tools, runner updates and security patches. You handle scaling, monitoring and cleanup of disk space, and you carry the security risk of running workflow code on your own machines. GitHub does not support problems with the runner machine. The cost saving is real at high usage, but it is not free.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0338","card":"q-github-actions-056-long-running-workflows","sec":"github-actions-s057","q":"A GitHub-hosted job sets <code>timeout-minutes: 720</code>. Does it get 12 hours?","a":"<p>No. Under the current documented limits, a job on a GitHub-hosted runner is stopped after 6 hours no matter what <code>timeout-minutes</code> says, and a whole workflow run is limited to 35 days. A self-hosted runner allows longer jobs (documented as up to 5 days). Check the current usage limits, because these values can change.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>runs-on: ubuntu-latest\ntimeout-minutes: 720</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0339","card":"q-github-actions-056-long-running-workflows","sec":"github-actions-s057","q":"How would you handle a CI task that needs more than 6 hours in GitHub Actions?","a":"<p>Split it into smaller jobs, or shard it with a matrix so the pieces run in parallel. Save intermediate results as artifacts or cache so a later job can continue. Use self-hosted runners for the longest jobs. For very long processing, start the work in an external system (a batch service or cluster) and have the workflow poll or be notified of the result, so the runner is not held for hours.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0340","card":"q-github-actions-057-common-problems","sec":"github-actions-s058","q":"In GitHub Actions, which commit message text skips workflow runs, and for which events does it work?","a":"<p>Putting <code>[skip ci]</code>, <code>[ci skip]</code>, <code>[no ci]</code>, <code>[skip actions]</code> or <code>[actions skip]</code> in the commit message skips workflows triggered by <code>push</code> and <code>pull_request</code> events. It does not apply to other triggers such as <code>workflow_dispatch</code> or <code>schedule</code>. A workflow that is skipped like this shows no run at all, which is a common reason for \"my workflow did not run\".</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0341","card":"q-github-actions-057-common-problems","sec":"github-actions-s058","q":"A step fails with \"Resource not accessible by integration\". What does it mean and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>The <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> lacks a permission that the API call needs, for example <code>pull-requests: write</code> to comment or <code>contents: write</code> to push. Add the least permission needed in the job's <code>permissions</code> block. On pull requests from forks the token is read-only, so the call fails even with the permission set.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0342","card":"q-github-actions-057-common-problems","sec":"github-actions-s058","q":"What are the problems in this retry step?","a":"<p><code>curl</code> without <code>-f</code> (<code>--fail</code>) exits with 0 for HTTP 500 responses, so the loop stops after the first attempt. The loop does not fail after the last attempt unless you add an exit. Also <code>continue-on-error: true</code> hides a real failure and lets the job pass. Use <code>curl --fail --retry 3</code>, or a retry action, and let the step fail if all attempts fail.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Retry network call\n  continue-on-error: true\n  run: |\n    for i in {1..3}; do curl http://example.com &amp;&amp; break || sleep 5; done</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0343","card":"q-github-actions-057-common-problems","sec":"github-actions-s058","q":"A workflow is not picked up by any runner and the job waits with \"Waiting for a runner\". What are the common causes?","a":"<p>The <code>runs-on</code> label does not match any runner (a typo, or a label that no longer exists, such as a retired runner image). For self-hosted runners, the runner is offline or lacks one of the labels, or the runner group does not allow the repository. For hosted larger runners, the concurrency or spending limit may be reached.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0344","card":"q-github-actions-058-limitations-of-github-actions","sec":"github-actions-s059","q":"What are the limitations (execution time, storage, concurrency)?","a":"<p>A 6-hour job limit, 500 MB artifact storage, limited concurrency on the free tier, and fixed runner specs.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0345","card":"q-github-actions-058-limitations-of-github-actions","sec":"github-actions-s059","q":"What platform limits of GitHub Actions should you design around?","a":"<p>Under the current documentation: a job on a GitHub-hosted runner stops after 6 hours, a workflow run after 35 days, a matrix can create at most 256 jobs per workflow run, and the number of concurrent jobs depends on the plan. Storage for artifacts and packages depends on the plan, and the API has rate limits. Hosted runner sizes are fixed by the runner type. These values change, so check the current usage limits before relying on one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0346","card":"q-github-actions-058-limitations-of-github-actions","sec":"github-actions-s059","q":"How do you work around the fixed hardware of GitHub-hosted runners for a heavy build?","a":"<p>Use larger hosted runners (paid, with more CPU and memory) or self-hosted runners with the hardware you need. Also reduce the work with caching, parallel jobs and test sharding.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0347","card":"q-github-actions-059-workflow-not-triggering-on-a-pr","sec":"github-actions-s060","q":"A workflow is not triggering on a PR. How do you troubleshoot?","a":"<p>Check the <code>on</code> block, permissions, branch and path filters, the logs and GitHub status.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on:\n  pull_request:\n    branches: [main]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0348","card":"q-github-actions-059-workflow-not-triggering-on-a-pr","sec":"github-actions-s060","q":"A pull request has no workflow run at all. What do you check, in order?","a":"<p>First check that the workflow file is in <code>.github/workflows</code> with valid YAML (a syntax error stops it from running, and the Actions tab shows the error). Then check that <code>on:</code> includes <code>pull_request</code> and that its <code>branches</code> and <code>paths</code> filters match the PR's base branch and changed files. Check whether the PR has a merge conflict, whether Actions is enabled for the repository, whether the commit message has <code>[skip ci]</code>, and whether the run is waiting for approval because the author is a first-time contributor from a fork.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0349","card":"q-github-actions-059-workflow-not-triggering-on-a-pr","sec":"github-actions-s060","q":"In GitHub Actions, why does a workflow with <code>on: pull_request</code> not run when the pull request has a merge conflict?","a":"<p>The <code>pull_request</code> event runs against a temporary merge commit of the PR head and the base branch. If the merge conflicts, that commit cannot be created and the workflow does not run. Resolving the conflict triggers the run again. A <code>push</code> workflow would still run on the branch.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0350","card":"q-github-actions-059-workflow-not-triggering-on-a-pr","sec":"github-actions-s060","q":"Why does <code>on: pull_request: branches: [main]</code> use the base branch and not the PR's source branch?","a":"<p>For <code>pull_request</code> events, <code>branches</code> filters on the branch the PR targets (the base branch). This workflow runs for PRs opened into <code>main</code>, whatever the source branch is called. Source-branch filtering needs an <code>if</code> on <code>github.head_ref</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on:\n  pull_request:\n    branches: [main]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0351","card":"q-github-actions-060-enforcing-tests-before-merge","sec":"github-actions-s061","q":"Enforce passing tests for PRs and prevent bypassing.","a":"<p>Enable branch protection, require status checks and limit merge permissions.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on: pull_request\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - run: npm test</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0352","card":"q-github-actions-060-enforcing-tests-before-merge","sec":"github-actions-s061","q":"A production deployment fails because of an outdated branch. How do you prevent it?","a":"<p>Restrict triggers to <code>main</code>, use tags and add approvals.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0353","card":"q-github-actions-060-enforcing-tests-before-merge","sec":"github-actions-s061","q":"In GitHub, which settings together make failing tests block a merge, and how can an administrator still bypass them?","a":"<p>Turn on branch protection (or a ruleset) for the target branch, require status checks to pass, select the test job as a required check, and require pull requests. Admins can bypass unless you enable the \"Do not allow bypassing\" setting (or enforce rules for administrators). Also restrict who can push directly to the branch.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0354","card":"q-github-actions-060-enforcing-tests-before-merge","sec":"github-actions-s061","q":"How does a required status check in branch protection know which workflow job to wait for?","a":"<p>It matches by the check name, which is the job's name (or <code>name:</code> value, and for matrix jobs the name with the matrix values added). Renaming a job in the workflow changes the check name, so the old required name stays pending and blocks merges until you update the rule.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0355","card":"q-github-actions-060-enforcing-tests-before-merge","sec":"github-actions-s061","q":"What must a workflow do to work with GitHub's merge queue?","a":"<p>It must also trigger on the <code>merge_group</code> event (<code>on: merge_group</code>). The merge queue runs the required checks on a temporary branch that combines the PR with the queue, and if the workflow does not listen to that event, the required checks never report and the PR is stuck.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0356","card":"q-github-actions-060-enforcing-tests-before-merge","sec":"github-actions-s061","q":"Why use \"Require branches to be up to date before merging\" together with required checks?","a":"<p>Tests passed on a PR may no longer pass after another PR merges into the base branch, because the combined code was never tested. This setting forces the PR to include the latest base branch and rerun checks before merge. The cost is more reruns when many PRs are open. A merge queue gives the same safety with less manual rebasing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0357","card":"q-github-actions-061-service-containers","sec":"github-actions-s062","q":"The app needs a database for tests that the runners do not have. How do you set it up?","a":"<p>Use a service container such as PostgreSQL and configure the connection.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>services:\n  postgres:\n    image: postgres\n    env:\n      POSTGRES_DB: test</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0358","card":"q-github-actions-061-service-containers","sec":"github-actions-s062","q":"In GitHub Actions, a job runs directly on the runner VM and declares <code>services: postgres</code> with <code>ports: ['5432:5432']</code>. Which host and port does the test code connect to?","a":"<p><code>localhost:5432</code>. The <code>ports</code> mapping publishes the container port on the runner host. If the job itself runs inside a <code>container:</code>, the job and the service share a Docker network. Then you connect with the service label as the hostname (<code>postgres</code>) and the container port, and no port mapping is needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0359","card":"q-github-actions-061-service-containers","sec":"github-actions-s062","q":"In a GitHub Actions workflow, why add <code>options: --health-cmd pg_isready --health-interval 10s --health-timeout 5s --health-retries 5</code> to a PostgreSQL service container?","a":"<p>The job's steps wait until the service container reports healthy. Without a health check, the first test step can start while the database is still booting and fail with \"connection refused\". The <code>options</code> value is passed to <code>docker create</code>, so any Docker health flag works.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0360","card":"q-github-actions-061-service-containers","sec":"github-actions-s062","q":"Why does this GitHub Actions service container fail to start?","a":"<p>The official <code>postgres</code> image refuses to start without a superuser password. Add <code>POSTGRES_PASSWORD</code> (a throwaway value is fine for CI) to <code>env</code>. Also add <code>ports</code> if the job runs on the runner, and pin the image tag (<code>postgres:16</code>) so a new major version does not break the pipeline.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>services:\n  postgres:\n    image: postgres\n    env:\n      POSTGRES_DB: test</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0361","card":"q-github-actions-061-service-containers","sec":"github-actions-s062","q":"What are the trade-offs of GitHub Actions service containers versus installing a database with <code>apt-get</code> inside a step?","a":"<p>Service containers start a clean, isolated database per job, are destroyed with the job, can use any image or version, and support health checks. Installing by hand is slower and leaves you managing services and versions. Limits: service containers need a Linux runner, and each job gets its own copy, so data is not shared between jobs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0362","card":"q-github-actions-061-service-containers","sec":"github-actions-s062","q":"When would you replace a database service container with an in-memory fake or a mock in CI tests?","a":"<p>Use the real database service when tests depend on SQL behaviour, migrations, constraints or the database's dialect. Use SQLite in memory or mocks for fast unit tests that only need a data-access seam. A common split is mocks in unit tests on every push and a service container for integration tests.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0363","card":"q-github-actions-061-service-containers","sec":"github-actions-s062","q":"In GitHub Actions, how does test code reach a service container whose host port is random?","a":"<p>Map the port without a host value, for example <code>ports: ['5432']</code>, and read the assigned host port from <code>${{ job.services.postgres.ports['5432'] }}</code>. When the job itself runs in a container, use the service label as hostname and the container port.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0364","card":"q-github-actions-062-environment-problems","sec":"github-actions-s063","q":"A workflow fails because a tool version is missing. How do you handle it?","a":"<p>Install the tool, use a custom container or use a self-hosted runner.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>container: custom-image:1.0</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0365","card":"q-github-actions-062-environment-problems","sec":"github-actions-s063","q":"Test results are inconsistent across runners. How do you fix it?","a":"<p>Pin versions, use the same runner and control test randomness.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>container: node:16</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0366","card":"q-github-actions-062-environment-problems","sec":"github-actions-s063","q":"A legacy app needs a custom build environment. How do you address it?","a":"<p>Use a custom Docker image or a self-hosted runner.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>container: legacy-env:1.0</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0367","card":"q-github-actions-062-environment-problems","sec":"github-actions-s063","q":"A GitHub Actions step fails because the runner lacks a required tool version. What are your options, from least to most effort?","a":"<ol>\n  <li>Use a setup action (<code>actions/setup-node</code>, <code>actions/setup-java</code>, etc.) to install the version.</li>\n  <li>Install the tool in a step (for instance with a package manager or a downloaded binary).</li>\n  <li>Run the job in a <code>container:</code> image that already contains it.</li>\n  <li>Use a self-hosted runner with the tool baked in. Prefer the first options. The later ones add maintenance, and self-hosted runners add security and operations work.</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0368","card":"q-github-actions-062-environment-problems","sec":"github-actions-s063","q":"Tests pass on one GitHub Actions run and fail on another with the same commit. What are the usual environmental causes and fixes?","a":"<p>Typical causes: unpinned dependencies that resolved to new versions, a moving runner image (<code>ubuntu-latest</code> changes over time), tests that depend on order, shared state, time or randomness, and network calls to external services. Fixes: pin dependencies with a lock file, pin the runner image or use a container image by tag or digest, seed random generators, isolate state per test, and mock or stub external calls.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0369","card":"q-github-actions-062-environment-problems","sec":"github-actions-s063","q":"A legacy application needs an old OS or toolchain to build. How do you run that build in GitHub Actions, and what are the trade-offs?","a":"<p>Build a Docker image with the legacy toolchain, push it to a registry, and run the job with <code>container: &lt;image&gt;</code>. It is reproducible and versioned in code, but the image must be maintained and pulled on every run (use layer caching or a smaller image). A self-hosted runner is the alternative, but it carries patching and security cost and state can leak between jobs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0370","card":"q-github-actions-062-environment-problems","sec":"github-actions-s063","q":"In GitHub Actions, you set <code>container: registry.example.com/team/build:1.0</code> and the pull fails with an authentication error. What is missing?","a":"<p>The job's <code>container</code> block takes a <code>credentials</code> map (<code>username</code> and <code>password</code>) for private registries. Feed it from secrets. Public images need no credentials.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0371","card":"q-github-actions-063-reporting-deployments","sec":"github-actions-s064","q":"Report successful deployments for the past month. How do you extract them?","a":"<p>Use the GitHub API, filter successes and automate the report.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- run: curl -H &quot;Authorization: Bearer ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}&quot; https://api.github.com/repos/${{ github.repository }}/deployments</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0372","card":"q-github-actions-063-reporting-deployments","sec":"github-actions-s064","q":"This step calls the GitHub REST API from a workflow. Does the response tell you which deployments succeeded?","a":"<p>No. The list returns deployment records (ref, environment, creator, time) but not the outcome. Outcome lives in the deployment statuses: <code>GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/deployments/{deployment_id}/statuses</code> returns states such as <code>success</code>, <code>failure</code> and <code>in_progress</code>. For a monthly report, list deployments, fetch the latest status of each, filter on <code>success</code> and the date range.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- run: curl -H &quot;Authorization: Bearer ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}&quot; https://api.github.com/repos/${{ github.repository }}/deployments</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0373","card":"q-github-actions-063-reporting-deployments","sec":"github-actions-s064","q":"In GitHub Actions, how do deployment records get created so they appear in the repository's Deployments view?","a":"<p>Setting <code>environment:</code> on a job creates a deployment and updates its status as the job runs and finishes, with no extra code. Without <code>environment:</code>, you can create deployments and statuses yourself through the Deployments API.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0374","card":"q-github-actions-063-reporting-deployments","sec":"github-actions-s064","q":"How can deployment history from GitHub Actions feed delivery metrics such as deployment frequency?","a":"<p>Query the Deployments API per environment (production), count successful statuses per time window, and chart the counts. Deployment frequency is one of the four DORA metrics. Lead time can be computed by comparing commit time with the success status time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0375","card":"q-github-actions-064-migrating-from-jenkins","sec":"github-actions-s065","q":"What are the challenges of moving from Jenkins to GitHub Actions?","a":"<p>Learning YAML, setting up runners, migrating plugins and cost for private repos.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0376","card":"q-github-actions-064-migrating-from-jenkins","sec":"github-actions-s065","q":"What tool helps automate a Jenkins to GitHub Actions migration, and what does it do?","a":"<p>GitHub Actions Importer, used through the <code>gh actions-importer</code> CLI extension. It audits your existing pipelines to estimate how much converts automatically, can dry-run a conversion to show the resulting workflow YAML, and can open a pull request with the migrated workflow. Plugins and custom steps with no equivalent still need manual work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0377","card":"q-github-actions-064-migrating-from-jenkins","sec":"github-actions-s065","q":"What strategy limits risk when moving many pipelines from Jenkins to GitHub Actions?","a":"<p>Migrate incrementally. Start with a low-risk service, run both pipelines in parallel and compare results, then switch the required checks and deployments over and retire the Jenkins job. Inventory plugins and credentials first so surprises show up early.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0378","card":"q-github-actions-065-how-python-fits-in","sec":"github-actions-s066","q":"How does Python integrate with GitHub Actions?","a":"<p>Python scripts can trigger workflows through the GitHub API or run as steps inside workflows to automate CI/CD tasks.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0379","card":"q-github-actions-065-how-python-fits-in","sec":"github-actions-s066","q":"How can GitHub Actions be orchestrated using Python scripts?","a":"<p>Run Python scripts as workflow steps for tests or deployment, use <code>PyGithub</code> to trigger workflows, manage repos or update issues, and use repository dispatch events to trigger workflows programmatically.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from github import Github\ng = Github(&quot;&lt;token&gt;&quot;)\nrepo = g.get_repo(&quot;owner/repo&quot;)\nrepo.create_repository_dispatch(event_type=&quot;trigger-workflow&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0380","card":"q-github-actions-065-how-python-fits-in","sec":"github-actions-s066","q":"How can Python automate or trigger workflows programmatically?","a":"<p>The same three approaches: run scripts in workflows, call the GitHub API with <code>PyGithub</code> (trigger workflows, manage PRs, update issues), and send repository dispatch events. The example above with <code>event_type=\"custom-event\"</code> works the same way.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0381","card":"q-github-actions-065-how-python-fits-in","sec":"github-actions-s066","q":"How do you ensure idempotency across these tools?","a":"<p>For GitHub Actions, use conditional steps to avoid redundant actions. For the other tools: Terraform uses state files, Ansible uses <code>state</code> parameters, boto3 checks existence before creating (for example <code>head_bucket</code>) and Kubernetes uses <code>apply</code> semantics.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0382","card":"q-github-actions-065-how-python-fits-in","sec":"github-actions-s066","q":"What are the best practices for structuring Python scripts that manage multiple tools, GitHub Actions included?","a":"<p>Modular design (a module per tool), config files, robust exception handling for API failures, the <code>logging</code> module, Git versioning and unit tests for critical functions.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0383","card":"q-github-actions-065-how-python-fits-in","sec":"github-actions-s066","q":"In what two ways does Python interact with GitHub Actions?","a":"<p>Python can run inside a workflow, as a step that tests, builds, deploys or manages resources. Python can also sit outside, calling the GitHub API (directly with <code>requests</code> or with <code>PyGithub</code>) to trigger workflows, read run status, manage issues, pull requests and secrets. The first is automation within a pipeline, the second is automation of the pipeline itself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0384","card":"q-github-actions-065-how-python-fits-in","sec":"github-actions-s066","q":"In this PyGithub snippet, what does the call do, and what must the target repository's workflow contain to react to it?","a":"<p>It sends a <code>repository_dispatch</code> event with the custom type <code>trigger-workflow</code>. A workflow starts only if it listens for it:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on:\n  repository_dispatch:\n    types: [trigger-workflow]</code></pre></div>\n<p>Runs from <code>repository_dispatch</code> use the default branch's workflow file. The token needs write access to the repository (<code>repo</code> scope for a classic token, or Contents write for a fine-grained one).</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from github import Github\ng = Github(&quot;&lt;token&gt;&quot;)\nrepo = g.get_repo(&quot;owner/repo&quot;)\nrepo.create_repository_dispatch(event_type=&quot;trigger-workflow&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0385","card":"q-github-actions-065-how-python-fits-in","sec":"github-actions-s066","q":"In Python, when would you call the GitHub REST API with <code>requests</code> rather than use <code>PyGithub</code>?","a":"<p><code>PyGithub</code> gives typed objects, pagination and fewer lines of code for common operations. <code>requests</code> has one fewer dependency and can call any endpoint immediately, including new ones the library has not wrapped yet. For a small script with one or two endpoints, <code>requests</code> is fine. For a larger tool that lists, creates and updates many resources, use a library.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0386","card":"q-github-actions-065-how-python-fits-in","sec":"github-actions-s066","q":"In PyGithub 1.59 and later, what is the recommended way to authenticate instead of <code>Github(\"&lt;token&gt;\")</code>?","a":"<p>Pass an auth object: <code>Github(auth=Auth.Token(token))</code>, with <code>from github import Auth</code>. Passing the token positionally (<code>login_or_token</code>) is deprecated. The <code>Auth</code> classes also support app installation auth, which suits automation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0387","card":"q-github-actions-065-how-python-fits-in","sec":"github-actions-s066","q":"A Python script that creates AWS resources runs on every push in GitHub Actions. How do you make it idempotent?","a":"<p>Check state before creating (describe or <code>head_bucket</code> calls), or use calls that are naturally idempotent (upsert operations, <code>exist_ok=True</code>). Catch the \"already exists\" error and treat it as success. Better still, use a declarative tool such as Terraform, which keeps state and computes the difference. Idempotency matters because workflows get re-run and retried.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0388","card":"q-github-actions-065-how-python-fits-in","sec":"github-actions-s066","q":"Why use the <code>logging</code> module and not <code>print</code> in Python scripts that run in CI?","a":"<p><code>logging</code> gives levels (DEBUG for troubleshooting, ERROR for failures), timestamps and one place to configure output, so you can turn up verbosity when a run fails. It also lets shared modules log without deciding where output goes. Do not log tokens or full request headers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0389","card":"q-github-actions-066-triggering-a-workflow-through-the-api","sec":"github-actions-s067","q":"How do you trigger a workflow programmatically via the GitHub API?","a":"<p>Use the \"Create a workflow dispatch event\" endpoint, <code>POST /repos/{owner}/{repo}/actions/workflows/{workflow_id}/dispatches</code>, with <code>requests</code> and a PAT. A 204 response means success.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import requests\ndef trigger_workflow(owner, repo, workflow_id, ref, token, inputs=None):\n    url = f&quot;https://api.github.com/repos/{owner}/{repo}/actions/workflows/{workflow_id}/dispatches&quot;\n    headers = {\n        &quot;Authorization&quot;: f&quot;Bearer {token}&quot;,\n        &quot;Accept&quot;: &quot;application/vnd.github.v3+json&quot;\n    }\n    data = {\n        &quot;ref&quot;: ref,  # Branch or tag to run the workflow on\n        &quot;inputs&quot;: inputs or {}\n    }\n    response = requests.post(url, json=data, headers=headers)\n    if response.status_code == 204:\n        print(&quot;Workflow triggered successfully!&quot;)\n    else:\n        print(f&quot;Failed to trigger workflow: {response.json()}&quot;)\n# Example usage\ntrigger_workflow(\n    owner=&quot;my-org&quot;,\n    repo=&quot;my-repo&quot;,\n    workflow_id=&quot;deploy.yml&quot;,\n    ref=&quot;main&quot;,\n    token=os.environ[&quot;GITHUB_TOKEN&quot;]\n)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0390","card":"q-github-actions-066-triggering-a-workflow-through-the-api","sec":"github-actions-s067","q":"In the GitHub REST API, what does <code>POST /repos/{owner}/{repo}/actions/workflows/{workflow_id}/dispatches</code> require on both sides to work?","a":"<p>The workflow must declare <code>on: workflow_dispatch</code>, otherwise the API returns an error. The request body has <code>ref</code> (branch or tag to run) and optional <code>inputs</code>. The caller needs a token with permission to write Actions: <code>actions: write</code> for a fine-grained token or <code>repo</code> scope for a classic one. Success is HTTP 204 with an empty body. <code>workflow_id</code> can be the file name (<code>deploy.yml</code>) or the numeric ID.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0391","card":"q-github-actions-066-triggering-a-workflow-through-the-api","sec":"github-actions-s067","q":"In a <code>workflow_dispatch</code> API call, what happens if the JSON <code>inputs</code> contains a key that the workflow did not declare?","a":"<p>The API rejects the request with a validation error (HTTP 422). Inputs must be declared under <code>on.workflow_dispatch.inputs</code>, and all values are sent as strings, even booleans and numbers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0392","card":"q-github-actions-066-triggering-a-workflow-through-the-api","sec":"github-actions-s067","q":"In GitHub Actions, how do <code>workflow_dispatch</code> and <code>repository_dispatch</code> differ when triggered from the API?","a":"<p><code>workflow_dispatch</code> targets one named workflow file on a chosen <code>ref</code>, accepts typed inputs, and the file on that ref is used. <code>repository_dispatch</code> is a repository-wide custom event with an <code>event_type</code> and a free-form <code>client_payload</code>. Every workflow that listens for that type starts, always from the default branch. Use <code>workflow_dispatch</code> to run one pipeline on demand, and <code>repository_dispatch</code> to let an external system announce an event.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0393","card":"q-github-actions-066-triggering-a-workflow-through-the-api","sec":"github-actions-s067","q":"A workflow pushes a commit using <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code>, and the push does not start other workflows. Do <code>workflow_dispatch</code> and <code>repository_dispatch</code> calls made with <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> behave the same way?","a":"<p>No. Events created by <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> do not start new workflow runs, to prevent loops, with two exceptions: <code>workflow_dispatch</code> and <code>repository_dispatch</code>. So one workflow can chain into another by dispatching it. For <code>workflow_dispatch</code> the token needs <code>actions: write</code> in the calling workflow's <code>permissions</code>; the <code>repository_dispatch</code> endpoint needs <code>contents: write</code>. Watch for infinite loops yourself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0394","card":"q-github-actions-067-checking-run-status-and-listing-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s068","q":"How do you check the status of a workflow run with <code>PyGithub</code>?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from github import Github\ndef check_workflow_status(owner, repo, run_id, token):\n    g = Github(token)\n    repo = g.get_repo(f&quot;{owner}/{repo}&quot;)\n    workflow_run = repo.get_workflow_run(run_id)\n    status = workflow_run.status\n    conclusion = workflow_run.conclusion\n    print(f&quot;Workflow Run ID: {run_id}&quot;)\n    print(f&quot;Status: {status}&quot;)\n    print(f&quot;Conclusion: {conclusion}&quot;)\n    return status, conclusion</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0395","card":"q-github-actions-067-checking-run-status-and-listing-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s068","q":"How do you list all workflows in a repository?","a":"<p>Use <code>GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/actions/workflows</code> or <code>PyGithub</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from github import Github\ndef list_workflows(owner, repo, token):\n    g = Github(token)\n    repo = g.get_repo(f&quot;{owner}/{repo}&quot;)\n    workflows = repo.get_workflows()\n    for workflow in workflows:\n        print(f&quot;Workflow: {workflow.name}, ID: {workflow.id}, Path: {workflow.path}&quot;)\n    return workflows</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0396","card":"q-github-actions-067-checking-run-status-and-listing-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s068","q":"In the GitHub API, what is the difference between a workflow run's <code>status</code> and <code>conclusion</code>?","a":"<p><code>status</code> is where the run is in its lifecycle: <code>queued</code>, <code>in_progress</code> or <code>completed</code>. <code>conclusion</code> is the result and is only set once the run is <code>completed</code>: <code>success</code>, <code>failure</code>, <code>cancelled</code>, <code>skipped</code>, <code>timed_out</code> and others. Before completion <code>conclusion</code> is <code>None</code>. A script that checks only <code>status == \"completed\"</code> cannot tell pass from fail.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0397","card":"q-github-actions-067-checking-run-status-and-listing-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s068","q":"How would you make a Python script wait for a GitHub Actions run to finish and then fail if the run failed?","a":"<p>Poll <code>get_workflow_run</code> until <code>status == \"completed\"</code>, then read <code>conclusion</code>. Use a sleep interval and an overall timeout so the script cannot hang, and watch the rate limit (a longer interval uses fewer calls). The caller should exit non-zero when the returned conclusion is not <code>\"success\"</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import time\nfrom github import Github\n\ndef wait_for_run(repo_name, run_id, token, timeout=1800, interval=15):\n    repo = Github(token).get_repo(repo_name)\n    deadline = time.time() + timeout\n    while time.time() &lt; deadline:\n        run = repo.get_workflow_run(run_id)\n        if run.status == &quot;completed&quot;:\n            return run.conclusion\n        time.sleep(interval)\n    raise TimeoutError(&quot;run did not finish in time&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0398","card":"q-github-actions-067-checking-run-status-and-listing-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s068","q":"When listing workflows or runs through the GitHub REST API, why can results seem incomplete?","a":"<p>List endpoints are paginated. The default page size is 30 and the maximum <code>per_page</code> is 100. In <code>PyGithub</code>, <code>get_workflows()</code> returns a lazy <code>PaginatedList</code> that fetches the next page as you iterate. With raw <code>requests</code>, follow the <code>Link</code> header or loop over <code>page</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0399","card":"q-github-actions-067-checking-run-status-and-listing-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s068","q":"Besides <code>POST</code> to the dispatch endpoint, how can PyGithub start a workflow from a workflow object returned by <code>repo.get_workflows()</code>?","a":"<p>Call <code>workflow.create_dispatch(ref, inputs)</code> on a <code>Workflow</code> object (or <code>repo.get_workflow(\"deploy.yml\")</code> to fetch it by name). It sends the same <code>workflow_dispatch</code> request and returns whether it was accepted.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0400","card":"q-github-actions-068-running-a-python-script-in-a-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s069","q":"How do you configure a workflow to run a Python script for resource management?","a":"<p>Set up Python, install dependencies, and run the script with secrets passed as environment variables.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Run Python Script\non: [push]\njobs:\n  manage-resources:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n    - name: Set up Python\n      uses: actions/setup-python@v4\n      with:\n        python-version: &#x27;3.10&#x27;\n    - name: Install dependencies\n      run: pip install -r requirements.txt\n    - name: Run Python script\n      env:\n        AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID: ${{ secrets.AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID }}\n        AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY: ${{ secrets.AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY }}\n      run: python manage_resources.py</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0401","card":"q-github-actions-068-running-a-python-script-in-a-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s069","q":"How do you integrate GitHub Actions with AWS or Kubernetes using Python?","a":"<p>Use <code>boto3</code> for AWS (S3, EC2, EKS), the <code>kubernetes</code> client for clusters, pass credentials as secrets, and add retry logic and logging.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Manage AWS and Kubernetes\non: [push]\njobs:\n  manage:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n    - name: Set up Python\n      uses: actions/setup-python@v4\n      with:\n        python-version: &#x27;3.10&#x27;\n    - name: Install dependencies\n      run: pip install boto3 kubernetes\n    - name: Run script\n      env:\n        AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID: ${{ secrets.AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID }}\n        AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY: ${{ secrets.AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY }}\n        KUBE_CONFIG: ${{ secrets.KUBE_CONFIG }}\n      run: python manage_resources.py</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0402","card":"q-github-actions-068-running-a-python-script-in-a-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s069","q":"In GitHub Actions, how does <code>boto3</code> pick up <code>AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID</code> and <code>AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY</code> that a step sets under <code>env</code>?","a":"<p><code>boto3</code> reads credentials through its default chain: explicit arguments, then environment variables, then shared config files, then container or instance role credentials. Because the step exports the two variables, <code>boto3.client(\"s3\")</code> works with no credentials in code. For a better setup, replace long-lived keys with an OIDC role, which also sets these environment variables for the step.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0403","card":"q-github-actions-068-running-a-python-script-in-a-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s069","q":"Why does a Python script in a GitHub Actions step still show a green step when it prints an error and carries on?","a":"<p>A step fails only when its process exits with a non-zero status. If the script catches an exception and only logs it, the exit code is 0. Let the exception propagate, or call <code>sys.exit(1)</code> (or <code>raise SystemExit(1)</code>) in the failure path.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0404","card":"q-github-actions-068-running-a-python-script-in-a-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s069","q":"Why use <code>actions/setup-python</code> with <code>cache: pip</code> for a Python workflow?","a":"<p><code>setup-python</code> selects an exact Python version and puts it on the <code>PATH</code>, so the job does not depend on the runner's preinstalled version. <code>cache: pip</code> restores the pip download cache keyed on the requirements file hash, so installs are faster and results are the same across runs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0405","card":"q-github-actions-068-running-a-python-script-in-a-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s069","q":"A Python step in GitHub Actions receives a kubeconfig through <code>KUBE_CONFIG: ${{ secrets.KUBE_CONFIG }}</code>. Why does <code>config.load_kube_config()</code> from the <code>kubernetes</code> client fail with that value?","a":"<p><code>load_kube_config</code> expects a path to a kubeconfig file, but the secret's value is the file's content. Either write the content to a file (for instance under <code>$RUNNER_TEMP</code>) and set that path, or parse the YAML and call <code>config.load_kube_config_from_dict(...)</code>. Where possible prefer short-lived cluster credentials, such as cloud OIDC, over a stored kubeconfig.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0406","card":"q-github-actions-068-running-a-python-script-in-a-workflow","sec":"github-actions-s069","q":"Why should a Python script in a workflow read secrets from environment variables and not from command-line arguments?","a":"<p>Command-line arguments are visible in process listings and often echoed in logs or shell tracing. Environment variables scoped to the one step expose the value only to that process. Prefer step-level <code>env:</code> over job-level or workflow-level <code>env:</code> to limit the exposure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0407","card":"q-github-actions-069-generating-workflow-files-with-python","sec":"github-actions-s070","q":"How do you create a workflow file dynamically with Python?","a":"<p>Write YAML into <code>.github/workflows</code>, using the <code>yaml</code> library for correct formatting.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import yaml\nimport os\ndef create_workflow_file(filename, workflow_name, trigger, steps):\n    workflow = {\n        &quot;name&quot;: workflow_name,\n        &quot;on&quot;: trigger,\n        &quot;jobs&quot;: {\n            &quot;build&quot;: {\n                &quot;runs-on&quot;: &quot;ubuntu-latest&quot;,\n                &quot;steps&quot;: steps\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    os.makedirs(&quot;.github/workflows&quot;, exist_ok=True)\n    with open(f&quot;.github/workflows/{filename}&quot;, &quot;w&quot;) as f:\n        yaml.dump(workflow, f, default_flow_style=False)\n    print(f&quot;Workflow file {filename} created!&quot;)\n# Example usage\nsteps = [\n    {&quot;uses&quot;: &quot;actions/checkout@v3&quot;},\n    {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;Set up Python&quot;, &quot;uses&quot;: &quot;actions/setup-python@v4&quot;, &quot;with&quot;: {&quot;python-version&quot;: &quot;3.10&quot;}},\n    {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;Run script&quot;, &quot;run&quot;: &quot;python script.py&quot;}\n]\ncreate_workflow_file(&quot;dynamic.yml&quot;, &quot;Dynamic Workflow&quot;, [&quot;push&quot;], steps)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0408","card":"q-github-actions-069-generating-workflow-files-with-python","sec":"github-actions-s070","q":"Scenario: automate creating workflows for multiple repositories.","a":"<p>Use <code>PyGithub</code> to create the workflow file in each repo, generating YAML from a template.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from github import Github\nimport yaml\ndef create_workflow_in_repo(owner, repo_name, token, workflow_name, steps):\n    g = Github(token)\n    repo = g.get_repo(f&quot;{owner}/{repo_name}&quot;)\n    workflow = {\n        &quot;name&quot;: workflow_name,\n        &quot;on&quot;: [&quot;push&quot;],\n        &quot;jobs&quot;: {\n            &quot;build&quot;: {\n                &quot;runs-on&quot;: &quot;ubuntu-latest&quot;,\n                &quot;steps&quot;: steps\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    workflow_content = yaml.dump(workflow, default_flow_style=False)\n    repo.create_file(\n        path=f&quot;.github/workflows/{workflow_name.lower()}.yml&quot;,\n        message=f&quot;Add {workflow_name} workflow&quot;,\n        content=workflow_content\n    )\n    print(f&quot;Workflow created in {repo_name}&quot;)\n# Example usage\nsteps = [\n    {&quot;uses&quot;: &quot;actions/checkout@v3&quot;},\n    {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;Run script&quot;, &quot;run&quot;: &quot;python script.py&quot;}\n]\ncreate_workflow_in_repo(&quot;my-org&quot;, &quot;my-repo&quot;, token, &quot;CI&quot;, steps)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0409","card":"q-github-actions-069-generating-workflow-files-with-python","sec":"github-actions-s070","q":"You parse a workflow with PyYAML (<code>yaml.safe_load</code>) and read <code>data[\"on\"]</code>, but you get a <code>KeyError</code>. Why?","a":"<p>PyYAML follows YAML 1.1, where an unquoted <code>on</code> is a boolean, so the key is loaded as <code>True</code>. You would read <code>data[True]</code>. Options: quote the key in the file, use a YAML 1.2 parser such as <code>ruamel.yaml</code>, or handle both keys. When dumping, PyYAML writes the string <code>on</code> quoted (<code>'on':</code>), and GitHub accepts that.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0410","card":"q-github-actions-069-generating-workflow-files-with-python","sec":"github-actions-s070","q":"In <code>yaml.dump(workflow, f, default_flow_style=False)</code>, what does <code>default_flow_style=False</code> do?","a":"<p>It makes PyYAML write nested mappings and lists in block style (one item per line) and not in inline <code>{}</code> or <code>[]</code> style, which is how workflow files are normally written. In PyYAML 5.1 and later the default is already <code>False</code> for most data, so passing it matters mainly for older versions and for being explicit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0411","card":"q-github-actions-069-generating-workflow-files-with-python","sec":"github-actions-s070","q":"In PyGithub, a script calls <code>repo.create_file(path=\".github/workflows/ci.yml\", ...)</code> on a repository that already has that file. What happens, and what do you use instead?","a":"<p><code>create_file</code> fails with HTTP 422 because the file exists. Use <code>repo.get_contents(path)</code> to obtain the file's <code>sha</code>, then <code>repo.update_file(path, message, content, sha)</code>. This get-then-update flow also guards against overwriting a change someone else made in between.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0412","card":"q-github-actions-069-generating-workflow-files-with-python","sec":"github-actions-s070","q":"A Python step in a workflow pushes a change to a file under <code>.github/workflows</code> using <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> and the push is rejected. Why?","a":"<p><code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> cannot create or modify workflow files, because that would let a workflow rewrite its own permissions. Use a personal access token with <code>workflow</code> scope (fine-grained: Workflows read and write) or a GitHub App with the Workflows permission, stored as a secret.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0413","card":"q-github-actions-069-generating-workflow-files-with-python","sec":"github-actions-s070","q":"When is generating workflow YAML with a Python script a bad idea compared with reusable workflows?","a":"<p>Copies written by the script drift apart: each repository holds its own file, and fixing the template means regenerating and pushing to every repository again. Reusable workflows and composite actions keep the logic in one place, and callers pick up changes by version. Generation fits when files must differ structurally per repository, or for the one-time bootstrap of many repositories.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0414","card":"q-github-actions-069-generating-workflow-files-with-python","sec":"github-actions-s070","q":"In a Python function that writes <code>.github/workflows/&lt;name&gt;.yml</code>, why call <code>os.makedirs(\".github/workflows\", exist_ok=True)</code> before opening the file?","a":"<p><code>open(..., \"w\")</code> fails with <code>FileNotFoundError</code> if the directory does not exist. <code>makedirs</code> creates the whole path, and <code>exist_ok=True</code> stops it raising when the directory is already there, so the function can run repeatedly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0415","card":"q-github-actions-070-logs-and-secrets-through-the-api","sec":"github-actions-s071","q":"How do you download workflow logs with the GitHub API?","a":"<p>Use <code>GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/actions/runs/{run_id}/logs</code>, which returns a ZIP file.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import requests\nimport zipfile\nimport io\ndef download_workflow_logs(owner, repo, run_id, token):\n    url = f&quot;https://api.github.com/repos/{owner}/{repo}/actions/runs/{run_id}/logs&quot;\n    headers = {\n        &quot;Authorization&quot;: f&quot;Bearer {token}&quot;,\n        &quot;Accept&quot;: &quot;application/vnd.github.v3+json&quot;\n    }\n    response = requests.get(url, headers=headers, stream=True)\n    if response.status_code == 200:\n        with zipfile.ZipFile(io.BytesIO(response.content)) as z:\n            z.extractall(&quot;workflow_logs&quot;)\n        print(&quot;Logs downloaded and extracted!&quot;)\n    else:\n        print(f&quot;Failed to download logs: {response.status_code}&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0416","card":"q-github-actions-070-logs-and-secrets-through-the-api","sec":"github-actions-s071","q":"How can Python automate the creation of Actions secrets?","a":"<p>Use <code>PUT /repos/{owner}/{repo}/actions/secrets/{secret_name}</code>. The value must be encrypted with the repository's public key (PyNaCl sealed box).</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from github import Github\nfrom nacl import public\ndef create_secret(owner, repo, secret_name, secret_value, token):\n    g = Github(token)\n    repo = g.get_repo(f&quot;{owner}/{repo}&quot;)\n    public_key = repo.get_public_key()\n    public_key_obj = public.PublicKey(public_key.key.encode(), encoder=public.Base64Encoder)\n    sealed_box = public.SealedBox(public_key_obj)\n    encrypted_value = sealed_box.encrypt(secret_value.encode())\n    repo.create_secret(secret_name, encrypted_value.hex())\n    print(f&quot;Secret {secret_name} created!&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0417","card":"q-github-actions-070-logs-and-secrets-through-the-api","sec":"github-actions-s071","q":"When downloading workflow logs with <code>GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/actions/runs/{run_id}/logs</code>, what does the response look like and what pitfalls exist?","a":"<p>The endpoint replies with a redirect (HTTP 302) to a short-lived URL that serves a ZIP archive. The URL expires after about a minute, so download it immediately. <code>requests</code> follows the redirect automatically. Logs are retained only for the repository's retention period (90 days by default), and the token needs read access to Actions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0418","card":"q-github-actions-070-logs-and-secrets-through-the-api","sec":"github-actions-s071","q":"In a Python function that downloads Actions logs, why is the ZIP opened with <code>zipfile.ZipFile(io.BytesIO(response.content))</code>?","a":"<p>The response body is bytes. <code>io.BytesIO</code> wraps them in a file-like object, so <code>ZipFile</code> can read and extract the archive from memory without writing a temporary file first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0419","card":"q-github-actions-070-logs-and-secrets-through-the-api","sec":"github-actions-s071","q":"When creating a GitHub Actions secret through the REST API, why can't you send the value as plain text?","a":"<p>The API only accepts values encrypted with the repository's public key. You fetch the key and its <code>key_id</code> (<code>GET .../actions/secrets/public-key</code>), encrypt the value with a libsodium sealed box (PyNaCl <code>SealedBox</code>), base64-encode the result, and send <code>encrypted_value</code> and <code>key_id</code> in the <code>PUT</code> body. This way the secret never travels or sits in plain text. Secrets are write-only: the API lists names, never values.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0420","card":"q-github-actions-070-logs-and-secrets-through-the-api","sec":"github-actions-s071","q":"What is a quick way to get the logs of a failed run from the command line or a script without calling the logs endpoint?","a":"<p>Use the GitHub CLI: <code>gh run view &lt;run-id&gt; --log-failed</code> prints logs of only the failed steps, and <code>gh run download &lt;run-id&gt;</code> fetches the artifacts. <code>gh</code> uses your login or <code>GH_TOKEN</code>, so it needs less code than raw HTTP calls.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0421","card":"q-github-actions-071-api-tokens-and-rate-limits","sec":"github-actions-s072","q":"How do you secure GitHub API tokens in Python scripts for Actions automation?","a":"<ul>\n  <li>Store tokens in Actions secrets and read them with <code>${{ secrets.TOKEN }}</code>.</li>\n  <li>Pass secrets to scripts as environment variables.</li>\n  <li>Never hardcode tokens in scripts or the repo.</li>\n  <li>Use short-lived tokens with minimal permissions.</li>\n  <li>Audit and rotate tokens regularly.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Secure Python Script\non: [push]\njobs:\n  run-script:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n    - name: Run Python script\n      env:\n        GITHUB_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}\n      run: python script.py</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0422","card":"q-github-actions-071-api-tokens-and-rate-limits","sec":"github-actions-s072","q":"How do you handle GitHub API rate limits in Python?","a":"<ul>\n  <li>Monitor the <code>X-RateLimit-Remaining</code> header.</li>\n  <li>Use exponential backoff (<code>backoff</code>, <code>ratelimit</code>).</li>\n  <li>On HTTP 429, wait for the <code>Retry-After</code> duration.</li>\n  <li>Use conditional requests (ETags, <code>If-Modified-Since</code>) to reduce calls.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import requests\nimport time\ndef make_github_request(url, token):\n    headers = {&quot;Authorization&quot;: f&quot;Bearer {token}&quot;, &quot;Accept&quot;: &quot;application/vnd.github.v3+json&quot;}\n    response = requests.get(url, headers=headers)\n    if response.status_code == 429:\n        retry_after = int(response.headers.get(&quot;Retry-After&quot;, 60))\n        time.sleep(retry_after)\n        return make_github_request(url, token)\n    response.raise_for_status()\n    return response.json()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0423","card":"q-github-actions-071-api-tokens-and-rate-limits","sec":"github-actions-s072","q":"What are the GitHub API rate limits for personal access tokens and for <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code>, as of current GitHub docs?","a":"<p>Authenticated requests with a personal access token get 5,000 requests per hour. The <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> inside a workflow gets 1,000 requests per hour per repository (15,000 on GitHub Enterprise Cloud). Unauthenticated calls get 60 per hour per IP. GitHub changes these numbers, so check the rate-limit docs, and read <code>GET /rate_limit</code> (which is free) for your actual limits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0424","card":"q-github-actions-071-api-tokens-and-rate-limits","sec":"github-actions-s072","q":"How do GitHub's primary and secondary rate limits differ, and how should a script react to each?","a":"<p>The primary limit is the hourly quota. When it is exhausted, the response is 403 (or 429) with <code>X-RateLimit-Remaining: 0</code>, and you wait until the time in <code>X-RateLimit-Reset</code>. Secondary limits guard against bursts and heavy concurrent requests, and respond with 403 or 429 and often a <code>Retry-After</code> header, so you wait that long. Reduce concurrency and spread out writes, and always back off, never retry in a tight loop.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0425","card":"q-github-actions-071-api-tokens-and-rate-limits","sec":"github-actions-s072","q":"How do conditional requests help against GitHub API rate limits?","a":"<p>Send the stored <code>ETag</code> as <code>If-None-Match</code> (or <code>If-Modified-Since</code>). If the data did not change the API answers 304 with no body, and for authenticated requests a 304 does not count against the primary rate limit. This suits polling a resource that changes rarely.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0426","card":"q-github-actions-071-api-tokens-and-rate-limits","sec":"github-actions-s072","q":"Why use a GitHub App installation token for automation instead of a personal access token?","a":"<p>A personal access token belongs to a person: it breaks when they leave, and its permissions are often broader than needed. A GitHub App has fine-grained permissions, is installed per repository or organization, and its installation tokens expire after one hour. Apps also get higher rate limits that scale with the installation. Short-lived, scoped credentials limit the damage when one leaks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0427","card":"q-github-actions-071-api-tokens-and-rate-limits","sec":"github-actions-s072","q":"What is the difference between fine-grained and classic personal access tokens in GitHub?","a":"<p>Fine-grained tokens are limited to chosen repositories and to specific permissions (for example Actions read and write), can expire, and can require approval in an organization. Classic tokens use broad scopes such as <code>repo</code> that cover all the user's repositories. Prefer fine-grained, and prefer <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> or an App when the workflow can use it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0428","card":"q-github-actions-071-api-tokens-and-rate-limits","sec":"github-actions-s072","q":"What is weak about this retry logic for GitHub API calls?","a":"<p>It recurses with no retry limit, so repeated 429 responses can loop (and eventually exhaust the stack) while holding a CI job. It also handles only 429, but GitHub often signals rate limiting with 403 and <code>X-RateLimit-Remaining: 0</code>. And it ignores <code>X-RateLimit-Reset</code>, network errors and 5xx responses. Use a bounded loop with exponential backoff and jitter, and a <code>requests</code> <code>Retry</code> adapter or the <code>backoff</code> library.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>if response.status_code == 429:\n    retry_after = int(response.headers.get(&quot;Retry-After&quot;, 60))\n    time.sleep(retry_after)\n    return make_github_request(url, token)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0429","card":"q-github-actions-072-python-scenarios","sec":"github-actions-s073","q":"Scenario: a workflow is not triggering for a specific branch. How would Python troubleshoot via the GitHub API?","a":"<p>Use <code>PyGithub</code> to read the workflow file, check its <code>on</code> branch filter, and inspect branch protection rules.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from github import Github\ndef troubleshoot_workflow(owner, repo, workflow_path, branch, token):\n    g = Github(token)\n    repo = g.get_repo(f&quot;{owner}/{repo}&quot;)\n    # Check workflow file\n    try:\n        workflow_content = repo.get_contents(workflow_path).decoded_content.decode()\n        print(&quot;Workflow content:&quot;, workflow_content)\n        if f&quot;branches: [{branch}]&quot; not in workflow_content:\n            print(f&quot;Branch {branch} not configured in workflow&quot;)\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(f&quot;Error reading workflow file: {e}&quot;)\n    # Check branch protection\n    branch_obj = repo.get_branch(branch)\n    if branch_obj.protected:\n        print(&quot;Branch is protected. Check protection rules.&quot;)\n        print(branch_obj.get_protection())</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0430","card":"q-github-actions-072-python-scenarios","sec":"github-actions-s073","q":"A workflow does not run for pushes to one branch. Which GitHub API calls help a Python script find the cause?","a":"<p>List workflows and check each one's <code>state</code> (a workflow can be <code>disabled_manually</code> or <code>disabled_inactivity</code>). Read the workflow file on that branch and check <code>on.push.branches</code>, <code>paths</code> and <code>paths-ignore</code>. List runs for the branch with <code>event=push</code> to see whether any run exists or whether runs were skipped. Check whether the commit message contained <code>[skip ci]</code>. Confirm the workflow file exists on the branch that is pushed (for <code>push</code>, the file on the pushed commit is used).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0431","card":"q-github-actions-072-python-scenarios","sec":"github-actions-s073","q":"In a Python script that checks whether a branch is configured in a workflow, why is <code>f\"branches: [{branch}]\" not in workflow_content</code> a fragile test?","a":"<p>It is a text match. It misses valid forms such as multi-line lists, <code>branches-ignore</code>, glob patterns like <code>release/**</code>, and quoted names, and it can match inside comments. Parse the YAML and evaluate the <code>on</code> section. Remember that PyYAML loads the <code>on</code> key as <code>True</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0432","card":"q-github-actions-072-python-scenarios","sec":"github-actions-s073","q":"Does branch protection stop a push-triggered workflow from running on that branch?","a":"<p>No. Protection rules control who can push or merge and which checks are required, but the workflow still triggers on the allowed events. A protected branch can, however, block the workflow's own commit or tag pushes made with a token that lacks bypass rights, which looks like a trigger problem.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0433","card":"q-github-actions-073-python-scenarios-debugging-and-credentia","sec":"github-actions-s074","q":"Scenario: a workflow fails because of a dependency issue in a Python script. How do you debug it?","a":"<p>Check the logs, reproduce locally with the same environment, pin versions in <code>requirements.txt</code>, add debug output, and install dependencies in a separate step to isolate issues.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Debug Python Script\non: [push]\njobs:\n  debug:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n    - name: Set up Python\n      uses: actions/setup-python@v4\n      with:\n        python-version: &#x27;3.10&#x27;\n    - name: Install dependencies\n      run: pip install -r requirements.txt\n    - name: Run script with debug\n      run: python -m trace --trace script.py</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0434","card":"q-github-actions-073-python-scenarios-debugging-and-credentia","sec":"github-actions-s074","q":"Scenario: a workflow exposes AWS credentials in logs. How do you secure it?","a":"<p>Store credentials in secrets, make sure sensitive data is not printed, and check Python scripts for accidental logging.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Secure AWS Workflow\non: [push]\njobs:\n  deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n    - name: Set up Python\n      uses: actions/setup-python@v4\n      with:\n        python-version: &#x27;3.10&#x27;\n    - name: Run script\n      env:\n        AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID: ${{ secrets.AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID }}\n        AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY: ${{ secrets.AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY }}\n      run: python deploy.py</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># deploy.py\nimport boto3\nimport os\ndef deploy():\n    # Avoid printing credentials\n    session = boto3.Session(\n        aws_access_key_id=os.getenv(&quot;AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID&quot;),\n        aws_secret_access_key=os.getenv(&quot;AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY&quot;)\n    )\n    s3 = session.client(&quot;s3&quot;)\n    s3.list_buckets()  # Example operation\n    print(&quot;Deployment completed&quot;)\ndeploy()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0435","card":"q-github-actions-073-python-scenarios-debugging-and-credentia","sec":"github-actions-s074","q":"In a GitHub Actions step, what does <code>python -m trace --trace script.py</code> do, and when is it a good choice?","a":"<p>It prints every line of Python as it executes. It shows the exact path through the code, but the output is huge and slow, so use it for short reproductions only. For import problems <code>python -X importtime</code> or <code>pip check</code> is more focused, and for hard crashes <code>python -X faulthandler</code> helps. Targeted logging is usually better.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0436","card":"q-github-actions-073-python-scenarios-debugging-and-credentia","sec":"github-actions-s074","q":"A Python script passes locally but fails in CI with an import error after a library release. How do you debug and prevent it?","a":"<p>Compare environments: print <code>python --version</code> and <code>pip freeze</code> in CI and locally. Reproduce with a clean virtual environment from <code>requirements.txt</code>. Prevent it by pinning exact versions or using a lock file (<code>pip-compile</code>, <code>uv lock</code>, or <code>pip install --require-hashes</code>), and by installing dependencies in their own step so failures are easy to locate. Let Dependabot propose upgrades through pull requests.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0437","card":"q-github-actions-073-python-scenarios-debugging-and-credentia","sec":"github-actions-s074","q":"AWS keys appeared in a GitHub Actions log. What do you do?","a":"<p>Treat them as compromised. Deactivate and rotate the keys immediately (or delete the role session), review CloudTrail for use of the key, then remove the cause: scripts that print the environment, <code>set -x</code>, or verbose SDK logging. Delete the run's logs if needed, and move to OIDC so there are no long-lived keys. Removing the log line alone does not make the key safe again.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0438","card":"q-github-actions-074-role-of-github-actions-in-test-automatio","sec":"github-actions-s075","q":"How do Python, Bash and GitHub Actions complement each other?","a":"<p>Python writes robust test scripts, Bash handles environment setup and CLI tasks, and GitHub Actions orchestrates the workflows and triggers the tests.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0439","card":"q-github-actions-074-role-of-github-actions-in-test-automatio","sec":"github-actions-s075","q":"What is the role of GitHub Actions in automating testing?","a":"<p>It triggers tests on events, runs them in isolated runners, integrates tools and provides reports.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0440","card":"q-github-actions-074-role-of-github-actions-in-test-automatio","sec":"github-actions-s075","q":"What are the benefits of running tests in CI/CD with GitHub Actions?","a":"<p>Quality assurance, fast feedback, parallel execution, deployment automation and report generation.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0441","card":"q-github-actions-074-role-of-github-actions-in-test-automatio","sec":"github-actions-s075","q":"How do you secure sensitive data in test automation?","a":"<p>Use GitHub Secrets, avoid hardcoding, restrict access and encrypt local files.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0442","card":"q-github-actions-074-role-of-github-actions-in-test-automatio","sec":"github-actions-s075","q":"What are the limitations of GitHub Actions for test automation?","a":"<p>Limited free minutes, a 6-hour job limit, artifact storage caps, dependence on GitHub and less orchestration flexibility.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0443","card":"q-github-actions-074-role-of-github-actions-in-test-automatio","sec":"github-actions-s075","q":"How should test logic be divided among Python, Bash and GitHub Actions workflow files?","a":"<p>Keep the real test logic in scripts or the test framework (Python, Bash) so developers can run the same command locally. The workflow only orchestrates: trigger, environment, caching, matrix, secrets, artifacts and reporting. This keeps CI portable and avoids long YAML <code>run:</code> blocks that nobody can test locally.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0444","card":"q-github-actions-074-role-of-github-actions-in-test-automatio","sec":"github-actions-s075","q":"Which tests should run on every pull request and which on a schedule or before release in GitHub Actions?","a":"<p>Run fast tests on every pull request: lint, unit tests and a small set of integration tests. Run slow suites on a schedule (nightly) or before release: end-to-end, performance, cross-browser or full-platform matrices. This keeps pull request feedback within minutes and still gets full coverage regularly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0445","card":"q-github-actions-074-role-of-github-actions-in-test-automatio","sec":"github-actions-s075","q":"When might GitHub Actions alone be a poor fit for test automation?","a":"<p>When tests need hardware GitHub-hosted runners do not provide (GPUs, real mobile devices), exceed the six-hour job limit, need large long-lived environments, or need advanced orchestration (distributing a very large suite, test analytics). Options: self-hosted runners, a device farm, or a dedicated test platform that Actions triggers and reports from.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0446","card":"q-github-actions-074-role-of-github-actions-in-test-automatio","sec":"github-actions-s075","q":"Why does GitHub-hosted runner isolation help test reliability, and what does it cost?","a":"<p>Every job starts on a fresh virtual machine, so no leftovers from earlier runs change results. The cost is time: dependencies, tools and data must be installed or restored each run, which is why caching and prebuilt container images matter.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0447","card":"q-github-actions-074-role-of-github-actions-in-test-automatio","sec":"github-actions-s075","q":"Why should tests that call external APIs use stubs or recorded responses in CI?","a":"<p>Real calls make tests slow, flaky and dependent on rate limits and network health. They also need real credentials in CI, and fork pull requests get no secrets. Stubs (or recorded cassettes) make the result deterministic. Keep a smaller set of contract or smoke tests against the real service on a schedule.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0448","card":"q-github-actions-075-running-tests","sec":"github-actions-s076","q":"How do you run Python tests with pytest in GitHub Actions?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Tests\non: [push]\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n    - uses: actions/setup-python@v4\n      with: { python-version: &#x27;3.10&#x27; }\n    - run: pip install pytest\n    - run: pytest</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0449","card":"q-github-actions-075-running-tests","sec":"github-actions-s076","q":"How do you run matrix builds for multiple Python versions?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>strategy:\n  matrix:\n    python-version: [&#x27;3.8&#x27;, &#x27;3.9&#x27;, &#x27;3.10&#x27;]\nsteps:\n- uses: actions/setup-python@v4\n  with: { python-version: ${{ matrix.python-version }} }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0450","card":"q-github-actions-075-running-tests","sec":"github-actions-s076","q":"How do you run Python and Bash tests together?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps:\n- uses: actions/checkout@v3\n- uses: actions/setup-python@v4\n  with: { python-version: &#x27;3.10&#x27; }\n- run: pip install pytest\n- run: pytest\n- run: bash scripts/test.sh</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0451","card":"q-github-actions-075-running-tests","sec":"github-actions-s076","q":"How do you integrate a Bash script into a workflow?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps:\n- uses: actions/checkout@v3\n- run: bash scripts/test.sh</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0452","card":"q-github-actions-075-running-tests","sec":"github-actions-s076","q":"How do you integrate flake8 in a workflow?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps:\n- uses: actions/setup-python@v4\n  with: { python-version: &#x27;3.10&#x27; }\n- run: pip install flake8\n- run: flake8 .</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0453","card":"q-github-actions-075-running-tests","sec":"github-actions-s076","q":"What do pytest's exit codes mean for a GitHub Actions step?","a":"<p>pytest exits 0 when all tests pass, 1 when some fail, and 5 when no tests were collected. Any non-zero code fails the step. Exit 5 is a common surprise in CI: a wrong path or file pattern silently collects nothing and the job fails, so check the test paths.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0454","card":"q-github-actions-075-running-tests","sec":"github-actions-s076","q":"Which pytest options are useful in a CI workflow, and why?","a":"<p><code>--junitxml=report.xml</code> writes results that report tools read. <code>--cov</code> (pytest-cov) with <code>--cov-report=xml</code> produces coverage for upload. <code>-n auto</code> (pytest-xdist) runs tests in parallel. <code>-x</code> or <code>--maxfail</code> stops early, <code>-q</code> and <code>-ra</code> keep logs short but print failure summaries. Combine them with artifact uploads for the reports.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0455","card":"q-github-actions-075-running-tests","sec":"github-actions-s076","q":"In a GitHub Actions job that runs <code>pytest</code> and then <code>bash scripts/test.sh</code>, what happens to the Bash step if pytest fails?","a":"<p>It is skipped, because a step runs only if all previous steps succeeded (the implicit <code>if: success()</code>). To run both suites and see both results, put each in its own step with <code>if: always()</code> or <code>continue-on-error</code>, or split them into separate jobs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0456","card":"q-github-actions-075-running-tests","sec":"github-actions-s076","q":"A Bash step in GitHub Actions runs <code>bash scripts/test.sh</code>, which contains <code>cmd1 | tee out.txt</code>. A failure in <code>cmd1</code> is hidden. How do you fix it?","a":"<p>In a pipeline, the exit status is that of the last command, so <code>tee</code> hides the failure. Put <code>set -euo pipefail</code> at the top of the script. For <code>run:</code> steps, the default shell is <code>bash -e {0}</code>, which has no <code>pipefail</code>, while an explicit <code>shell: bash</code> uses <code>bash --noprofile --norc -eo pipefail {0}</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0457","card":"q-github-actions-075-running-tests","sec":"github-actions-s076","q":"Why run flake8 or another linter as a step before pytest in a test workflow?","a":"<p>Linting is cheap and finishes in seconds, so a style or syntax error fails the run before the slower tests start. It gives fast feedback and saves runner minutes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0458","card":"q-github-actions-076-triggers-reports-and-failures","sec":"github-actions-s077","q":"How do you trigger tests manually or on a schedule?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on:\n  workflow_dispatch:\n  schedule:\n    - cron: &#x27;0 0 * * *&#x27;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0459","card":"q-github-actions-076-triggers-reports-and-failures","sec":"github-actions-s077","q":"How do you schedule Bash tests?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on:\n  schedule:\n    - cron: &#x27;0 0 * * *&#x27;\nsteps:\n- uses: actions/checkout@v3\n- run: bash scripts/test.sh</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0460","card":"q-github-actions-076-triggers-reports-and-failures","sec":"github-actions-s077","q":"How do you upload test reports as artifacts?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- uses: actions/upload-artifact@v3\n  with:\n    name: coverage-report\n    path: coverage.xml</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0461","card":"q-github-actions-076-triggers-reports-and-failures","sec":"github-actions-s077","q":"How do you handle test failures?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- run: pytest\n  continue-on-error: true\n- run: echo &quot;Log failure&quot;\n  if: failure()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0462","card":"q-github-actions-076-triggers-reports-and-failures","sec":"github-actions-s077","q":"How do you debug failing tests?","a":"<p>Enable <code>ACTIONS_STEP_DEBUG</code>, add verbose output and upload logs.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- run: pytest -v\n- uses: actions/upload-artifact@v3\n  if: failure()\n  with: { name: logs, path: test_output.log }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0463","card":"q-github-actions-076-triggers-reports-and-failures","sec":"github-actions-s077","q":"How do you secure environment variables?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>env:\n  API_KEY: ${{ secrets.API_KEY }}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0464","card":"q-github-actions-076-triggers-reports-and-failures","sec":"github-actions-s077","q":"Why might a GitHub Actions <code>schedule</code> run start several minutes late, and how do you reduce the delay?","a":"<p>Scheduled runs are queued on shared infrastructure, so they can be delayed at busy times, especially at the top of the hour when many cron jobs fire. Pick an off-peak minute (for example <code>17 2 * * *</code> instead of <code>0 0 * * *</code>). A schedule is not a precise timer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0465","card":"q-github-actions-076-triggers-reports-and-failures","sec":"github-actions-s077","q":"What happens to scheduled GitHub Actions workflows in a public repository that has no activity for a long time?","a":"<p>GitHub automatically disables scheduled workflows in a public repository after 60 days without repository activity. Re-enable the workflow manually or push a commit. This catches teams that rely on a nightly test job in a quiet repository.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0466","card":"q-github-actions-076-triggers-reports-and-failures","sec":"github-actions-s077","q":"What is the pitfall in this GitHub Actions snippet?","a":"<p>With <code>continue-on-error: true</code> a failing pytest step is treated as passing for the job, so <code>failure()</code> is false and the logging step never runs. The step's own result is available as <code>steps.&lt;id&gt;.outcome</code> (<code>failure</code>), while <code>steps.&lt;id&gt;.conclusion</code> becomes <code>success</code>. Give pytest an <code>id</code> and test <code>steps.&lt;id&gt;.outcome == 'failure'</code>, or drop <code>continue-on-error</code> and use <code>if: failure()</code> or <code>always()</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- run: pytest\n  continue-on-error: true\n- run: echo &quot;Log failure&quot;\n  if: failure()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0467","card":"q-github-actions-076-triggers-reports-and-failures","sec":"github-actions-s077","q":"How can test results appear in a pull request, rather than only in raw logs?","a":"<p>Write JUnit XML (<code>pytest --junitxml</code>) and publish it with a test-reporter action, which creates a check run with per-test results. Write a Markdown summary to the file <code>$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY</code> to show a table on the run page. Post a pull request comment with coverage using a coverage action (needs <code>pull-requests: write</code>). Upload full reports as artifacts as well.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0468","card":"q-github-actions-076-triggers-reports-and-failures","sec":"github-actions-s077","q":"What are the options for handling flaky tests in a GitHub Actions pipeline, and what is the risk of automatic retries?","a":"<p>Options: quarantine the test, fix the cause, rerun failed tests with <code>pytest-rerunfailures</code> (<code>--reruns 2</code>), or retry a step with a retry action. Retrying hides real intermittent bugs and lengthens runs, so track retried tests and fix them. Never retry a whole deployment blindly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0469","card":"q-github-actions-077-test-automation-scenarios-environment-an","sec":"github-actions-s078","q":"The workflow is too slow. How do you optimize it?","a":"<p>Cache dependencies, parallelize, run affected tests and use faster runners.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- uses: actions/cache@v3\n  with:\n    path: ~/.cache/pip\n    key: pip-${{ hashFiles(&#x27;requirements.txt&#x27;) }}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0470","card":"q-github-actions-077-test-automation-scenarios-environment-an","sec":"github-actions-s078","q":"A Bash script fails because of a missing dependency. How do you resolve it?","a":"<p>Install the dependency, use Docker or check for tools.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- run: sudo apt-get install -y jq\n- run: bash scripts/test.sh</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0471","card":"q-github-actions-077-test-automation-scenarios-environment-an","sec":"github-actions-s078","q":"How do you ensure PRs pass tests before merging?","a":"<p>Enable branch protection and require the test workflow status.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on: [pull_request]\nsteps:\n- run: pytest</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0472","card":"q-github-actions-077-test-automation-scenarios-environment-an","sec":"github-actions-s078","q":"A test script exposes credentials in logs. How do you mitigate it?","a":"<p>Use secrets, mask logs and audit scripts.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>env:\n  DB_PASS: ${{ secrets.DB_PASS }}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0473","card":"q-github-actions-077-test-automation-scenarios-environment-an","sec":"github-actions-s078","q":"How do you set up a test database?","a":"<p>Use a service container such as PostgreSQL.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>services:\n  postgres:\n    image: postgres\n    env:\n      POSTGRES_DB: test\n    ports: [&#x27;5432:5432&#x27;]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0474","card":"q-github-actions-077-test-automation-scenarios-environment-an","sec":"github-actions-s078","q":"How do you automate a daily test report?","a":"<p>Schedule the workflow, run a Python report script and upload the artifact.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on:\n  schedule:\n    - cron: &#x27;0 0 * * *&#x27;\nsteps:\n- run: python generate_report.py\n- uses: actions/upload-artifact@v3\n  with:\n    name: report\n    path: report.txt</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0475","card":"q-github-actions-077-test-automation-scenarios-environment-an","sec":"github-actions-s078","q":"How can you cut the wall-clock time of a long test suite in GitHub Actions beyond caching?","a":"<p>Split the suite into shards across a matrix (each job runs a slice of the test files, using a splitting plugin or a file list) and run the shards in parallel. Use <code>pytest-xdist</code> to parallelise within a job. Run only tests affected by the change, put slow suites on a schedule, and use larger runners if CPU-bound. Parallelism costs more total minutes, so measure first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0476","card":"q-github-actions-077-test-automation-scenarios-environment-an","sec":"github-actions-s078","q":"A Bash script step fails with <code>jq: command not found</code> on a GitHub-hosted runner. How do you fix it, and what mistake do people make?","a":"<p>Install it with the package manager: <code>sudo apt-get update &amp;&amp; sudo apt-get install -y jq</code> on Ubuntu runners. Run <code>apt-get update</code> first, or the install can fail on stale package lists. <code>jq</code> is preinstalled on current GitHub-hosted Ubuntu images, so first check which runner image you use. If many steps need extra tools, bake them into a container image.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0477","card":"q-github-actions-077-test-automation-scenarios-environment-an","sec":"github-actions-s078","q":"Why is <code>set -x</code> risky in a Bash step that uses secrets in GitHub Actions?","a":"<p><code>set -x</code> prints each command after expansion, including any secret value placed into the command line. Masking only hides exact registered values, so a transformed value (base64, a substring) slips through. Avoid <code>set -x</code> around sensitive commands and pass secrets via environment variables, not arguments.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0478","card":"q-github-actions-077-test-automation-scenarios-environment-an","sec":"github-actions-s078","q":"Where should the output of a scheduled daily test report workflow go?","a":"<p>Options: upload it as an artifact (simple, kept for the retention period), write a summary to <code>$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY</code>, post to Slack or email for the team, or push it to a storage bucket or dashboard for history. Committing reports back to the repository clutters history. Add a failure notification so a broken nightly job gets noticed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0479","card":"q-github-actions-078-test-automation-scenarios-platforms-and","sec":"github-actions-s079","q":"A Bash script fails on Windows runners. How do you fix it?","a":"<p>Use portable commands, check the OS or use Unix runners.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>[ &quot;$OSTYPE&quot; = &quot;msys&quot; ] &amp;&amp; dir || ls</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0480","card":"q-github-actions-078-test-automation-scenarios-platforms-and","sec":"github-actions-s079","q":"Monorepo: run tests only for specific directories.","a":"<p>Use <code>paths</code> filters.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on:\n  push:\n    paths: [&#x27;frontend/**&#x27;]\njobs:\n  frontend:\n    steps:\n    - run: npm test --prefix frontend</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0481","card":"q-github-actions-078-test-automation-scenarios-platforms-and","sec":"github-actions-s079","q":"A workflow does not trigger on PRs. How do you troubleshoot?","a":"<p>Check <code>on: pull_request</code>, branch rules, file paths and permissions.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on: [pull_request]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0482","card":"q-github-actions-078-test-automation-scenarios-platforms-and","sec":"github-actions-s079","q":"How do you get a custom test environment?","a":"<p>Use a Docker container or a self-hosted runner.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>container: custom-image:latest</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0483","card":"q-github-actions-078-test-automation-scenarios-platforms-and","sec":"github-actions-s079","q":"A test fails because of a dependency version mismatch. How do you ensure consistency?","a":"<p>Pin versions in <code>requirements.txt</code>, cache, and use a fixed Python version.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- uses: actions/setup-python@v4\n  with: { python-version: &#x27;3.10&#x27; }\n- run: pip install -r requirements.txt</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"github-actions-f0484","card":"q-github-actions-078-test-automation-scenarios-platforms-and","sec":"github-actions-s079","q":"A Bash script that works on Linux fails on a <code>windows-latest</code> GitHub Actions runner because of line endings. What is the cause and the fix?","a":"<p>Git may check out files with CRLF endings on Windows, and Bash then fails on <code>\\r</code> (\"command not found\" or \"bad interpreter\"). Add a <code>.gitattributes</code> rule such as <code>*.sh text eol=lf</code>, or set <code>git config core.autocrlf false</code> before checkout. Use <code>shell: bash</code> for the step so Git Bash is used, because the Windows default shell is PowerShell.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0485","card":"q-github-actions-078-test-automation-scenarios-platforms-and","sec":"github-actions-s079","q":"What is wrong with this portable-looking shell line?","a":"<p><code>A &amp;&amp; B || C</code> is not an if/else. If <code>dir</code> returns a non-zero status, <code>ls</code> also runs. The line also assumes <code>dir</code> and <code>ls</code> produce equivalent output. Write an explicit <code>if [ ... ]; then ...; else ...; fi</code>, and prefer one implementation (a Python script or a cross-platform tool) over OS-specific branches.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>[ &quot;$OSTYPE&quot; = &quot;msys&quot; ] &amp;&amp; dir || ls</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0486","card":"q-github-actions-078-test-automation-scenarios-platforms-and","sec":"github-actions-s079","q":"Why is <code>container: custom-image:latest</code> a weak choice in a test workflow?","a":"<p><code>latest</code> moves, so the same commit can get different tools on different days and failures become hard to reproduce. Pin a version tag, or better a digest (<code>image@sha256:...</code>), and update it in a reviewed change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0487","card":"q-github-actions-078-test-automation-scenarios-platforms-and","sec":"github-actions-s079","q":"How do you run a test suite on Linux, Windows and macOS in one GitHub Actions workflow?","a":"<p>Use a matrix over the operating system and select the runner from it:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>strategy:\n  matrix:\n    os: [ubuntu-latest, windows-latest, macos-latest]\nruns-on: ${{ matrix.os }}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Keep steps portable (or set <code>shell: bash</code>), expect Windows and macOS minutes to cost more on private repositories, and run the full OS matrix only where it adds value.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0488","card":"q-github-actions-079-shared-workflow-skeleton","sec":"github-actions-s080","q":"In GitHub Actions, what does <code>aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials</code> do for the later <code>aws</code> CLI steps?","a":"<p>It exports the AWS credentials and region as environment variables (and a session token when using a role) for the rest of the job. Later <code>aws</code> CLI or SDK calls then pick them up through the default credential chain. In newer major versions (v4 and later) it supports assuming a role through OIDC with <code>role-to-assume</code>, so no stored access keys are needed. Version <code>v1</code> is old and uses a deprecated runtime.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0489","card":"q-github-actions-079-shared-workflow-skeleton","sec":"github-actions-s080","q":"Why is <code>AWS_REGION</code> a poor candidate for a GitHub secret?","a":"<p>A region is not sensitive, and storing it as a secret masks it in logs and makes it harder to read. Use a repository or environment variable (<code>vars.AWS_REGION</code>) for configuration and secrets only for credentials.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0490","card":"q-github-actions-079-shared-workflow-skeleton","sec":"github-actions-s080","q":"What is risky about a workflow with <code>on: [push]</code> that runs AWS CLI commands to create resources?","a":"<p>Every push runs the commands again, so you create duplicates, hit \"already exists\" errors, or pay for stray resources. Use a manual <code>workflow_dispatch</code>, a protected environment with required approval, or better a declarative tool (Terraform, CloudFormation) that compares desired and actual state.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0491","card":"q-github-actions-079-shared-workflow-skeleton","sec":"github-actions-s080","q":"What is the trade-off between creating AWS resources with <code>aws</code> CLI commands in a workflow and using Terraform or CloudFormation?","a":"<p>CLI commands are quick for one-off or small tasks, but they are imperative: no state, no diff, no automatic cleanup, and you must handle ordering and idempotency yourself. Terraform or CloudFormation track state, show a plan, update and delete safely, and are repeatable. Use IaC for anything long-lived.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0492","card":"q-github-actions-079-shared-workflow-skeleton","sec":"github-actions-s080","q":"How should the IAM permissions of the credentials used by an AWS workflow be scoped?","a":"<p>Give the role only the actions and resources the job needs (for instance <code>ec2:RunInstances</code> on specific resources), separate roles per environment, and limit trust to the repository and branch through the OIDC <code>sub</code> claim. Avoid administrator access for a pipeline: if a workflow or an action it runs is compromised, the access is its blast radius.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0493","card":"q-github-actions-080-ec2-ebs-and-elastic-ip","sec":"github-actions-s081","q":"In AWS, what must be true about the Availability Zone of an EBS volume and the EC2 instance it is attached to, and what step follows the attach?","a":"<p>The volume and the instance must be in the same Availability Zone. After <code>attach-volume</code>, the operating system still sees an empty block device: create a filesystem (<code>mkfs</code>) on first use, mount it, and add it to <code>/etc/fstab</code> to survive reboots. On Nitro instances the device name <code>/dev/xvdf</code> can appear as an NVMe device like <code>/dev/nvme1n1</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0494","card":"q-github-actions-080-ec2-ebs-and-elastic-ip","sec":"github-actions-s081","q":"In AWS CLI scripts, why does <code>VOLUME_ID=$(aws ec2 create-volume ... --query VolumeId --output text)</code> use <code>--query</code> and <code>--output text</code>?","a":"<p><code>--query</code> is a JMESPath filter that picks one field from the JSON response, and <code>--output text</code> prints it without quotes. The shell then stores the bare ID in a variable for the next command, such as <code>attach-volume</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0495","card":"q-github-actions-080-ec2-ebs-and-elastic-ip","sec":"github-actions-s081","q":"What does <code>aws ec2 modify-instance-attribute --instance-id i-... --groups sg-12345678</code> do to the instance's existing security groups?","a":"<p>It replaces the whole set of security groups with the list you give. It does not add one. To keep the old groups, pass them all along with the new one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0496","card":"q-github-actions-080-ec2-ebs-and-elastic-ip","sec":"github-actions-s081","q":"Why use an Elastic IP in AWS, and what does it cost?","a":"<p>An Elastic IP is a static public IPv4 address that you own until you release it, and you can remap it to another instance for failover. An automatically assigned public IP changes when the instance is stopped and started. Since February 2024 AWS charges hourly for every public IPv4 address, in use or idle, so release unused ones. A DNS name pointing to a load balancer is often better than a fixed IP.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0497","card":"q-github-actions-080-ec2-ebs-and-elastic-ip","sec":"github-actions-s081","q":"What do <code>--image-id</code>, <code>--key-name</code>, <code>--security-group-ids</code> and <code>--subnet-id</code> decide in <code>aws ec2 run-instances</code>?","a":"<p><code>--image-id</code> is the AMI (operating system and preinstalled software). <code>--key-name</code> is the key pair for SSH login. <code>--security-group-ids</code> sets the firewall rules for the instance's network interface. <code>--subnet-id</code> sets the subnet, which fixes the VPC and Availability Zone and the IP range. <code>--instance-type</code> sets CPU and memory.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0498","card":"q-github-actions-080-ec2-ebs-and-elastic-ip","sec":"github-actions-s081","q":"In AWS, which EBS volume type is usually a better default than gp2 and why?","a":"<p><code>gp3</code>. Its baseline of 3,000 IOPS and 125 MiB/s does not depend on volume size, and it is cheaper per GB than gp2. You can raise IOPS and throughput independently of size. With gp2, performance scales with size.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0499","card":"q-github-actions-080-ec2-ebs-and-elastic-ip","sec":"github-actions-s081","q":"What happens to EBS volumes when an EC2 instance is terminated?","a":"<p>The root volume is deleted by default (<code>DeleteOnTermination</code> is true), while additional volumes attached later are kept by default. Set the flag per volume to change this. Use snapshots for backups of volumes you care about.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0500","card":"q-github-actions-081-vpc-subnet-and-security-group","sec":"github-actions-s082","q":"How do you choose CIDR blocks for a VPC (<code>10.0.0.0/16</code>) and its subnets (<code>10.0.1.0/24</code>) in AWS?","a":"<p>Take a private range (RFC 1918) big enough for growth, and carve it into non-overlapping subnets, usually one or more per Availability Zone. A subnet lives in exactly one AZ. AWS reserves 5 addresses in each subnet. Choose ranges that do not overlap with other VPCs or on-premises networks you may peer or connect with later, because overlapping ranges cannot be routed together.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0501","card":"q-github-actions-081-vpc-subnet-and-security-group","sec":"github-actions-s082","q":"In AWS, what is the difference between a security group and a network ACL?","a":"<p>A security group is attached to a network interface (an instance), is stateful (return traffic is allowed automatically) and has only allow rules. A network ACL is attached to a subnet, is stateless (you must allow return traffic in both directions), supports allow and deny rules, and evaluates rules by number. Use security groups for most control and NACLs for coarse subnet-level blocks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0502","card":"q-github-actions-081-vpc-subnet-and-security-group","sec":"github-actions-s082","q":"What is the risk of <code>--cidr 0.0.0.0/0</code> on port 22 in a security group, and what are better options?","a":"<p>It exposes SSH to the whole internet, where scanners attempt brute force and exploit unpatched servers. Restrict the source to a known CIDR (office or VPN), or avoid inbound SSH entirely with AWS Systems Manager Session Manager or EC2 Instance Connect Endpoint. Use key-based login only.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0503","card":"q-github-actions-081-vpc-subnet-and-security-group","sec":"github-actions-s082","q":"What are the default rules of a new AWS security group?","a":"<p>Inbound: nothing is allowed. Outbound: all traffic is allowed. Rules can only allow, and a source can be a CIDR or another security group (for example, allow the database port only from the application's security group). Multiple security groups on one interface combine their rules.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0504","card":"q-github-actions-081-vpc-subnet-and-security-group","sec":"github-actions-s082","q":"What is the mistake in this command for a security group that lives in a non-default VPC?","a":"<p>For a security group in a non-default VPC, the command must identify the group by <code>--group-id</code>, because <code>--group-name</code> only works for the default VPC. Capture the group ID from <code>create-security-group</code> (<code>--query GroupId --output text</code>) and pass it. The source CIDR should also not be open to the world.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws ec2 authorize-security-group-ingress \\\n  --group-name my-security-group \\\n  --protocol tcp \\\n  --port 22 \\\n  --cidr 0.0.0.0/0</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"github-actions-f0505","card":"q-github-actions-082-internet-gateway-and-route-table","sec":"github-actions-s083","q":"In AWS, what makes a subnet public?","a":"<p>Its route table has a route for <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> that targets an internet gateway, and instances in it have a public IPv4 address (or Elastic IP). Without the route, or without a public address, the subnet is private. Security groups and network ACLs must also allow the traffic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0506","card":"q-github-actions-082-internet-gateway-and-route-table","sec":"github-actions-s083","q":"Why must you attach an internet gateway to the VPC and associate the route table with the subnet in AWS?","a":"<p>The internet gateway does nothing until it is attached to a VPC. A route to it does nothing until it is in the subnet's route table. A route table affects a subnet only when associated with it, and an unassociated subnet uses the VPC's main route table. So the order is: create and attach the gateway, create the route, associate the table to the subnet. The route needs an existing gateway ID.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0507","card":"q-github-actions-082-internet-gateway-and-route-table","sec":"github-actions-s083","q":"In AWS, how do an internet gateway and a NAT gateway differ?","a":"<p>An internet gateway allows two-way internet access for resources with public IPs. A NAT gateway lets instances in private subnets start outbound connections (software updates, API calls) while blocking inbound connections from the internet. A NAT gateway sits in a public subnet with an Elastic IP, and the private subnet's route table sends <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> to it. It is billed per hour and per GB, so use VPC endpoints for AWS services to cut cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0508","card":"q-github-actions-082-internet-gateway-and-route-table","sec":"github-actions-s083","q":"In an AWS route table with routes for <code>10.0.0.0/16</code> (local) and <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> (internet gateway), which route handles traffic to <code>10.0.1.5</code>?","a":"<p>The <code>local</code> route, since AWS uses the most specific (longest prefix) matching route. The <code>10.0.0.0/16</code> route is more specific than <code>0.0.0.0/0</code>. Traffic inside the VPC stays inside, and only other destinations use the default route.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0509","card":"q-github-actions-082-internet-gateway-and-route-table","sec":"github-actions-s083","q":"An EC2 instance in a public subnet has a public IP but cannot reach the internet. What do you check?","a":"<p>Check in order: the subnet's route table has <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> to an attached internet gateway and is associated with that subnet; the instance really has a public IPv4 address; the security group's outbound rules and the network ACL allow the traffic (including return traffic for the NACL); and OS firewall and DNS settings (VPC DNS support).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0510","card":"q-github-actions-083-iam-role-and-instance-profile","sec":"github-actions-s084","q":"In AWS, why does an EC2 instance need an instance profile when you already have an IAM role?","a":"<p>An instance profile is the container that passes a role to an EC2 instance. EC2 cannot be attached to a role directly. The console creates a profile automatically with the same name, but the CLI and APIs require you to create it, add the role (<code>add-role-to-instance-profile</code>), and associate it with the instance. An instance profile holds one role.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0511","card":"q-github-actions-083-iam-role-and-instance-profile","sec":"github-actions-s084","q":"In an IAM role created for EC2, what is the role of the trust policy compared with the attached permissions policy?","a":"<p>The trust policy (<code>assume-role-policy-document</code>) says who may assume the role. For EC2 it names the service principal <code>ec2.amazonaws.com</code> with <code>sts:AssumeRole</code>. The permissions policy says what the role may do once assumed, such as reading S3. Both are required: a role with the right permissions but the wrong trust cannot be used.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0512","card":"q-github-actions-083-iam-role-and-instance-profile","sec":"github-actions-s084","q":"Why use an IAM role on an EC2 instance rather than putting access keys on the instance?","a":"<p>The role gives the instance temporary credentials from the instance metadata service, rotated automatically, so nothing long-lived is stored on disk or in an image. AWS SDKs and the CLI find them through the default credential chain with no configuration. Require IMDSv2 to reduce the risk of credential theft through SSRF.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0513","card":"q-github-actions-083-iam-role-and-instance-profile","sec":"github-actions-s084","q":"A script creates an IAM instance profile and immediately calls <code>associate-iam-instance-profile</code>, which fails with \"Invalid IAM Instance Profile name\". Why?","a":"<p>IAM is eventually consistent. A new role or profile can take several seconds to propagate to other services. Retry with a short backoff, or wait (<code>aws iam wait instance-profile-exists</code>) before associating.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0514","card":"q-github-actions-083-iam-role-and-instance-profile","sec":"github-actions-s084","q":"How many IAM instance profiles can an EC2 instance have, and how do you change it?","a":"<p>One at a time. To switch roles, use <code>replace-iam-instance-profile-association</code> (or disassociate then associate). To give an instance more permissions, add policies to its role, or use a different role that has the union of what is needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0515","card":"q-github-actions-083-iam-role-and-instance-profile","sec":"github-actions-s084","q":"Why prefer a narrow customer-managed policy over <code>AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess</code> for a role?","a":"<p>The AWS managed policy grants read access to all buckets in the account. A customer-managed policy can restrict actions and resources to the single bucket and prefix the application needs, which is least privilege and limits damage if the instance is compromised.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0516","card":"q-github-actions-084-load-balancer-and-auto-scaling-group","sec":"github-actions-s085","q":"In AWS Elastic Load Balancing, how does an Application Load Balancer differ from a Network Load Balancer?","a":"<p>An ALB works at layer 7 (HTTP/HTTPS): it routes by host, path, header or query, supports redirects and authentication, and sends to instances, IPs or Lambda targets. An NLB works at layer 4 (TCP/UDP/TLS): very high throughput, low latency, static IPs per AZ. Choose ALB for web applications and microservice routing, and NLB for non-HTTP protocols or when you need static IPs or extreme performance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0517","card":"q-github-actions-084-load-balancer-and-auto-scaling-group","sec":"github-actions-s085","q":"Why does <code>aws elbv2 create-load-balancer</code> by itself not serve any traffic?","a":"<p>A load balancer needs a target group (where traffic goes, with health checks), registered targets, and a listener (port and protocol) with a rule that forwards to the target group. The create command only builds the load balancer. An ALB also needs subnets in at least two Availability Zones.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0518","card":"q-github-actions-084-load-balancer-and-auto-scaling-group","sec":"github-actions-s085","q":"In an Auto Scaling group, what do <code>--min-size</code>, <code>--max-size</code> and <code>--desired-capacity</code> mean?","a":"<p>Min is the floor the group never drops under, max is the ceiling it never exceeds, and desired is the number of instances it tries to keep running right now. Scaling policies change desired between min and max. If an instance fails a health check, the group replaces it to restore desired capacity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0519","card":"q-github-actions-084-load-balancer-and-auto-scaling-group","sec":"github-actions-s085","q":"What does <code>--vpc-zone-identifier subnet-a,subnet-b</code> do in an Auto Scaling group?","a":"<p>It tells the group which subnets to launch instances in. Giving subnets in different Availability Zones makes the group spread instances across the AZs and keeps running if one zone fails.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0520","card":"q-github-actions-084-load-balancer-and-auto-scaling-group","sec":"github-actions-s085","q":"In AWS Auto Scaling, why are launch templates preferred to launch configurations?","a":"<p>Launch templates are versioned, support newer EC2 features (mixed instance types, Spot, newer instance generations) and can be shared across services. AWS has deprecated launch configurations and no longer adds new features to them, so use templates for new work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0521","card":"q-github-actions-084-load-balancer-and-auto-scaling-group","sec":"github-actions-s085","q":"In an Auto Scaling group <code>--launch-template LaunchTemplateName=my-launch-template,Version=1</code>, what is the effect of pinning <code>Version=1</code> versus using <code>$Latest</code>?","a":"<p>A pinned number always launches the same template version, so changes are deliberate and repeatable. <code>$Latest</code> picks up each new version on the next launch, which is convenient but can change production instances unexpectedly. <code>$Default</code> lets you move a default version label.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0522","card":"q-github-actions-084-load-balancer-and-auto-scaling-group","sec":"github-actions-s085","q":"How do you make an Auto Scaling group work with a load balancer, and how should security groups be set up?","a":"<p>Attach the load balancer's target group to the group, and use the ELB health check type so that instances failing the load balancer's health check are replaced, not only those failing EC2 status checks. Let the load balancer's security group accept public traffic, and let the instances' security group accept traffic only from the load balancer's security group.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0523","card":"q-github-actions-084-load-balancer-and-auto-scaling-group","sec":"github-actions-s085","q":"Which scaling policy do you choose in an Auto Scaling group for a web application with variable load?","a":"<p>Target tracking: you set a target such as 50% average CPU or a number of requests per target, and the group adds or removes instances to hold it. Step scaling suits custom thresholds, scheduled scaling suits known traffic patterns, and predictive scaling suits recurring cycles. Instance warm-up and cooldown settings prevent flapping.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0524","card":"q-github-actions-085-s3-dynamodb-and-rds","sec":"github-actions-s086","q":"What extra parameter does <code>aws s3api create-bucket</code> need outside <code>us-east-1</code>?","a":"<p><code>--create-bucket-configuration LocationConstraint=&lt;region&gt;</code>, otherwise the call fails. In <code>us-east-1</code> it is left out. Bucket names are globally unique across all AWS accounts, so use a unique prefix.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0525","card":"q-github-actions-085-s3-dynamodb-and-rds","sec":"github-actions-s086","q":"What are the security defaults of a newly created S3 bucket?","a":"<p>Since April 2023, new buckets have S3 Block Public Access enabled and ACLs disabled (bucket owner enforced), and objects are encrypted at rest with SSE-S3 by default. Access then comes through IAM and bucket policies, and any public access needs deliberate changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0526","card":"q-github-actions-085-s3-dynamodb-and-rds","sec":"github-actions-s086","q":"In <code>aws dynamodb create-table</code>, what do <code>--attribute-definitions</code> and <code>--key-schema</code> each do?","a":"<p><code>--key-schema</code> defines the primary key: a partition key (<code>HASH</code>) and optionally a sort key (<code>RANGE</code>). <code>--attribute-definitions</code> declares the type (<code>S</code>, <code>N</code>, <code>B</code>) of attributes used in keys and indexes only. DynamoDB is schemaless for other attributes, so they are not declared.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0527","card":"q-github-actions-085-s3-dynamodb-and-rds","sec":"github-actions-s086","q":"In DynamoDB, when do you choose <code>PAY_PER_REQUEST</code> over provisioned capacity?","a":"<p>On-demand (<code>PAY_PER_REQUEST</code>) suits new, spiky or unpredictable workloads and needs no capacity planning, but costs more per request at steady high volume. Provisioned capacity with auto scaling is cheaper for steady, predictable traffic. You can switch between modes, with limits on how often.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0528","card":"q-github-actions-085-s3-dynamodb-and-rds","sec":"github-actions-s086","q":"How do you choose between DynamoDB and RDS?","a":"<p>DynamoDB is a managed key-value and document store with single-digit millisecond latency at any scale, and you design tables around access patterns. It lacks joins and flexible ad-hoc queries. RDS is relational (MySQL, PostgreSQL and others): SQL, joins, transactions and flexible queries, scaled mainly vertically with read replicas. Pick DynamoDB for known access patterns at high scale, RDS for relational data and complex queries.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0529","card":"q-github-actions-085-s3-dynamodb-and-rds","sec":"github-actions-s086","q":"In a workflow step, why is <code>--master-user-password \"${{ secrets.DB_PASSWORD }}\"</code> not the safest way to pass the RDS password, and what is better?","a":"<p>The <code>${{ }}</code> expression is expanded into the script text before the shell runs, so the value lands in the command line. Pass it as a step <code>env</code> variable and reference <code>\"$DB_PASSWORD\"</code>. Better, use <code>--manage-master-user-password</code> so RDS creates and rotates the password in AWS Secrets Manager and no workflow ever sees it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0530","card":"q-github-actions-085-s3-dynamodb-and-rds","sec":"github-actions-s086","q":"What is <code>--db-subnet-group-name</code> in <code>aws rds create-db-instance</code>, and why does it need several subnets?","a":"<p>A DB subnet group lists the subnets (in the VPC) where RDS may place the instance. It must cover at least two Availability Zones, which enables Multi-AZ standby placement and failover. Use private subnets so the database is not reachable from the internet.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0531","card":"q-github-actions-085-s3-dynamodb-and-rds","sec":"github-actions-s086","q":"What does enabling Multi-AZ on an RDS instance give you, and what does it not give you?","a":"<p>It keeps a synchronous standby in another AZ and fails over automatically on hardware or AZ problems, with the same endpoint. It does not add read capacity (a standby is not readable in the classic setup), and it does not replace backups. Use read replicas for read scaling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0532","card":"q-github-actions-086-rds-security-group-and-snapshot","sec":"github-actions-s087","q":"How should the security group of an RDS instance be configured?","a":"<p>Allow the database port (3306 for MySQL, 5432 for PostgreSQL) only from the application tier's security group, not from <code>0.0.0.0/0</code>. Keep the instance in private subnets and not publicly accessible. Databases should never be open to the internet, and referencing a security group keeps the rule correct as instances come and go.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0533","card":"q-github-actions-086-rds-security-group-and-snapshot","sec":"github-actions-s087","q":"What does <code>--apply-immediately</code> do in <code>aws rds modify-db-instance</code>?","a":"<p>Without it, changes are queued and applied in the next maintenance window. With it, RDS applies the modification now. Some changes cause a brief outage or reboot, so apply immediately during a quiet period and check the pending-modification status.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0534","card":"q-github-actions-086-rds-security-group-and-snapshot","sec":"github-actions-s087","q":"In AWS, how do manual RDS snapshots differ from automated backups?","a":"<p>Automated backups run daily within the backup window, include transaction logs for point-in-time restore, are kept for a retention period (1 to 35 days) and are deleted with the instance unless retained. Manual snapshots are taken when you ask, are kept until you delete them, survive instance deletion, and can be copied or shared across regions and accounts. Take a manual snapshot before risky changes such as schema migrations.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0535","card":"q-github-actions-086-rds-security-group-and-snapshot","sec":"github-actions-s087","q":"How do you restore an RDS database from a snapshot?","a":"<p>You restore into a new DB instance with a new endpoint. It does not overwrite the running one. Restore with <code>restore-db-instance-from-db-snapshot</code>, then point the application to the new endpoint or swap names. Recreate settings that are not in the snapshot, such as security groups and parameter groups.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0536","card":"q-github-actions-086-rds-security-group-and-snapshot","sec":"github-actions-s087","q":"A workflow calls <code>aws rds create-db-snapshot</code> and the next step needs the snapshot ready. How do you wait?","a":"<p>The create call returns immediately while the snapshot is still <code>creating</code>. Use <code>aws rds wait db-snapshot-available --db-snapshot-identifier &lt;id&gt;</code>, which polls until it is available, then continue.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0537","card":"q-github-actions-086-rds-security-group-and-snapshot","sec":"github-actions-s087","q":"How do you get an RDS snapshot into another region or AWS account for disaster recovery?","a":"<p>Use <code>aws rds copy-db-snapshot</code> with the source and target region to copy it across regions. To share it with another account use <code>modify-db-snapshot-attribute</code> with that account ID. Encrypted snapshots also need the KMS key shared or re-encrypted with a key in the target.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0538","card":"q-github-actions-087-lambda-sns-and-cloudwatch","sec":"github-actions-s088","q":"In <code>aws lambda create-function</code>, what do <code>--handler index.handler</code>, <code>--role</code> and <code>--zip-file fileb://lambda-code.zip</code> specify?","a":"<p><code>--handler index.handler</code> is the entry point: file <code>index</code> (for Node.js) and the exported function <code>handler</code>. <code>--role</code> is the execution role Lambda assumes to run the code, which gives the function its AWS permissions and CloudWatch Logs access. <code>--zip-file fileb://...</code> uploads the deployment package as a binary file (<code>fileb</code>, not <code>file</code>, so the CLI does not treat it as text).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0539","card":"q-github-actions-087-lambda-sns-and-cloudwatch","sec":"github-actions-s088","q":"You subscribe a Lambda function to an SNS topic with <code>aws sns subscribe --protocol lambda</code>, but the function never runs. What is missing?","a":"<p>SNS needs permission to invoke the function. Add a resource-based policy with <code>aws lambda add-permission --principal sns.amazonaws.com --source-arn &lt;topic-arn&gt; --action lambda:InvokeFunction</code>. The console adds it for you, but the CLI does not.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0540","card":"q-github-actions-087-lambda-sns-and-cloudwatch","sec":"github-actions-s088","q":"In AWS, when do you choose SNS and when SQS?","a":"<p>SNS is pub/sub: one message is pushed to many subscribers (Lambda, SQS, HTTP, email) at once. SQS is a queue: consumers pull messages, which are stored until processed, with retries and dead-letter queues. A common pattern is SNS fanning out to several SQS queues, giving each consumer its own buffer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0541","card":"q-github-actions-087-lambda-sns-and-cloudwatch","sec":"github-actions-s088","q":"In <code>aws cloudwatch put-metric-alarm</code> with <code>--period 300 --evaluation-periods 2 --threshold 80 --statistic Average</code>, when does the alarm go to ALARM?","a":"<p>When the average CPU exceeds 80% in two consecutive 5-minute periods, so after about 10 minutes of sustained load. Short spikes are ignored. Raise <code>--evaluation-periods</code> to reduce noise, or lower it for faster detection. <code>--alarm-actions</code> lists what to trigger, such as an SNS topic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0542","card":"q-github-actions-087-lambda-sns-and-cloudwatch","sec":"github-actions-s088","q":"What states does a CloudWatch alarm have?","a":"<p><code>OK</code>, <code>ALARM</code> and <code>INSUFFICIENT_DATA</code> (not enough data points yet, or the metric is missing). Set how missing data is treated with <code>--treat-missing-data</code> to avoid false alarms.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0543","card":"q-github-actions-087-lambda-sns-and-cloudwatch","sec":"github-actions-s088","q":"What does the default EC2 CloudWatch monitoring cover, and what does it miss?","a":"<p>Basic monitoring reports metrics such as CPU, network and disk I/O (instance level) every 5 minutes, and detailed monitoring does it every 1 minute for a fee. Memory and disk space usage are not reported by default. Install the CloudWatch agent to publish them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0544","card":"q-github-actions-087-lambda-sns-and-cloudwatch","sec":"github-actions-s088","q":"Why should you pin the Lambda <code>--runtime</code> to a currently supported version?","a":"<p>AWS deprecates runtimes when their language version reaches end of life: first you cannot create new functions, then updates are blocked, and there are no security patches. Pick a supported runtime and plan upgrades.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0545","card":"q-github-actions-088-route-53-record","sec":"github-actions-s089","q":"In <code>aws route53 change-resource-record-sets</code>, how do <code>CREATE</code>, <code>UPSERT</code> and <code>DELETE</code> actions differ, and which fits a pipeline?","a":"<p><code>CREATE</code> fails if the record exists. <code>DELETE</code> removes a record and must match the existing values exactly. <code>UPSERT</code> creates the record or updates it if present. <code>UPSERT</code> is the safe choice for repeated runs of a workflow because it is idempotent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0546","card":"q-github-actions-088-route-53-record","sec":"github-actions-s089","q":"In Route 53, when should you use an alias record and not a CNAME?","a":"<p>Use an alias to point a name at an AWS resource (CloudFront, ALB, S3 website, API Gateway). Aliases are allowed at the zone apex (<code>example.com</code>), where a CNAME is not allowed by DNS rules. Queries to aliases for AWS resources are free and follow the target's IP changes. Use a CNAME for names that point outside AWS, on non-apex names.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0547","card":"q-github-actions-088-route-53-record","sec":"github-actions-s089","q":"What does the <code>TTL</code> of 300 in a Route 53 record control, and how do you use it for a migration?","a":"<p>It is the time in seconds that resolvers and clients cache the answer. A low TTL means changes spread faster but there are more queries. Before a planned cutover lower the TTL (for instance to 60) well ahead of time, switch the record, then raise it again.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0548","card":"q-github-actions-088-route-53-record","sec":"github-actions-s089","q":"Why is it not enough to create a Route 53 hosted zone for a domain registered elsewhere?","a":"<p>The registrar must delegate the domain to Route 53 by setting the domain's name servers to the four NS records of the hosted zone. Until then public resolvers do not ask Route 53, and the records have no effect.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0549","card":"q-github-actions-088-route-53-record","sec":"github-actions-s089","q":"Which Route 53 routing policies would you use for failover, latency and gradual traffic shifting?","a":"<p>Failover routing (primary and secondary, driven by health checks), latency-based routing (send users to the region with the lowest latency), and weighted routing (split traffic by percentage, useful for canary releases). Geolocation routes by user location, and multivalue answer returns several healthy records.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0550","card":"q-github-actions-088-route-53-record","sec":"github-actions-s089","q":"In <code>aws route53 change-resource-record-sets --change-batch '...'</code>, how can you avoid long inline JSON?","a":"<p>Put the JSON in a file and pass <code>--change-batch file://change.json</code>. It is easier to review, version and quote correctly, and avoids shell quoting errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0551","card":"q-github-actions-089-ecs-cluster-and-fargate-task","sec":"github-actions-s090","q":"In Amazon ECS, how do a cluster, a task definition, a task and a service relate?","a":"<p>A cluster is the logical group of capacity. A task definition is the versioned blueprint (images, CPU, memory, ports, roles). A task is one running instance of a task definition. A service keeps a desired number of tasks running, replaces failed ones and can register them with a load balancer. <code>run-task</code> starts one-off tasks, and <code>create-service</code> runs long-lived ones.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0552","card":"q-github-actions-089-ecs-cluster-and-fargate-task","sec":"github-actions-s090","q":"How do ECS on Fargate and ECS on EC2 differ, and how do you choose?","a":"<p>With Fargate, AWS runs the compute: you set CPU and memory per task and pay per task, and manage no servers. With the EC2 launch type you manage the instances in the cluster, and can pick instance types, use GPUs, and get better cost at high steady utilization. Choose Fargate for less operations work, EC2 when you need control or lower cost at scale.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0553","card":"q-github-actions-089-ecs-cluster-and-fargate-task","sec":"github-actions-s090","q":"Why does the Fargate task definition use <code>--network-mode awsvpc</code>?","a":"<p>Fargate requires <code>awsvpc</code>. Each task gets its own elastic network interface and private IP in your subnet, so security groups apply per task and the task behaves like a VPC host. The <code>containerPort</code> and <code>hostPort</code> in port mappings must then be the same.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0554","card":"q-github-actions-089-ecs-cluster-and-fargate-task","sec":"github-actions-s090","q":"In an ECS task definition, what is the difference between the execution role (<code>--execution-role-arn</code>) and the task role?","a":"<p>The execution role is used by ECS to start the task: pull the image from ECR and write logs to CloudWatch. The task role is what the application code in the container assumes for its own AWS calls, such as reading S3 or DynamoDB. Keep them separate for least privilege.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0555","card":"q-github-actions-089-ecs-cluster-and-fargate-task","sec":"github-actions-s090","q":"In ECS, what happens when you run <code>register-task-definition</code> again with the same <code>--family</code>?","a":"<p>A task definition is immutable. Registering again creates a new revision (<code>my-task:2</code>) and leaves the old one as is. Services and run-task calls can reference a revision or the latest one in the family. Roll back by pointing the service to an earlier revision.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0556","card":"q-github-actions-089-ecs-cluster-and-fargate-task","sec":"github-actions-s090","q":"A Fargate task in a public subnet stops with <code>CannotPullContainerError</code>. What do you check?","a":"<p>The task needs a route to the registry. In a public subnet, set <code>assignPublicIp=ENABLED</code> and check the route to the internet gateway. In a private subnet, use a NAT gateway or VPC endpoints for ECR (and S3 for image layers) and CloudWatch Logs. Also check the execution role's permissions, the image name and tag, and the security group's outbound rules.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0557","card":"q-github-actions-089-ecs-cluster-and-fargate-task","sec":"github-actions-s090","q":"In Fargate, the task definition has <code>--cpu \"256\" --memory \"512\"</code>. Can you choose any pair of values?","a":"<p>No. Fargate allows only specific combinations. 256 CPU units (0.25 vCPU) accepts 512 MB, 1 GB or 2 GB, and larger CPU sizes allow larger memory ranges. An invalid pair is rejected when the task definition is registered. Right-size from measurements, since you pay for the configured size.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"github-actions-f0558","card":"q-github-actions-089-ecs-cluster-and-fargate-task","sec":"github-actions-s090","q":"How do you deploy a new container image to an ECS service from GitHub Actions?","a":"<p>Build and push the image to ECR, render a new task definition with the image (<code>aws-actions/amazon-ecs-render-task-definition</code>), and deploy with <code>aws-actions/amazon-ecs-deploy-task-definition</code>, which registers a revision and updates the service, optionally waiting for stability. Authenticate with OIDC and tag images with the commit SHA so a rollback means redeploying an earlier revision.</p>","hand":true}];
